var WK_JAPAN = [
{"id":"japan-staple-dashi","name":"Dashi","nameAlt":"Ichiban Dashi — the first stock","aliases":["Dashi","Ichiban Dashi","Japanese stock","Kombu and Bonito Stock"],"course":"staple","type":["staple","basic","soup"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","batch","quick"],"ingredients":"300ml cold water · 3g dried kombu (a piece about 8cm square) · 5g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)","method":"Almost everything Japanese food tastes of starts here, and the entire technique is a lesson in restraint: two ingredients, no browning, no salt, no boiling, and it is finished in under fifteen minutes. Understand it once and half of Japanese cooking opens up. Start with the kombu, and start it cold. Do not scrub the fine white bloom off the surface — that powder is mannitol and glutamate drying out of the seaweed itself, which is to say it is the flavour you are paying for, and every recipe that tells you to wipe the kombu clean is telling you to wash off the point. If there is visible grit or sand, brush it off dry with a cloth, and leave the white alone. Put the kombu in the cold water and give it time. Thirty minutes is good, a few hours in the fridge is better, and overnight is better still — a cold soak extracts the glutamate slowly and cleanly and asks nothing of you. Now heat it, and heat it gently, and here is the one rule of dashi: THE KOMBU MUST NEVER BOIL. Bring the pan up slowly over a low flame, aiming to take a good ten minutes to reach the point where small bubbles are beginning to gather on the bottom of the pan and the water is steaming but not moving — around 60 to 65°C, which in practical terms is the moment before it looks like anything is happening. Pull the kombu out at that point with tongs. Past roughly 80°C the seaweed starts giving up alginates instead of glutamate, and the stock turns slippery, faintly bitter and unmistakably marine in a way it should not be. That change is not reversible, and a boiled dashi cannot be corrected by any later step. Once the kombu is out, bring the water to a proper boil for a few seconds, then kill the heat completely and let it settle for thirty seconds. Now add all the katsuobushi at once and DO NOT STIR IT. Stirring beats the flakes apart, clouds the stock and pushes bitterness out of them; left alone they will swell, sink and release what they have in about a minute. Give them sixty to ninety seconds, no more, then strain the whole thing through a fine sieve lined with a piece of muslin or a clean cloth. And then the second rule, which matters as much as the first: DO NOT SQUEEZE THE FLAKES. Let it drip, and let it take a minute or two. Squeezing the sodden bonito is exactly the same mistake as boiling the kombu and produces the same result — a cloudy stock with a bitter, slightly fishy edge — and it is the most common way a home cook ruins a dashi they otherwise made perfectly. What you should have is a clear, pale golden liquid that smells of the sea and of woodsmoke and tastes savoury, round and almost sweet, with no salt in it at all. Taste it on its own before you use it, because you need to know what it tastes like unseasoned in order to recognise it later underneath everything else. Do not throw the spent kombu and katsuobushi away. They still hold a great deal, and the second extraction is a real ingredient in its own right — see the leftovers below.","cookTime":"Soak 30 min (or overnight) · Heat 10 min · Steep 2 min","kcal":"~5–10 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 1g, Carbs 0g, Fat 0g, Sodium ~60mg (before any seasoning)","storage":"Best on the day it is made, and its aroma is at its peak in the first hour. It keeps 3 days in a sealed jar in the fridge and freezes beautifully — an ice-cube tray of dashi is one of the most useful things you can have in a freezer, because it turns any pan of vegetables into a Japanese dish in a minute. Freeze it flat in a bag if you would rather have sheets you can snap.","chefNotes":"Kombu into cold water and never let it boil — pull it at 60–65°C. Katsuobushi off the heat, no stirring, 60–90 seconds. Strain and never squeeze. If your dashi is cloudy or bitter, one of those three is why.","pairsWith":"It is the base rather than the partner — miso soup, chawanmushi, every simmered vegetable, the batter of an okonomiyaki and the broth of a donburi. Keep a jar in the fridge and you will find uses for it that have nothing to do with Japan.","trivia":"Umami was discovered in this exact bowl. In 1908 Kikunae Ikeda, a chemistry professor at Tokyo Imperial University, became preoccupied with a question about his wife's dashi: it tasted strongly of something that was clearly not sweet, sour, salty or bitter, and the four-taste model of the day had no room for it. He boiled down a very large quantity of kombu, crystallised out the compound responsible, identified it as glutamic acid, and named the taste umami — savoury. He patented the manufacture of its sodium salt the following year, which is how monosodium glutamate came to exist. The better half of the story arrived five years later: in 1913 his student Shintaro Kodama went looking for the same thing in katsuobushi and found something different, inosinate, the savoury compound of dried fish and meat. Glutamate and inosinate are synergistic, and not mildly so — put together they register as several times more savoury than the sum of the two alone. So the two-ingredient stock that Japanese cooks had been making for centuries turns out to be a precisely built chemical amplifier, and it took Western analytical chemistry until the twentieth century to explain what the technique had already worked out. The katsuobushi deserves a word of its own, because it may be the most laboriously made ingredient in any kitchen: bonito is filleted, simmered, boned, smoked over weeks, then deliberately inoculated with a mould, sun-dried, re-inoculated, and repeated over months until the fillet is dry, dense and hard enough to be shaved on what is essentially a woodworking plane. It is routinely described as the hardest food in the world, and that is not a figure of speech.","howThisFeels":"That first smell as the flakes go in and the steam comes up — smoke and clean sea and something warm underneath it — and the small surprise of tasting a liquid with no salt in it that is somehow already delicious.","leftovers":["(easy) The spent kombu and katsuobushi make niban dashi, the second stock, and this is standard practice rather than thrift: cover them with the same volume of fresh water, simmer gently for 10 minutes — this time boiling does no harm because the delicate compounds are already gone — add a small pinch of fresh flakes at the end, and strain. It is rougher and less fragrant than the first, and it is what miso soup and long-simmered vegetables actually want.","(easy) After the second extraction, chop the soft kombu finely and simmer it with soy, mirin and a little sugar until sticky and dark. This is tsukudani, it keeps a fortnight in the fridge, and a teaspoon on hot rice is a complete small pleasure.","(creative) Dry the spent bonito flakes in a low oven, then blitz them with sesame seeds, a little salt and a torn sheet of nori into a coarse powder. That is a homemade furikake, it costs you nothing, and it will improve rice, eggs, popcorn and roast vegetables for weeks."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Kombu & Shiitake (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":4,"howThisFeels":"Deeper and darker than the classic, and it smells of a forest floor rather than the sea — the version that makes vegetables taste like they have meat behind them.","trivia":"This is shojin dashi, the stock of Japanese Buddhist temple cooking, and it is not a substitute so much as the older sibling. Temple kitchens have worked without fish for centuries, and the dried shiitake is doing the same job the bonito does in the classic: mushrooms concentrate guanylate as they dry, which is a third savoury compound, and it is synergistic with the kombu's glutamate in exactly the same way inosinate is. A dried shiitake gives up far more to its soaking water than a fresh one ever gives a pan, which is why the soaking liquid here is not a by-product but half the recipe.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"5g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)","to":"8g dried shiitake mushrooms"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Soak the dried shiitake in the same cold water as the kombu from the start — they need hours, not minutes, and cold water is what gets the flavour out without turning the mushrooms to mush. Overnight in the fridge is ideal. Lift both the kombu and the mushrooms out before the water reaches 65°C, then bring the liquid to a bare simmer for 5 minutes and take it off. Strain it, and POUR OFF THE LAST CENTIMETRE rather than tipping the whole pan through the sieve, because grit settles out of dried mushrooms and it will follow the liquid if you let it. The soaked mushrooms themselves are now a good ingredient with the caps sliced into a stir-fry or a rice bowl; do not bin them."}]}},{"name":"Classic Ichiban Dashi","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":9},{"name":"Niboshi (Dried Sardine) Dashi","icon":"🐟","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":7,"howThisFeels":"Assertive, oily and properly fishy in the best way — the stock that stands up to a heavy miso and does not disappear underneath it.","trivia":"The western half of Japan tends to reach for niboshi rather than katsuobushi, and it makes a noticeably more aggressive stock — this is the base under a lot of miso soup and a great deal of ramen, and it is considerably cheaper than bonito. It also has a preparation step that is not optional and is nearly always skipped: the heads and the black gut of each little sardine must be pinched out before soaking. Those are where the bitterness lives, and a niboshi dashi made from whole fish tastes harsh in a way people wrongly blame on the sardines themselves. The best method is not to heat it at all — a cold overnight steep in the fridge gives a cleaner, rounder stock than any amount of careful simmering.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"5g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)","to":"8g niboshi (dried sardines), heads and guts removed"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Pinch the head off each sardine and press out the dark line of gut with a thumbnail — it takes two minutes for a handful and it is the difference between a good stock and a bitter one. Add them to the cold water alongside the kombu and leave the whole thing in the fridge overnight, then simply strain it: no heat at all. If you want it the same day, heat it slowly with the kombu as written, pull the kombu at 65°C, then hold the sardines at a bare simmer for 8 minutes and strain without pressing."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"},{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"},{"name":"Oyakodon","target":"japan-oyakodon","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"japan-shoyu-ramen","name":"Shoyu Ramen","nameAlt":"Soy-seasoned ramen, Tokyo style","aliases":["Shoyu Ramen","Soy Sauce Ramen","Tokyo Ramen","Ramen"],"course":"main","type":["main","noodles","soup"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["weekend","project","family","winter"],"ingredients":"300g chicken wings and carcass, chopped · 60g pork belly, in one piece, for the chashu · 1.2L cold water · 8g dried kombu · 6g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes) · 25g onion, halved · 15g fresh ginger, sliced · 10g garlic, crushed · 3 spring onions · 45ml light soy sauce (for the tare) · 15ml mirin · 15ml sake · 8g white sugar · 120g fresh ramen noodles · 1 egg · 20ml neutral oil (for the aroma oil) · 15g spring onion greens (for the aroma oil) · 20g menma (seasoned bamboo shoots), optional · 1/4 sheet nori","method":"The single most useful thing to understand about ramen is that a bowl is not one recipe but THREE separate things made separately and assembled at the last second: the soup, the tare, and the aroma oil. And the second most useful thing follows from it, and it is the thing almost every home cook gets wrong: THE BOWL IS SEASONED BY THE TARE, NOT BY THE SOUP. The broth itself is left essentially unsalted. If you taste your finished ramen broth on its own and it tastes properly seasoned and delicious, you have made a soup, and when the tare and the noodles go in you will get something too salty and flat. It should taste rich, savoury and slightly underwhelming on its own. That is correct. Start the soup. Cover the chopped chicken bones with cold water in a pot, bring it to the boil, and let it boil hard for two or three minutes, at which point a great deal of grey scum will rise. Tip the whole lot into the sink, then wash each piece under the tap and SCRUB the bones with your fingers, and wash the pot as well. This is fifteen minutes of unglamorous work and it is what separates a clear golden broth from a murky beige one. Return the bones to the clean pot with fresh cold water, the onion, ginger, garlic and the white parts of the spring onion, bring it barely to the boil and then drop it immediately to the faintest possible movement — an occasional bubble, nothing more. Three hours at that whisper. Add the kombu for the last thirty minutes only and never let it boil, and the katsuobushi for the last two minutes off the heat, exactly as in the dashi. Strain. Meanwhile, roll the pork belly tightly and tie it, brown it all over in a dry pan, then simmer it very gently for two hours in a small pan of water with a splash of soy, mirin and sake until a skewer meets no resistance. Cool it in its liquid and chill it before slicing; a warm belly cannot be cut thin and thin is the whole point of chashu. Now the tare, which takes four minutes and does most of the work. Warm the soy, mirin, sake and sugar together with a small piece of kombu until the sugar dissolves, and do not boil it — you want the mirin and sake to lose their raw alcohol edge, not to reduce. Take it off, let the kombu sit in it while it cools, and remove it. That is roughly enough tare for two or three bowls, and it will keep in the fridge for weeks. The aroma oil is faster still: warm the neutral oil with the sliced spring onion greens over a low heat for six or seven minutes until the greens are frizzled and the oil smells strongly of them, then strain. Do it while something else is simmering. The egg wants six and a half minutes from the fridge into already-boiling water, then straight into iced water, then peeled and left in a splash of the tare thinned with water for four hours or overnight. Do not leave it in the marinade longer than about twelve hours; past that the white turns rubbery and unpleasantly salty, and there is no way back from it. Now assemble, and assemble fast, because this is where a good bowl is won or lost. Fill the serving bowl with boiling water to heat it through, and heat the soup to a genuine rolling boil at the same time. Put a big pot of UNSALTED water on for the noodles — unsalted because everything about this bowl has been measured, and salted noodle water quietly adds an amount of salt nobody accounted for. Tip out the hot water from the bowl. Into the empty hot bowl goes 30ml of tare and a teaspoon of the aroma oil. Pour about 300ml of boiling soup in on top of them and swirl it once; the pouring does the mixing. Cook the noodles in the meantime — fresh ramen noodles want 60 to 90 seconds and no more — then lift them out and SHAKE THEM BONE DRY, hard, several times. Water clinging to noodles is the last enemy: it dilutes a tare you measured to the millilitre and turns a sharp bowl into a vague one. Fold the noodles neatly into the soup, top with sliced chashu, the halved egg, menma, sliced spring onion and the nori tucked against the rim, and take it to the table immediately. From the moment the noodles hit the water you have about three minutes before the bowl starts declining, which is why every ramen shop in Japan is built around a counter.","cookTime":"Broth 3 hr · Chashu 2 hr · Tare 5 min · Assembly 3 min","kcal":"~640–780 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g, Carbs 72g, Fat 26g, Sodium ~2100mg","storage":"Never store an assembled bowl — noodles sitting in hot broth are ruined within about ten minutes and there is no rescuing them. Store the components instead, which is how shops do it: broth keeps 4 days in the fridge and freezes for 3 months, tare keeps a month in a jar, aroma oil keeps 2 weeks, chashu keeps 4 days in its liquid and slices better on day two than on day one. With those in the fridge a bowl is a five-minute job on a weeknight, which is the real reason to make the full version on a Sunday.","chefNotes":"Three components, not one recipe. The tare seasons the bowl, so the broth stays almost unsalted. Blanch and scrub the bones. Unsalted noodle water, and shake the noodles bone dry. Assemble in twenty seconds and eat immediately.","pairsWith":"Gyoza on the side is the standard pairing and needs no defending. A cold beer, a small dish of something pickled and sharp, and nothing else — ramen is not a dish that wants company.","trivia":"Japan's most famous food is Chinese, and Japan said so in the name for the first half of the twentieth century: until the 1950s it was generally called shina soba or chuka soba, both of which simply mean Chinese noodles. It arrived with Chinese cooks in the treaty ports after 1859, and Yokohama's Chinatown is the usual starting point. What turned a port-city curiosity into the national dish, though, was neither Chinese nor Japanese but American. Japan's rice harvest failed catastrophically in 1945 and the country was starving; the occupation authorities shipped in enormous quantities of surplus American wheat flour as food aid, and the Japanese government, holding a mountain of wheat and almost no rice, actively promoted bread and noodles in schools and public campaigns. Cheap flour plus a hungry population plus a black market full of small stalls is the entire recipe for ramen's explosion, and nearly every regional style that people now discuss as though it were ancient tradition was invented in the decades immediately after: Sapporo's miso ramen in the 1950s, Hakata's tonkotsu around the same time, the instant packet in 1958. The dish is younger than most of the people who tell you how it should be made, which is worth remembering when someone gets very serious about the rules.","howThisFeels":"The moment the boiling broth hits the tare in the bottom of the bowl and the whole kitchen suddenly smells like a shop on a cold night — and then that first pull of noodles, too hot, eaten anyway.","leftovers":["(easy) Leftover broth is the single most valuable thing in the fridge — use it instead of water to cook rice, or heat it with a nest of noodles and whatever greens are wilting for a five-minute lunch that tastes like it took three hours, because it did.","(easy) Leftover chashu is superb cold: slice it thin, torch or grill it briefly until the edges blister, and eat it on rice with a spoonful of tare and a raw egg yolk. This is a real dish in Japan and not a scrap-eating exercise.","(creative) Chop leftover chashu small and fry it hard with cold rice, the last of the aroma oil and a beaten egg, finishing with a spoon of tare instead of soy sauce. It is the best fried rice you will make, and every element of it was already seasoned for you."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Shiitake & Kombu Shoyu (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"Dark, woody and startlingly deep for a bowl with nothing in it — the version that convinces people the meat was never the point.","trivia":"A vegan ramen has one real problem and it is not flavour but body: meat broth is full of gelatine, and gelatine is what makes a spoonful of soup cling to the noodles instead of running off them. Flavour is easy to replace with dried mushrooms and kombu; that clinging weight is not. Two things do most of the work. A small handful of soaked and blitzed cashews or a spoonful of white sesame paste emulsified into the hot broth adds fat and viscosity at once, and roasting the mushrooms and onion hard before they go in the pot builds the browned depth that a long bone simmer would otherwise provide. Skip both and you get a pleasant mushroom tea; do both and you get ramen.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"300g chicken wings and carcass, chopped"},{"item":"60g pork belly, in one piece, for the chashu"},{"item":"6g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)"},{"item":"1 egg"}],"addIng":[{"item":"25g dried shiitake mushrooms"},{"item":"150g fresh mushrooms, halved"},{"item":"15g white sesame paste or 20g cashews"},{"item":"80g firm tofu, pressed and cubed"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Roast the fresh mushrooms and the halved onion at 220°C for 25 minutes until they are properly browned at the edges — pale roasted vegetables give a pale broth. Soak the dried shiitake in the cold water for several hours, then build the broth from the soaking liquid, the roasted vegetables, the ginger, garlic and spring onion whites, simmered gently for 45 minutes; add the kombu for the last 20 and never boil it. Strain, POURING OFF THE LAST CENTIMETRE because mushroom grit settles. Whisk the sesame paste or the blitzed cashews into the hot strained broth until it is smooth and no longer separating — this is what gives it body, and skipping it is the difference between ramen and soup. Fry the pressed tofu cubes hard on all sides until they have a real skin, and use them in place of the chashu. The tare is unchanged and already vegan."}]}},{"name":"Classic Tokyo Shoyu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48},{"name":"The Full Bowl — Rolled Chashu, Ajitama & Menma","icon":"🍜","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":62,"howThisFeels":"Two slabs of belly with a lacquered edge, a yolk that is still liquid at the centre, and the sense that you have built something rather than cooked it.","trivia":"The full bowl is where ramen stops being a recipe and becomes a project, and the component worth the most attention is the egg. An ajitama is a soft-boiled egg with a jammy — not runny, not set — yolk, and the timing is unforgiving because it is measured from a fridge-cold egg into water that is already at a rolling boil: six and a half minutes, and thirty seconds either side is a visibly different egg. The ice bath is not optional either, because carryover heat will keep cooking the yolk for another minute if you let it, and by then the window has closed. The last refinement is the finishing torch on the chashu: a blowtorch or a very hot grill for thirty seconds renders the fat cap and blisters the edge, and it is the one restaurant trick that transfers to a home kitchen without any equipment worth arguing about.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"60g pork belly, in one piece, for the chashu","to":"140g pork belly, in one piece, rolled and tied for the chashu"}],"addIng":[{"item":"1 extra egg"},{"item":"10g garlic, extra, for the aroma oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Roll the belly skin-side out into a tight cylinder and tie it at 2cm intervals with butcher's string — the tying is what gives you round slices rather than ragged strips, and a loose roll unravels in the simmer. Brown it hard on every face in a dry pan before it goes into the braise, then simmer it two and a half hours and, crucially, LET IT COOL COMPLETELY IN ITS OWN LIQUID before it goes anywhere near a knife. Chill it overnight if you can. Slice it 5mm thick, lay the slices in the bowl on top of the noodles, and blister the top of each one with a blowtorch or under a fierce grill for thirty seconds before serving. Marinate two eggs rather than one and halve them with a wet knife, wiping the blade between cuts, or the yolk drags."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🍖"},{"name":"Oyakodon","target":"japan-oyakodon","emoji":"🍚"}]},
{"id":"japan-tonkatsu","name":"Tonkatsu","nameAlt":"Panko-crumbed pork cutlet","aliases":["Tonkatsu","Pork Katsu","Japanese Pork Cutlet","Katsu"],"course":"main","type":["main","roast"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","weekend"],"ingredients":"180g pork loin, cut 2.5cm thick, in one piece · 3g fine salt · 1g white pepper · 20g cake flour · 1 egg, beaten · 45g panko breadcrumbs · 500ml neutral oil · 120g white cabbage, very finely shredded · 30ml tonkatsu sauce · 5g Japanese mustard (karashi), optional · 1 lemon wedge","method":"A tonkatsu is a thick cutlet, and every rule in it exists because of that thickness. Thin it out and you have a schnitzel, which is a good thing but a different thing; the whole ambition here is a crust that shatters over meat that is still faintly pink and running with juice, and that is a heat-management problem before it is anything else. Start with the pork, and start with the step that is left out of most recipes and explains most failures. Along one edge of a loin chop there is a band of fat, and between that fat and the meat there is a thin white line of connective tissue. That tissue shrinks far harder and faster than muscle does, and if you leave it intact the cutlet will pull itself into a bowl shape in the oil, which means the middle lifts clear of the pan, cooks unevenly and comes out with a pale hinge across it. So take a small knife and SNIP THROUGH THAT LINE at three or four points along the edge, cutting about a centimetre in. It takes ten seconds and it is the single most useful thing in this method. Now season it: salt and white pepper on both faces, and then leave it fifteen minutes at room temperature. The salt draws a little moisture out and then carries itself back in, seasoning the meat rather than just its surface, and the rest takes the fridge-chill off so the centre is not starting from cold. Before crumbing, pat both faces completely dry with kitchen paper. Set up three shallow dishes: flour, beaten egg, panko. The flour is a DUSTING and nothing more — press the cutlet in, then hold it up and tap it firmly so that almost all of it falls away, leaving a barely visible film. A thick coat of flour hydrates into a paste under the egg, and that paste is what makes a crust that slides off the meat in one piece when you cut it. Through the egg, letting the excess run off, and then into the panko: press it on firmly on both faces and around the edges, then lift it, shake off what is loose, and DO NOT PRESS IT AGAIN. Compacting panko defeats its entire purpose. And now the frying, which is done at two temperatures, and the reason is thickness rather than tradition. A 2.5cm cutlet fried at one temperature gives you a choice between raw in the middle and burnt on the outside; there is no single number that does both. So: heat the oil to 160°C, lower the cutlet in away from you, and fry it for six or seven minutes, turning once, until it is a pale even gold. It will not look finished, and it should not. Lift it onto a rack — a rack, not paper, so steam escapes from underneath instead of soaking the crust you have just built — and STAND IT UP ON ITS EDGE if it will balance, so both faces drain. Rest it there for four minutes. That rest is not a pause; carryover heat is finishing the centre while the crust dries. Now bring the oil up to 190°C and put the cutlet back in for thirty to sixty seconds, purely for colour and crispness. Out onto the rack again for two minutes. While all this is happening, deal with the cabbage, which is not a garnish and should not be treated as one. Shred it as finely as you physically can — fine enough to be almost translucent — then drop it into a bowl of iced water for ten minutes and spin or shake it thoroughly dry. Limp cabbage becomes crisp, sweet and cold, and a cold sweet crunch is the exact thing a plate of fried pork needs. Slice the tonkatsu across into 2cm strips with the sharpest knife you own, using one long draw rather than a sawing motion; sawing tears the crust off the meat. Fan the slices against a heap of cabbage, put the sauce in a small dish rather than over the top, and add the lemon and a small dab of karashi if you have it. Rice, and a bowl of miso soup, and it is done.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · First fry 7 min · Rest 4 min · Second fry 1 min","kcal":"~680–820 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 42g, Carbs 44g, Fat 40g, Sodium ~980mg","storage":"Eat it inside ten minutes; a rested tonkatsu is a good sandwich but it is no longer a tonkatsu. It will keep 2 days in the fridge and reheats better than most fried food if you use a hot oven or an air fryer at 200°C for 6–7 minutes on a rack — never a microwave, which turns panko to wet sand in about forty seconds. The frying oil, cooled and strained through a cloth, is good for several more uses.","chefNotes":"Snip the membrane along the fat edge or it curls. Dust the flour and tap almost all of it off. Press the panko once and never again. Two temperatures — 160°C to cook, rest, then 190°C for colour. Ice-water the shredded cabbage.","pairsWith":"Rice and miso soup, and the shredded cabbage that comes with it as a matter of law. A sharp pickle on the side, and nothing rich — the plate is already doing that.","trivia":"Tonkatsu is yoshoku — Western food, made Japanese — and it is the clearest example of the category. Its ancestor is the French cotelette, and it exists at all because of a deliberate act of government policy. For roughly twelve centuries Japan had an effective prohibition on eating four-legged animals, dating from an imperial edict of 675 AD and renewed often enough to hold; meat-eating was Buddhist taboo and socially unthinkable for most people. Then in 1872 the Meiji Emperor publicly ate beef, and it was reported as news, because the entire point was to announce that the prohibition was over and that Japan intended to modernise, diet included. It was not universally welcomed — a group of men broke into the Imperial Palace in protest at the change and several were shot. Western restaurants followed, and in 1899 Rengatei in Ginza took the pan-fried veal cutlet and made three changes to it: pork instead of veal, deep-fried in a deep vat like tempura rather than pan-fried in butter, and — the decisive one — served already sliced into strips, with rice and chopsticks and a heap of raw shredded cabbage, instead of whole with a knife and fork. Those three changes are the entire distance between a French dish and a Japanese one. The cabbage, by most accounts, was not a culinary decision at all: the restaurant was short-staffed during the war with China and raw shredded cabbage was simply faster to produce than cooked vegetables. It turned out to be the right answer, and it never left.","howThisFeels":"The specific noise a good tonkatsu makes under the knife — a dry crackle, quite loud — and the pale pink centre steaming as the slices fall apart against the cold cabbage.","leftovers":["(easy) A katsu sando is a real and famous thing and not a consolation prize: cold or briefly reheated tonkatsu, a smear of sauce, a little mustard, between two slices of soft white bread with the crusts cut off. Press it gently, cut it in half, and it is arguably better than the original.","(easy) Slice leftover cutlet thin and lay it over a bowl of hot rice with a spoonful of the sauce and a soft egg — that is most of the way to katsudon and takes four minutes.","(creative) Chop cold tonkatsu into cubes, crust and all, and fold it through fried rice at the very last second so the panko keeps some texture. The crumb turns into little crisp nuggets through the rice and the whole thing tastes deliberate rather than salvaged."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Chicken Katsu (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":28,"howThisFeels":"Softer and juicier than the pork, with the crust doing even more of the work — the version children ask for by name.","trivia":"Chicken katsu is not a lesser tonkatsu, it is a different technical problem, and the difference is thickness again. A chicken thigh is thin and uneven, so the two-temperature method that the pork needs is wasted on it — there is no thick centre to worry about. Fry it once, at a steady 175°C, for about five minutes. Use thigh rather than breast: breast is lean enough that by the time the crust is properly golden the meat has gone dry and stringy, whereas thigh has enough fat to stay juicy through the same fry, and it also folds to an even thickness in a way breast does not. Open the thigh out flat, butterfly the thick part, and press it into a rough rectangle before crumbing.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"180g pork loin, cut 2.5cm thick, in one piece","to":"180g chicken thighs, boned and opened out flat"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Open each boned thigh out skin-side down, slash the thickest part and fold it back so the piece is roughly even, then flatten it lightly with the heel of your hand — not with a mallet, which tears it. There is no membrane to snip on a thigh, so that step falls away. Season, rest 15 minutes, pat dry and crumb exactly as written. Fry ONCE at a steady 175°C for about 5 minutes, turning halfway, until deep gold; there is no thick centre here so the two-stage fry gains you nothing and only risks drying it out. Rest on a rack for 2 minutes and slice."}]}},{"name":"Classic Tonkatsu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42},{"name":"Katsu Curry","icon":"🍛","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":52,"howThisFeels":"Dark, thick, faintly sweet curry poured beside — never over — the crust, so you get one bite crisp, the next bite drowned, and both on purpose.","trivia":"Japanese curry arrived from Britain rather than from India, which is why it is thick, mild and brown rather than thin and hot: it came in with the Royal Navy in the Meiji period as a British Indian-style stew, was adopted by the Imperial Japanese Navy as a Friday ration because it was cheap, filling and easy to cook at sea, and spread through the country as sailors went home. It is thickened with a roux, which is a European technique and not an Indian one, and that roux is the whole texture of the dish. Two details make a homemade one taste like a shop one. First, cook the onions far longer than feels sensible — thirty to forty minutes until they are genuinely brown and jammy — because that sweetness is the base note under the spice. Second, a grated apple and a spoonful of honey are standard rather than eccentric, and they are what give Japanese curry its characteristic rounded sweetness. Serve the sauce beside the cutlet and never poured over it, so the crust survives to the last slice.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"80g onion, thinly sliced"},{"item":"60g carrot, cut into chunks"},{"item":"80g potato, cut into chunks"},{"item":"20g butter"},{"item":"18g cake flour, extra, for the roux"},{"item":"8g mild curry powder"},{"item":"3g garam masala"},{"item":"40g apple, grated"},{"item":"10ml honey"},{"item":"300ml chicken or vegetable stock"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the curry first — it is happy to sit while you fry. Cook the sliced onion in the butter over a low heat for a full 30 to 40 minutes, stirring now and then, until it is deep brown, sweet and collapsed. Rushing this is the single reason a home Japanese curry tastes thin. Push the onion aside, stir the extra flour into the butter and cook it for 2 minutes until it smells biscuity, then stir in the curry powder and garam masala and give them 30 seconds in the fat — dry spice bloomed in fat tastes of something, dry spice dropped into liquid does not. Add the stock a little at a time, whisking each addition smooth before the next, or you will get lumps that never come out. Add the carrot, potato, grated apple and honey, and simmer 25 minutes until the potato is soft and the sauce coats a spoon thickly. Fry and slice the tonkatsu exactly as written, put the rice on one side of the plate and the sliced cutlet next to it, and pour the curry over the RICE and against the edge of the cutlet — never over the crust."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Oyakodon","target":"japan-oyakodon","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"},{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"}]},
{"id":"japan-okonomiyaki","name":"Okonomiyaki","nameAlt":"Osaka cabbage pancake","aliases":["Okonomiyaki","Japanese Savoury Pancake","Osaka Pancake","Cabbage Pancake"],"course":"main","type":["main","street-food","bread"],"diet":["omnivore","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","street-food","quick"],"ingredients":"180g white cabbage, cut into 1cm squares · 55g cake flour · 60ml dashi, cold · 1 egg · 35g nagaimo · 2 spring onions, sliced · 20g pickled red ginger (beni shoga), chopped · 60g pork belly, sliced very thin · 15ml neutral oil · 30ml okonomiyaki sauce · 20ml Japanese-style mayonnaise · 2g aonori (dried green seaweed flakes) · 4g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)","method":"The name means as-you-like-it, grilled, and the name is a licence — but there are four things that are not up to you, and they are the difference between the pancake people rave about and the heavy grey disc most people produce at home. Understand first what this actually is. It is NOT a pancake with cabbage in it. It is a large quantity of cabbage held together by a very small quantity of batter, and the ratio is roughly equal weights of cabbage to everything else combined. If your mixture looks like batter with some cabbage stirred through, you have too much batter, and the result will be dense and doughy. It should look, worryingly, like a bowl of cabbage that will never hold together. Cut the cabbage rather than grating or shredding it: rough 1cm squares. Grated cabbage bleeds water and collapses, and you want distinct pieces that steam inside and keep a little bite. Make the batter first and separately: whisk the flour, cold dashi and egg together until just smooth, then grate the nagaimo in. Nagaimo is a mountain yam and when grated it goes slimy and stringy in a way that looks alarming and is exactly right — it aerates the batter and makes the finished pancake light, fluffy and slightly custardy inside rather than flat and chewy. It is genuinely hard to find outside a well-stocked Asian grocer, and rather than pretend otherwise: the honest stand-in is 2g of baking powder and a slightly wetter batter, which gets you most of the lift, though not the texture. Let the batter rest twenty minutes in the fridge. Now the third rule: FOLD THE CABBAGE IN AT THE LAST SECOND, immediately before it goes in the pan, and never before. Cabbage and salt and time make water, and a mixture that has been standing for ten minutes is a wet mixture that will not brown. Fold the cabbage, spring onion and pickled ginger through the rested batter with a spatula, using cutting strokes from the bottom, ten or twelve turns at most. Do not beat it, do not whisk it, do not try to make it smooth. You are coating cabbage, not making a batter, and every extra stir develops gluten and adds weight. Get a heavy pan or a flat griddle to a steady medium heat with the oil — medium, not high, because this needs eight or nine minutes and a hot pan burns the outside long before the middle sets. Pile the mixture in and shape it gently into a round about 2cm thick and 15cm across, using the spatula to tuck the edge in rather than to flatten the top. Lay the thin pork belly slices flat over the top, raw. And now the fourth rule, which is the one everybody breaks: DO NOT PRESS IT DOWN. Every instinct you have will tell you to squash it with the spatula, and it is the single most destructive thing you can do. Everything so far — the nagaimo, the loose folding, the cut cabbage — has been about getting air into it, and one press with a spatula puts you back to a flat rubbery disc with a wet middle. Leave it alone. Four to five minutes, until the edge looks set and dry and a spatula slides under cleanly. Then flip it once, confidently, in a single movement — use two spatulas if you want, and commit, because a hesitant flip is what breaks it. The pork is now against the pan and will crisp there. Another four or five minutes, then flip it back so the pork side is up for a final minute. It is cooked when a skewer into the centre comes out clean and hot. Onto the plate, and dress it in order: brush the okonomiyaki sauce over the top, zigzag the mayonnaise across it, drag a skewer through the lines if you want the marbled look, then scatter the aonori and finally a generous handful of katsuobushi. Take it to the table straight away, and watch the flakes.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Batter rest 20 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~520–620 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 22g, Carbs 48g, Fat 28g, Sodium ~1150mg","storage":"Best within minutes, and the toppings should never be applied to something you intend to keep — sauce and mayonnaise go soggy fast. An undressed pancake keeps 2 days in the fridge and reheats surprisingly well in a dry pan over medium heat for 3 minutes a side, which crisps the outside again; dress it after reheating, never before. It freezes acceptably undressed, wrapped tightly, for a month.","chefNotes":"It is cabbage held together by batter, not the other way round. Cut the cabbage into squares, fold it in at the last second, mix ten turns and no more — and never, ever press it down with the spatula.","pairsWith":"A cold beer, and a bowl of rice if you are treating it as dinner rather than as a snack. Miso soup alongside is standard at home. Nothing rich — the mayonnaise has that covered.","trivia":"There are very few famous dishes where the cooking surface is also the dining table, and okonomiyaki is the clearest one: in a great many restaurants a hot steel teppan is set into the table itself, the mixture arrives in a bowl, and the customer cooks their own. That single fact quietly explains most of the recipe. Everything in it has to survive an amateur — which is why nothing in the dish is delicate, why the cabbage is cut into robust squares rather than shredded fine, why the cooking temperature is a forgiving medium rather than a fierce heat, and why the sauce and mayonnaise are powerful enough to carry a pancake that has been flipped badly. It is a dish engineered around the certainty that the cook does not know what they are doing, and it is generous about it. The regional argument is real and institutional rather than a matter of taste: Hiroshima layers its version instead of mixing it, building crepe, cabbage, pork and a nest of fried noodles in strata, and the city contains a building of several floors housing nothing but okonomiyaki stalls, one after another, in competition. Hiroshima people call the Osaka method the mixed style; Osaka people simply call it okonomiyaki. And the katsuobushi on top is a small piece of deliberate theatre — the flakes are shaved so thin, a few microns, that the convection rising off the hot pancake lifts and moves them, so the whole surface appears to writhe. It settles as the plate cools, and it is meant to be seen.","howThisFeels":"That first flip landing cleanly, the smell of cabbage and pork fat hitting hot steel — and then the bonito flakes on top moving as though the thing were breathing.","leftovers":["(easy) Reheat an undressed pancake in a dry pan for 3 minutes a side and dress it fresh; it comes back far better than it has any right to, because the cabbage inside re-steams itself.","(easy) Leftover batter keeps a day in the fridge — fold fresh cabbage through it in the morning and make a smaller one for breakfast with an egg fried on top.","(creative) Cut a cold undressed pancake into fingers, crisp them hard in a dry pan and serve them with the sauce and mayonnaise as a dip. They eat like a savoury croquette and disappear faster than the original did."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Cabbage & Egg (Budget · Vegetarian)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":16,"howThisFeels":"Sweeter and cleaner without the pork fat, with the cabbage doing all the talking — the version that proves the pancake never needed the meat.","trivia":"Take the pork and the bonito flakes out and the dish barely notices, which tells you something about where its flavour actually comes from: the cabbage, the sauce and the browning. There is one thing to be careful about, and it is invisible. Both the dashi in the batter and the katsuobushi on top are fish, so a vegetarian version has to replace the stock as well as the topping — using the classic dashi and simply leaving off the flakes is the mistake people make. Use the kombu and shiitake dashi instead, which is a better match here anyway because its woodier depth stands in for the missing pork fat. In place of the bonito on top, a heavier scatter of aonori and some toasted sesame does the work.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"60g pork belly, sliced very thin"},{"item":"4g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"40g sweetcorn kernels"},{"item":"5g toasted sesame seeds"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use the kombu and shiitake dashi for the batter rather than the classic — the classic contains bonito and this version does not want it. Fold the sweetcorn in with the cabbage; it adds the sweetness and the little bursts of moisture that the pork fat was providing. With nothing laid on top, the first side needs a slightly longer 5 to 6 minutes so the surface sets properly before the flip. Finish with a heavier hand of aonori than usual and a scatter of toasted sesame in place of the bonito flakes. Check the okonomiyaki sauce you are using — several commercial ones contain fish extract, and a home mix of 3 parts tomato sauce, 2 parts Worcestershire-style sauce, 1 part soy and 1 part honey is close enough and takes a minute."}]}},{"name":"Classic Osaka Pork Belly","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":26},{"name":"Hiroshima-Style, Layered with Noodles","icon":"🍜","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":38,"howThisFeels":"A tower rather than a pancake — crisp noodles, a mountain of sweet steamed cabbage and a soft fried egg holding the whole thing down.","trivia":"Hiroshima's version is not a variation on the Osaka one so much as a rebuttal of it. Nothing is mixed: a paper-thin crepe goes down first, then an enormous heap of cabbage on top of it, then the pork, and the whole tower is flipped as a unit and left to steam under its own weight for several minutes while a nest of noodles fries alongside and an egg cooks flat on the griddle. It is then assembled in reverse. The engineering reason for the layers is that the cabbage steams rather than fries, so you can use far more of it — roughly double — without it going wet, and the sweetness of a large volume of gently steamed cabbage is the whole character of the dish. It is also considerably harder to do at home than the Osaka style, and the flip is the moment it lives or dies, which is precisely why Hiroshima cooks are proud of it.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"100g cabbage, extra, shredded"},{"item":"120g cooked yakisoba noodles"},{"item":"1 extra egg"},{"item":"30g beansprouts"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Do not mix anything. Thin the batter with an extra 30ml of dashi and spread a very thin crepe, about 18cm across, on the griddle. Dust it with a little aonori, then pile ALL the cabbage and the beansprouts on top in a tall heap — it will look absurd and it is meant to. Lay the thin pork slices over the heap. Cook 4 minutes, then slide two spatulas underneath and flip the entire tower in one committed movement; if it collapses at the edges, push it back in and carry on. Cover with a lid or a bowl and let it steam 6 to 8 minutes, during which the cabbage will sink to about a third of its height. Meanwhile fry the cooked noodles hard in a little oil on another part of the pan until the edges crisp, shape them into a round the same size as the pancake, and slide the whole tower on top of them. Fry the egg flat, slide the tower onto the egg, and flip the lot one final time so the egg is on top. Sauce, mayonnaise and aonori as usual."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🍖"},{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"}]},
{"id":"japan-oyakodon","name":"Oyakodon","nameAlt":"Parent-and-child rice bowl","aliases":["Oyakodon","Chicken and Egg Rice Bowl","Oyako Donburi"],"course":"main","type":["main","breakfast"],"diet":["omnivore","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","quick","family"],"ingredients":"120g chicken thighs, boned, cut into 2cm pieces · 60g onion, sliced thinly with the grain · 120ml dashi · 20ml light soy sauce · 20ml mirin · 10ml sake · 5g white sugar · 2 eggs · 200g hot cooked short-grain rice · 1 spring onion, sliced · 1g shichimi togarashi, to serve · 5g mitsuba or flat-leaf parsley, optional","method":"This is a ten-minute dish with one genuinely unusual instruction in it, and if you take nothing else from this card, take that one: it is served DELIBERATELY UNDERCOOKED, and the residual heat and the hot rice finish it in the bowl. A properly set oyakodon is an overcooked oyakodon. Everything else follows from that. First, the practical rule that shapes the whole method: THIS IS COOKED ONE PORTION AT A TIME AND IS NOT SCALED UP. In Japan it is made in a small dedicated pan roughly the width of the bowl it will slide into, and the reason is geometry rather than tradition. The dish depends on a shallow, wide layer of liquid so that the egg sets in seconds rather than minutes; double the quantity in the same pan and you have a deep pool that must be stirred, and stirring turns it into scrambled egg in broth. If you are feeding four people, cook four bowls one after another, and it will take you twelve minutes rather than three. Slice the onion with the grain rather than across it — with the grain the slices hold their shape and stay slightly sweet and firm; cut across, they collapse into strings. Put the dashi, soy, mirin, sake and sugar into a small frying pan or shallow saucepan, add the onion, and simmer it for three or four minutes until the onion is translucent and just tender. Taste the liquid at this point, because this is the only moment you can adjust anything: it should taste distinctly sweet-savoury and a little strong, since it is about to be diluted by two eggs and served over unseasoned rice. Add the chicken in a single layer, spread out, and simmer for four to five minutes until just cooked through. Do not stir it about; nudge the pieces once so they are evenly covered. Now the eggs, and here is the technique that makes the difference between this and a plain chicken-and-egg pan. Break the eggs into a bowl and beat them BADLY — five or six strokes with chopsticks or a fork, just enough to break the yolks and streak them through the white, and no further. You want visible ribbons of yolk and white rather than a uniform yellow, because those two set at different speeds and give the finished bowl its marbled, layered look. A properly beaten egg gives you a flat single-textured sheet. Then pour it in TWO POURS. Pour roughly two-thirds of the egg in a spiral over the whole surface, cover the pan, and give it forty-five to sixty seconds over a medium heat. Then take the lid off, pour the remaining third over the top, put the lid back on and count to twenty. Kill the heat. The bottom layer is now set and holding everything together; the top layer is barely cooked, glossy and still moving — and that contrast between set and almost-liquid in the same mouthful is the entire dish. Do not be tempted to give it another thirty seconds because it looks underdone. It IS underdone, on purpose, and it will carry on cooking on its way to the table. Have the rice already in a deep bowl, hot. Slide the whole thing out of the pan in one piece, using the lid or a spatula to keep the layers intact rather than tipping it in, and let it sit on top of the rice without stirring it in. Scatter the spring onion, add the mitsuba if you have it, and a pinch of shichimi togarashi. Eat it immediately, and eat it with a spoon.","cookTime":"Prep 5 min · Cook 8 min","kcal":"~610–700 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g, Carbs 74g, Fat 16g, Sodium ~1250mg","storage":"Honestly, do not keep it. The entire dish is a texture that exists for about ninety seconds, and a refrigerated oyakodon is set egg on cold rice, which is a different and much sadder thing. If you must, it keeps a day and is best reheated gently in a covered pan with a splash of dashi rather than in a microwave. The seasoning liquid — dashi, soy, mirin, sake and sugar — is worth making in a larger quantity and keeping: it is the base of half the donburi family and lasts a fortnight in the fridge.","chefNotes":"One bowl at a time in a small pan, never scaled up. Slice the onion with the grain. Beat the eggs badly — five strokes. Two pours, lid on, and stop while the top is still loose. It finishes in the bowl.","pairsWith":"A bowl of miso soup and something pickled and sharp — that is the whole meal and it wants nothing else. Green tea rather than beer.","trivia":"The name is oyako — parent and child — and it means precisely what you think it means: the chicken and the egg in the same bowl. It is printed on menus across Japan without comment, and it is one of the more startling pieces of matter-of-fact naming in any cuisine. The dish is usually traced to 1891 at Tamahide, a restaurant in Nihonbashi in Tokyo that specialised in shamo, a gamecock breed, served as hotpot. The story is that customers finishing a hotpot were in the habit of dipping the last of the chicken and broth into beaten egg and tipping it over rice, and that the proprietor's wife formalised the leftover ritual into a dish and put it on the menu. Tamahide is still trading and still queued for. What makes the story more than a nice anecdote is what it says about the whole donburi category, which is Japan's fast food and is built almost entirely on this logic: something savoury, cooked in a seasoned liquid, poured over a bowl of rice. Gyudon is beef, katsudon is a fried pork cutlet given the same egg treatment, tendon is tempura. They are cheap, they are fast, they are eaten standing up, and every one of them is the same idea — that rice is the meal and the thing on top of it is the seasoning, which is close to the opposite of how a Western plate is put together.","howThisFeels":"Lifting the lid and finding the top still wobbling — and knowing to take it off the heat anyway, then that first spoonful where set egg, loose egg, sweet onion and hot rice all arrive at once.","leftovers":["(easy) The seasoning liquid is the real leftover here — make triple and keep it in a jar. It turns thin-sliced beef into gyudon, a fried cutlet into katsudon and a pan of mushrooms into a very good vegetarian bowl, all in under ten minutes.","(easy) Any leftover cooked chicken and onion, chopped small, folded into fried rice with a beaten egg the next morning — the seasoning is already done and it needs nothing.","(creative) Spread cold leftovers into a small oiled pan, pour a fresh beaten egg over and cook it flat like a thick omelette, then cut it into wedges cold for a lunchbox. It is a real thing in Japan and it travels far better than the original bowl does."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Mushroom & Tofu Oyakodon (Budget · Vegetarian)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":18,"howThisFeels":"Woodier and darker than the original, with mushrooms that have gone silky in the sweet broth and the same trembling egg on top.","trivia":"The name stops making literal sense the moment the chicken leaves, and Japanese menus are relaxed about this — a version of this bowl made with mushrooms and tofu is common in temple-influenced cooking and simply gets called a mushroom donburi. There is a real technical gain as well as a saving: mushrooms give up their own liquid into the broth, so the sauce ends up deeper and more savoury than the chicken version manages in the same time. Use two or three kinds if you can — a firm one for bite and a soft one for silkiness — and fry them hard in a dry pan first, before any liquid, so their water drives off and they brown rather than stew. The tofu wants to go in late and be left alone.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"120g chicken thighs, boned, cut into 2cm pieces","to":"140g mixed mushrooms, thickly sliced"},{"from":"120ml dashi","to":"120ml kombu and shiitake dashi"}],"addIng":[{"item":"80g firm tofu, cut into 2cm cubes"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Dry-fry the mushrooms hard in the empty pan first, with no oil and no liquid, for 4 to 5 minutes until they have given up their water, it has boiled away, and they are browning at the edges. This step is not optional — mushrooms added straight to the broth stew and go grey. Set them aside, then build the seasoned liquid with the onion exactly as written, return the mushrooms to it with the tofu cubes, and simmer 3 minutes. Handle the tofu as little as possible from that point; it wants to be nudged, not stirred. Then the two egg pours and the lid, unchanged."}]}},{"name":"Classic Oyakodon","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30},{"name":"Katsudon — the Fried Cutlet Version","icon":"🍖","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"A crumbed cutlet half-drowned in sweet broth and soft egg, one edge still crisp above the waterline — the most comforting bowl in Japan and not remotely a coincidence.","trivia":"Katsudon is oyakodon with a fried pork cutlet in place of the chicken, and it has a peculiar cultural life: it is the traditional thing a Japanese student eats the night before an exam, because katsu is a homophone of the verb to win, and it is such a fixture of police dramas as the meal offered to a suspect in an interrogation room that the trope is instantly recognisable. Technically there is exactly one thing to get right and it is timing. The cutlet is already cooked and already crisp, and every second it spends in liquid is a second of that crispness lost — so it goes in sliced, at the very last moment, for barely a minute before the egg, and the egg goes over it rather than around it. Done properly you get a cutlet that is soaked and soft underneath and still audibly crisp along its top edge, in the same mouthful. Done slowly you get wet breadcrumbs.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"120g chicken thighs, boned, cut into 2cm pieces","to":"1 tonkatsu, fried and sliced into 2cm strips"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry a tonkatsu exactly as its own card describes and slice it, then let it rest on a rack while you make the broth — do not let it sit on a plate, where the underside will go soft before it ever reaches the pan. Simmer the onion in the seasoned liquid as written, then lay the sliced cutlet in, keeping the slices together in their original shape, and give it 45 to 60 seconds only. Then straight to the two egg pours and the lid. The whole point is a cutlet that is soft where the broth reached it and still crisp along the top, so the shorter the contact the better — err on the side of too fast rather than too long."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🍖"},{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"}]},
{"id":"japan-gyoza","name":"Gyoza","nameAlt":"Yaki-gyoza — pan-fried dumplings with a lace skirt","aliases":["Gyoza","Yaki Gyoza","Japanese Pan-Fried Dumplings","Potstickers"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","main","snack"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","party","family"],"ingredients":"90g white cabbage, finely chopped · 2g fine salt (for the cabbage) · 100g pork mince (20% fat) · 15g garlic chives, finely sliced · 5g garlic, grated · 4g fresh ginger, grated · 8ml soy sauce · 5ml sesame oil · 3ml sake · 2g sugar · 1g white pepper · 10 gyoza wrappers, the thinnest you can find · 10ml sunflower oil · 5g plain flour · 100ml water · 15ml rice vinegar · 8ml soy sauce, to dip · 2ml chilli oil, to dip","method":"Gyoza are two separate skills wearing one name: a filling that must be dry, and a fry that must be committed to. Get those right and everything else is folding practice. Start with the cabbage, because the cabbage is the dish's one real enemy. Chop it as finely as you have patience for — 2 to 3mm, not shredded and not minced to mush — then toss it with the 2g of salt in a bowl and walk away for fifteen minutes. It will slump and go glassy and a puddle will appear underneath it. Now gather it in both hands and WRING IT OUT properly, over the sink, squeezing until it genuinely hurts your hands and no more liquid comes; you should end up with a tight, damp little ball that has lost close to a third of its weight. Throw that liquid away. This is the single step that separates a gyoza that seals, fries and holds together from one that steams itself soggy from the inside, splits along the seam and spits fat at you the moment it hits the pan. It also seasons the cabbage all the way through, which is why no more salt appears anywhere in this recipe. Now the filling. Put the wrung cabbage in a bowl with the pork mince, garlic chives, grated garlic and ginger, soy, sesame oil, sake, sugar and white pepper, and mix it with your hand until it is completely uniform and starting to feel tacky rather than loose. Do not overwork it into a paste — you want it homogenous, not emulsified. Chill it for twenty minutes if you have time, because a cold filling is far easier to fold. Take a moment on the wrappers, because Japan's wrapper is the reason a gyoza is not a jiaozi. A Japanese gyoza wrapper is noticeably thinner than a Chinese boiling wrapper — machine-rolled thin, almost translucent at the edge — and the reason is simple: this dumpling is fried, not boiled, and A THICK WRAPPER WILL NEVER CRISP. It just turns leathery and chewy and no amount of extra pan time fixes it, because the problem is thickness, not colour. Buy the thinnest packet you can find, keep them under a damp cloth while you work, and if the packet has been in the freezer let them come fully to room temperature or they will crack along every fold. To fold: a rounded teaspoon of filling in the centre, wet the edge of the far half with a fingertip of water, fold into a half moon and pinch it shut at the top point. Then, working from one end, make five or six small pleats in the front side only, pressing each into the flat back side as you go — the back stays smooth, the pleats are all on the face. This is not decoration. Pleating on one side only is what pulls the dumpling into a curve and gives it a FLAT BASE that stands up on its own, and a gyoza that stands is a gyoza that fries evenly. As you finish each one, press it lightly down onto the board to set that flat bottom and stand it on a floured tray. Now the fry, and once it starts you are committed. Mix the 5g of flour into the 100ml of water and stir it until it is smooth and milky — this is the hane, the lace skirt, and it is the reason Japanese gyoza look the way they do. Heat the sunflower oil in a non-stick or well-seasoned frying pan over a medium-high heat until it shimmers, then arrange the gyoza flat-side down in a tight spiral or a fan, all of them touching. Arrange them properly now, because ONCE THE LIQUID GOES IN YOU CANNOT MOVE THEM. Fry undisturbed for two minutes until the bases are properly golden — lift one to check, and do not go early. Then stir the flour-water once more and pour it in around them until it comes about a third of the way up, clap the lid on and turn the heat down to medium. Six to seven minutes, and leave the lid alone. The wrappers will go translucent and puff slightly. When the loose liquid has gone, take the lid off, turn the heat back up and let it finish. Here is where nerve is required: THE SKIRT SHOULD GO PAST GOLDEN, well past — it is a sheet of cooked starch, not a dumpling, and it wants to be deep brown, brittle and lacy before you stop. Everyone pulls it too early. You will hear the sound in the pan change from a wet bubbling to a dry, high crackle and you will see the lace lift very slightly at the edges when you tilt the pan; that is it. Kill the heat, put a plate face down over the pan, and invert the whole thing in one confident movement so they land skirt-side UP, joined in a single golden disc. Mix the rice vinegar, dipping soy and chilli oil in a small saucer — sharper than you would expect, because it is cutting through a great deal of fat and garlic — and eat them immediately, while the lace still shatters.","cookTime":"Prep 40 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~520–600 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 26g, Carbs 48g, Fat 28g, Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"Uncooked gyoza freeze beautifully and this is the real reason to make a big batch: freeze them on the floured tray, spaced apart, until solid, then bag them. They go into the pan straight from frozen with no thawing at all — same method, two extra minutes under the lid — and they are genuinely as good as fresh. Uncooked and unfrozen they will not keep, because the wet filling starts softening the wrappers within a couple of hours and they weld to the tray. Cooked ones keep two days in the fridge; reheat in a dry pan base-down with a splash of water and the lid on, never in a microwave, which turns the wrapper to putty.","chefNotes":"Salt the cabbage and wring it until it hurts. Thinnest wrappers you can buy. Pleat one side only so the base is flat. Arrange them before the liquid goes in, because after it you cannot move them. Let the lace skirt go darker than feels safe.","pairsWith":"Cold beer, and nothing else pretending to be a starter. If it is the meal, a bowl of rice and something sharply pickled.","trivia":"Gyoza came home in the kit bags of repatriated soldiers, and Japan adopted the wrong version on purpose. Dumplings in northern China are overwhelmingly boiled; pan-frying them is what you do with yesterday's leftovers, which is why the Chinese name for potstickers refers to the pan. Japan skipped the original entirely — there is essentially no boiled-gyoza culture, and yaki-gyoza is simply what the word means. The route is documented: after 1945 several million Japanese civilians and soldiers were repatriated from Manchuria and northern China, and the ones who had learned to cook there came back to a country with almost no food and set up stalls with what they knew. The two cities that became gyoza towns, Utsunomiya and Hamamatsu, are both places tied to those returning divisions, and they now trade the national top spot for household gyoza spending every year, a statistic reported with the seriousness of a sports result. The garlic is the other Japanese fingerprint. Northern Chinese jiaozi frequently contain none, and are eaten with raw garlic on the side instead. Japanese gyoza is built on it, which is exactly why it was for decades a slightly disreputable thing — food for after drinking, not before work.","howThisFeels":"That moment of nerve at the end, waiting for the lace to go one shade darker than feels wise — then the plate inverted over the pan, and a whole golden disc landing in one piece.","leftovers":["(easy) Any filling left over after the wrappers run out is a meatball mix. Roll it into small balls, fry them and simmer them for five minutes in a splash of soy, water and sugar.","(easy) Cooked leftover gyoza, chopped in half and dropped into a bowl of hot broth with noodles, are excellent — the wrapper softens back into something dumpling-like and the fried base holds.","(creative) Fry cold gyoza base-down in a little oil, then pour a beaten egg with a spoonful of the flour-water over the whole panful and let it set into a flat omelette around them. Cut into wedges."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Chicken & Spring Onion (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":17,"howThisFeels":"Lighter and cleaner in the mouth, with the garlic doing more of the talking because there is less fat to hide behind.","trivia":"Chicken mince is leaner than pork and that is a problem, not a saving: fat is what keeps the filling juicy while the wrapper crisps, and a lean gyoza goes dry and grainy. The fix is not more oil in the pan but more moisture inside — keep a little more of the cabbage water in this version by wringing slightly less hard, and add a teaspoon of cold water to the mince as you mix. Chicken thigh mince, if you can get it, solves it outright.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"100g pork mince (20% fat)","to":"100g chicken mince, thigh if possible"},{"from":"15g garlic chives, finely sliced","to":"15g spring onion, finely sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Wring the cabbage a touch less aggressively than the classic, and mix 5ml of cold water into the chicken along with the seasonings. Lean mince has no fat reserve to keep it juicy while the wrapper fries, so the moisture has to be put in deliberately. Everything else is unchanged."}]}},{"name":"Classic Pork & Garlic Chive","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":25,"howThisFeels":"Garlic and chive hitting you before the plate has landed — the smell that is the entire reason gyoza is drinking food.","trivia":"Nira, garlic chives, are the specific green here and they are not interchangeable with spring onion or ordinary chives. They are flatter, softer and far more pungent, they collapse into the filling rather than staying stringy, and their flavour is closer to garlic than to onion. Most Asian grocers carry them and they grow like a weed in a South African garden, coming back every year from the same clump.","delta":{}},{"name":"Shiitake & Tofu (Vegan)","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":21,"howThisFeels":"Deep and woody rather than rich, and the one people go back for without noticing what is missing.","trivia":"Vegetable gyoza fail for the opposite reason meat ones do: not too much water, but no binder and no fat. Firm tofu crumbled and squeezed replaces the body, dried shiitake replaces the savoury depth, and a spoonful of sesame paste replaces the fat that carries it. Fry the mushrooms hard before they go in rather than adding them raw, because raw mushrooms release water inside the wrapper at exactly the wrong moment. Almost all commercial gyoza wrappers are flour, water and salt only, but check the packet for egg.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"100g pork mince (20% fat)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"12g dried shiitake mushrooms, soaked and finely chopped"},{"item":"90g firm tofu, crumbled and squeezed dry"},{"item":"8g white sesame paste"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Soak the dried shiitake in warm water for twenty minutes, squeeze them out and chop them finely, keeping a tablespoon of the strained soaking liquid. Fry them hard in a dry pan for three or four minutes until they squeak and start to brown — raw mushroom lets go of its water inside the wrapper, which is precisely where you do not want it. Crumble the tofu into a clean cloth and wring it out the same way you wrung the cabbage. Mix the tofu, fried mushrooms, sesame paste and the reserved spoonful of soaking liquid into the cabbage with all the seasonings, and fold as normal. The mix is looser than a meat filling, so chill it properly before you fold or it will fight you."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"},{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"}]},
{"id":"japan-chawanmushi","name":"Chawanmushi","nameAlt":"Savoury steamed egg custard, eaten with a spoon","aliases":["Chawanmushi","Chawan Mushi","Japanese Steamed Egg Custard","Savoury Egg Custard"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","light"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","special","family"],"ingredients":"1 egg, about 50g out of the shell · 150ml dashi · 5ml light soy sauce · 5ml mirin · 3ml sake · 1g fine salt · 30g chicken thigh, in 1cm pieces · 2 prawns, peeled and deveined · 1 fresh shiitake mushroom, thinly sliced · 15g kamaboko fish cake, sliced · 3 sprigs mitsuba","method":"This is a recipe with exactly one number in it, and everything else is decoration. THE RATIO IS THREE PARTS DASHI TO ONE PART EGG, BY WEIGHT — and you weigh the egg, which is a strange instruction in a home kitchen and the reason most chawanmushi go wrong. Eggs are not a unit of measurement. A large egg gives about 50g out of the shell and a small one gives closer to 40, so a recipe saying 'one egg to 150ml of stock' is quietly asking for anywhere between 3:1 and nearly 4:1 without telling you. Below about two and a half to one you have not made a custard, you have made a firm savoury omelette standing up in a cup. Above about four to one it will not set at all, and no extra steaming time will rescue it — you will simply have hot seasoned soup with an egg in it. So crack the egg into a small bowl on the scale, read the number, and multiply it by three. That is your dashi quantity. Now season the liquid, and season it before the egg goes anywhere near it, because THIS IS THE ONLY MOMENT YOU CAN TASTE IT. Warm the dashi very gently with the soy, mirin, sake and salt just until the salt dissolves, then take it off and LET IT COOL to no more than lukewarm. Hot dashi poured into raw egg starts cooking it on contact in fine ribbons, and those ribbons will not dissolve, will not strain out and will sit in the finished custard as visible threads. Taste the seasoned dashi now. It should taste like a slightly under-salted clear soup you would happily drink — remember that it is about to be diluted by a third with unseasoned egg, so it needs to be a shade stronger than you want the finished dish. Beat the egg gently with chopsticks or a fork, cutting through it rather than whipping, until the white is fully broken up and no clear jelly remains, but stopping well before it froths. Pour the cooled dashi in, stir to combine, then pass the whole thing through a fine sieve into a jug. Now build the cups, and the rule for the fillings is short: EVERYTHING GOING IN MUST ALREADY BE COOKED, CUT SMALL AND COOLED. The custard sets in twelve minutes at a temperature deliberately kept low, and nothing raw cooks properly in that window. A raw prawn dropped in will release its water into the setting custard and leave a wet hollow around itself; raw chicken is worse and is a food-safety problem as well as a texture one. So poach the chicken pieces and the prawns for a minute in a little dashi or salted water, drain them, and let them cool. Divide the chicken, prawns, sliced shiitake and kamaboko between two small heatproof cups or one large one, then pour the egg mixture over, filling to about two centimetres below the rim. Skim off any bubbles on the surface with the corner of a piece of kitchen paper. NOW COVER EACH CUP — a small lid, or a square of foil pressed on. This is not to keep heat in. It is because a steamer lid drips, and a single drop of condensation landing on setting custard leaves a crater in the surface; a chawanmushi is judged on being a flat mirror, and a pockmarked one has been photographed with its mistakes. Get the steamer going first so it is already at full steam, then turn it down until the water is at a bare, lazy simmer before the cups go in. This custard wants around 85°C, not 100, and a hard rolling steam blows it up into a bubbled honeycomb that weeps liquid the moment your spoon goes in — the failure looks like a sponge and it is not reversible. Wedge a chopstick under the steamer lid to let some heat escape and keep the pot honest. Steam for twelve to fourteen minutes for small cups. The test is not a wobble, it is a skewer: push a thin skewer or a knife tip in at the edge and watch what wells up. IF THE LIQUID THAT RISES IS CLEAR, IT IS DONE. If it is cloudy and yellow, that is uncooked egg and it needs another two minutes. Lay the mitsuba or spring onion on top for the last thirty seconds, just long enough to wilt and turn a deeper green. Serve it hot, in its cup, with a spoon.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 14 min","kcal":"~170–210 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 22g, Carbs 4g, Fat 8g, Sodium ~950mg","storage":"Two days covered in the fridge, and it is one of the rare custards that is genuinely good cold — served chilled in summer it is a completely legitimate version, not a compromise. Do not reheat it in a microwave under any circumstances: the custard passes straight from set to curdled and separates into scrambled egg sitting in a puddle. If you want it hot again, put the covered cup back in a gentle steamer for four or five minutes. The seasoned dashi base keeps a week in the fridge and is worth making in quantity, because it is also the liquid for udon broth and for simmered vegetables.","chefNotes":"Weigh the egg and use three times its weight in dashi. Season and taste the dashi before the egg goes in — after that you are blind. Everything inside must be cooked and cool. Cover each cup or the steamer will drip craters into it. Bare simmer, never a hard steam. Skewer test: clear liquid means done.","pairsWith":"It opens a meal rather than joining one — a cup of it before grilled fish and rice, or alongside a tray of sashimi. Warm sake or green tea.","trivia":"It is one of the very few dishes in Japanese cuisine eaten with a spoon, and that spoon is the fingerprint of a foreign parent. A Japanese place setting is chopsticks, and dishes are designed around them; a bowl of soup is drunk straight from the bowl. Chawanmushi arrives with a spoon because it came in through Nagasaki, the single port left open during the two centuries of national seclusion, where Chinese and Dutch traders were confined and where a hybrid banquet cuisine called shippoku developed — Japanese ingredients on a round Chinese table with shared dishes and, notably, spoons. The restaurant generally credited with putting it on a menu, Yossou in Nagasaki, has been serving it since 1866 and is still open. The name is the other tell: chawan means tea bowl and mushi means steamed, so the dish is named for its container rather than its contents, because it was originally made in whatever teacup was to hand. The lidded, purpose-made chawanmushi cups sold today are a later formalisation of a dish whose whole original character was improvisation.","howThisFeels":"The lid coming off a small warm cup, and a surface so flat it reflects the ceiling — then the spoon going through it with almost no resistance at all.","leftovers":["(easy) Eat it cold from the fridge with a little grated ginger on top. In summer this is not a compromise, it is how it is served.","(easy) The seasoned dashi base, made in triple quantity, is the broth for udon and the simmering liquid for pumpkin or daikon — it lasts a week and saves the seasoning step entirely.","(creative) Break up leftover custard into a bowl of hot udon broth. It fragments into soft savoury clouds and turns a plain noodle bowl into something that tastes far more considered than it was."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Mushroom & Spring Onion (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":9,"howThisFeels":"Quieter and woodier, and somehow more comforting for having nothing in it to chase.","trivia":"Without egg there is nothing to set, so this version stops being a custard and becomes a savoury silken pudding — the mechanism is different but the mouthfeel lands in the same place. Silken tofu blitzed smooth carries the body, and a small amount of kudzu or cornflour does the setting. The ratio law still applies in spirit: too much liquid and it slumps, too little and it is tofu in a cup. The dashi must be the kombu-and-shiitake version, and it should be a strong one, because there is no egg richness to lean on.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"1 egg, about 50g out of the shell"},{"item":"30g chicken thigh, in 1cm pieces"},{"item":"2 prawns, peeled and deveined"},{"item":"15g kamaboko fish cake, sliced"}],"addIng":[{"item":"120g silken tofu"},{"item":"8g cornflour"},{"item":"30g mixed mushrooms, sliced and fried"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use the kombu-and-shiitake dashi and make it stronger than usual — soak the kombu overnight and use double the dried shiitake — because there is no egg or bonito to carry it. Blitz the silken tofu completely smooth with the cornflour and 100ml of the cooled seasoned dashi, then sieve it, which matters more here than with egg because tofu holds grain. Fry the mushrooms hard first and let them cool. Steam exactly as the classic, covered, at a bare simmer, for fourteen to sixteen minutes. The skewer test does not work on this one — instead, tilt the cup gently: a set pudding moves as a single mass, an unset one ripples."}]}},{"name":"Classic Chawanmushi","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":21,"howThisFeels":"Finding a prawn at the bottom of the cup you had entirely forgotten was coming.","trivia":"Mitsuba is worth hunting down rather than substituting. It looks like flat-leaf parsley and tastes like nothing else — somewhere between celery leaf, chervil and coriander stem — and it is one of the very small handful of herbs native to Japan rather than imported. Ginkgo nuts are the other classic inclusion, small, jade-green and faintly bitter, sold tinned; they are a genuinely acquired taste and leaving them out is not a failure of authenticity.","delta":{}},{"name":"Odamaki Mushi — with Udon (Osaka)","icon":"🍜","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"Spoon going in expecting custard and hitting noodles — a starter that turns out to have been dinner all along.","trivia":"Odamaki mushi is chawanmushi with a nest of udon hidden underneath, and it is an Osaka speciality that turns a delicate opening course into an entire winter meal in one bowl. The name refers to a ball of wound thread, which is what the coiled noodles look like through the custard. It needs a bigger bowl and a longer steam, and the noodles must be fully cooked and rinsed cold first, because a dry noodle drinks the custard and you end up with a bowl of stodge. It is also the version that proves the ratio law from the other direction: use the same 3:1 mix and simply pour more of it.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"120g cooked udon noodles, rinsed and drained"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Use one large bowl rather than small cups, and increase the egg-and-dashi mix by half — weighing the egg and multiplying by three exactly as before, because the ratio does not change, only the quantity. Cook the udon, rinse it under cold water until it is completely cold and drain it very well; a warm or wet noodle bleeds starch and water into the custard. Coil it into the bottom of the bowl, arrange the chicken, prawns and mushroom on top of it, and pour the mixture over. Cover and steam at the same bare simmer for twenty to twenty-two minutes — the extra depth needs the extra time, and the skewer test at the edge is still the only reliable one."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Oyakodon","target":"japan-oyakodon","emoji":"🍚"},{"name":"Gyoza","target":"japan-gyoza","emoji":"🥟"}]},
{"id":"japan-yakitori","name":"Yakitori","nameAlt":"Charcoal-grilled chicken skewers — negima, shio and tare","aliases":["Yakitori","Negima","Japanese Chicken Skewers","Grilled Chicken Skewers"],"course":"main","type":["main","starter","grill"],"diet":["omnivore"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","party","family","braai"],"ingredients":"180g chicken thighs, boneless with the skin on, in 25g pieces · 60g spring onions or baby leeks, in 3cm lengths · 15g chicken wing tips or bones · 45ml Japanese soy sauce · 45ml mirin · 20ml sake · 12g sugar · 2g fine sea salt · 1g sansho pepper · 5ml sunflower oil · 4 bamboo skewers, soaked 30 minutes","method":"Yakitori looks like the simplest thing on any Japanese menu and is in fact a set of small decisions made in the right order, none of which is difficult and all of which show. Start by making the tare, because it wants half an hour and it improves while you do everything else. Grill or dry-fry the chicken wing tips or bones hard in a small pan until they are properly browned all over — this is where the tare's depth comes from and pale bones give a thin, one-note sauce. Add the soy, mirin, sake and sugar, bring it to a simmer and let it reduce gently for twenty-five to thirty minutes until it coats a spoon and falls off it slowly, like thin cream. Strain it and set it aside; it will thicken further as it cools, so stop it looser than you want it. Now the skewering, and this is where most home yakitori is lost. THE SKEWER IS A COOKING TOOL, NOT A WAY OF CARRYING FOOD TO THE TABLE. Cut the thigh into pieces of genuinely equal thickness — not equal weight, equal thickness, because thickness is what determines cooking time and a fat piece next to a thin one guarantees that one of them is wrong. Thread them so that they TOUCH BUT ARE NOT COMPRESSED. A gap between pieces exposes two cut faces to direct heat and they dry out and go grey; pieces jammed hard together shield each other so completely that the middle is still raw when the outsides are done. They should sit against each other the way books sit on a shelf. For the classic negima, alternate chicken and spring onion, and understand that the onion is not there for colour: it releases steam right beside the chicken as it cooks, which keeps the meat from drying, and a 3cm length of spring onion happens to cook in almost exactly the time a 25g piece of thigh does. Fold the skin outward on each piece rather than tucking it in, so it faces the fire and has a chance to crisp. Now the fire, and here is the law that decides everything: YOU ARE COOKING OVER RADIANT HEAT AND THERE MUST BE NO FLAME. Let the coals burn all the way down until they are covered in grey ash and glowing steadily with nothing visible above them. Chicken thigh with the skin on drips fat continuously, fat meeting flame produces soot, and soot lands on the meat and tastes exactly like what it is. If a flare-up starts, move the skewers — DO NOT SPRAY IT WITH WATER, which lifts a cloud of ash straight onto the food and puts out the part of the fire you needed. Set the skewers close to the coals, about 8 to 10cm, and lay the exposed wooden ends over the edge of the grill or shield them with a strip of foil so they do not burn through. Turn them every minute or so. You want six to eight minutes total for thigh, and the test is the fat: when the skin has stopped weeping and has gone from soft to visibly tight and glossy, the piece is nearly there. Now the seasoning decision, which is not a matter of taste but of the cut, and every yakitori-ya in Japan makes it the same way. SHIO — SALT — IS FOR LEAN AND DELICATE THINGS, TARE IS FOR FAT AND STRONG ONES. Breast, wing and gizzard go shio, because tare is sweet and dark and would simply bury them. Thigh, skin and liver take tare, because they have the fat and the flavour to stand up to it. And the timing of each is opposite: SHIO GOES ON BEFORE, salted lightly the moment the skewers are threaded so it has time to draw and season; TARE GOES ON AT THE END AND NEVER AT THE START. Tare is a third sugar by the time it has reduced, and sugar over live coals burns black in under a minute, so it goes on only for the last third of the cooking, in THREE THIN COATS with a turn between each — brush, turn, thirty seconds, brush, turn, thirty seconds, brush, turn, off. Three thin layers build a lacquer that clings; one thick layer slides off into the fire and takes the flavour with it. Brush from a small bowl decanted from the main tare, and never dip a raw skewer into the master pot — the reason a yakitori shop can keep the same tare going for years is that only cooked meat ever touches it. Serve the shio skewers with a pinch of sansho pepper, which is a citrus-scented relative of the Sichuan peppercorn and does for salt-grilled chicken what lemon does for fish, and the tare skewers straight off the grill while the lacquer is still tacky. Eat them with your fingers, off the skewer, standing up if possible.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 30 min tare + 8 min grill","kcal":"~480–560 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 42g, Carbs 22g, Fat 26g, Sodium ~1400mg","storage":"The tare is the thing worth keeping and it is close to indestructible: it is a reduced, sugared, salted, alcohol-cooked liquid, which is to say it is a preserve. Keep it in a clean jar in the fridge and it will hold for a month or more, deepening as it goes; top it up with a fresh batch rather than starting again, and every time you make yakitori it gets better, because each grilling adds a little rendered chicken fat and juice back into it. Bring it to a simmer once a fortnight if it is being topped up regularly. Cooked skewers keep two days and are best revived under a hot grill for a minute rather than in a pan — they were cooked by radiant heat and they reheat best the same way.","chefNotes":"Equal thickness, not equal weight. Pieces touching but never compressed. Grey coals, no flame, and never water on a flare-up. Salt goes on before; tare goes on only in the last third, in three thin coats with a turn between each. Decant the tare you brush with — nothing raw ever touches the main pot.","pairsWith":"Cold beer, ice-cold, and a bowl of cabbage wedges with a salty dip to eat between skewers. Rice only if it is the whole meal rather than the start of one.","trivia":"The fuel is the famous part, and it explains the equipment. Binchotan is a white charcoal made from ubame oak, fired at very high temperature and then smothered in a damp mix of ash and sand, which is what leaves the pale coating that gives it its name. What comes out is close to pure carbon: it burns with almost no smoke and no flame at all, holds around a thousand degrees, and lasts for hours. Two pieces struck together ring like metal rather than knocking like wood, and that sound is part of how it is graded. Because it gives radiant infrared heat rather than smoke and hot air, the grill built for it is long, narrow and open, with no lid anywhere in sight — the entire geometry of a yakitori counter follows from the properties of the charcoal, and a lid would be actively wrong. It is also expensive enough to be a genuine line item in a small restaurant's costs, which is why a good yakitori-ya charges what it does for what is, on paper, a few pieces of chicken on a stick.","howThisFeels":"The particular hiss and sweet smell the moment tare hits hot coals — and knowing you have about thirty seconds before it goes from lacquer to charcoal.","leftovers":["(easy) Pull cold leftover chicken off the skewers and stir it through hot rice with a spoonful of the tare and a beaten egg — a two-minute donburi that tastes like it took effort.","(easy) The tare is the leftover that matters. It glazes salmon, pork belly and aubergine, and it is the base of a very good marinade for a braai.","(creative) Chop leftover skewers small, fold them into a cabbage-heavy okonomiyaki batter, and use the tare in place of the usual sauce."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Thigh & Spring Onion, Grill Pan (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":27,"howThisFeels":"Kitchen window open, extractor on full, and the smell going through the whole house anyway.","trivia":"Being honest about what is lost: without coals there is no radiant infrared heat and no smoke, so this will taste of grilled chicken and tare rather than of fire. What you can recover is the char, and the way to get it is a ridged cast-iron pan taken hotter than feels sensible and left completely alone between turns. Do not crowd it — three skewers at a time, not six — because a crowded pan drops in temperature and the chicken poaches in its own juices instead of searing. The oven grill is the second-best option, on the top shelf, with the door slightly ajar so the element stays on continuously rather than cycling off.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"15g chicken wing tips or bones"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the tare without the bones — reduce the soy, mirin, sake and sugar for fifteen minutes instead of thirty. It will be simpler and slightly sweeter, so pull it a little short. Get a ridged cast-iron pan properly hot over a high heat, wipe it with the oil, and lay the skewers on with plenty of space between them. Leave them undisturbed for two minutes a side for four turns; every time you move them early you lose the crust. Brush the tare on in the last ninety seconds only, in the same three thin coats, and expect a certain amount of smoke and honest sticking — that caramelised residue in the pan is worth deglazing with a splash of water and pouring back over."}]}},{"name":"Classic Negima over Coals","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":40,"howThisFeels":"Standing over the coals turning skewers while everyone else is inside, which is the correct place to be.","trivia":"Negima is written with the characters for spring onion and for the gaps between things, and the second half of the name is a small joke that has outlived its own origin: the original negima was spring onion and tuna, ma being short for maguro, and chicken replaced the fish somewhere in the early twentieth century when tuna got expensive and chicken got cheap. The name did not change. A dish named after an ingredient it no longer contains is more common than it should be.","delta":{}},{"name":"The Yakitori-ya Spread — Skin, Liver & Tsukune","icon":"🍢","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":58,"howThisFeels":"Six different skewers arriving one at a time, each one a different texture, and realising nobody at the table has spoken for ten minutes.","trivia":"A yakitori-ya buys whole birds and puts every part of them on a stick, and the menu is essentially a map of a chicken. Kawa is the skin alone, wound tightly onto the skewer and cooked long and slow until it renders down to something between crackling and toffee — it takes fifteen minutes rather than eight and it cannot be rushed. Reba is liver, grilled just to pink inside and no further. Tsukune is a minced patty, and it is the one item on the list that exists to use up everything the other skewers could not: trim, cartilage, off-cuts. The raw-egg-yolk dip that comes with it is a Japanese convention that assumes a national egg supply graded for raw eating, so treat it as optional here. Nothing on the bird is thrown away, and the whole-bird economy is exactly why these shops can charge what they do for thigh and still make sense.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"60g chicken skin, in strips"},{"item":"60g chicken livers, trimmed"},{"item":"80g chicken mince with 5g grated ginger, for tsukune"},{"item":"10g shiso or perilla leaves"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Wind the skin strips tightly onto their own skewer, concertina fashion, and give them fifteen minutes at the cooler end of the coals rather than eight at the hot end — skin needs its fat rendered out slowly and then crisped, and hurrying it gives you something rubbery that will not improve. Skewer the livers whole, salt them, and cook them fast and briefly: four minutes, turning often, and off while the centre is still just pink, because a fully cooked liver goes chalky and there is no coming back. For the tsukune, work the mince with the ginger and a pinch of salt in one direction until it goes tacky and holds together, then mould it around the skewers in a long oval and grill it eight minutes, brushing with tare for the last two. Serve the liver shio, the skin shio, and the tsukune tare — the seasoning follows the cut, as always."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Gyoza","target":"japan-gyoza","emoji":"🥟"},{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"},{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"}]},
{"id":"japan-tempura","name":"Tempura","nameAlt":"Prawn and vegetable tempura with tentsuyu","aliases":["Tempura","Tempura Prawns","Ebi Tempura","Japanese Battered Prawns"],"course":"main","type":["main","starter"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","special","family"],"ingredients":"4 large prawns, peeled with the tails left on · 60g sweet potato, in 5mm rounds · 40g aubergine · 30g green beans · 20g shiitake mushrooms · 60g cake flour · 15g cornflour · 1 egg yolk · 150ml iced water · 500ml sunflower oil · 100ml dashi · 25ml mirin · 25ml Japanese soy sauce · 30g daikon, finely grated · 3g fresh ginger, grated · 2g fine sea salt","method":"Tempura is not a batter recipe, it is a campaign against gluten, and once you understand that everything odd about the method becomes obvious. Flour plus water plus agitation makes gluten; gluten makes bread; bread is the exact opposite of what you are trying to produce. So every instruction below exists to stop the flour from ever properly becoming a batter. Prepare absolutely everything first, because from the moment the batter is mixed you have about ten minutes and no time to be looking for the sieve. Make the tentsuyu: warm the dashi, mirin and soy together just to a simmer, then take it off and let it cool to warm. Grate the daikon finely and squeeze it lightly — it goes into the dipping bowl at the table, and its job is enzymatic as much as it is flavour, cutting the richness of fried oil in a way that lemon does not. Grate the ginger. Now the ingredients, and the rule is that they must be BONE DRY and cut so they cook in the time the coating takes. Pat the prawns completely dry with kitchen paper. Water repels hot oil violently, so a wet prawn spits dangerously and, worse, the batter slides straight off it in the pan. Prawns curl into a tight comma as they cook because the connective tissue on the belly contracts faster than the muscle — the same problem the pork cutlet has, solved the same way, with three or four small snips across the belly and a firm press to straighten; that is a borrowed law rather than a new one, and it takes ten seconds. Slice the sweet potato into 5mm rounds — thicker and the inside will still be raw when the coating is dark. Cut the aubergine into a fan: lengthways slices leaving 1cm attached at the stem end, then press it open. Trim the beans and halve the shiitake. Dust everything very lightly with a little of the flour and tap most of it off, which gives the batter something to grip. Heat the oil in a deep, heavy pan to 180°C, filling it no more than a third full. If you have no thermometer, drop a little batter in once it is mixed: it should sink about halfway, hesitate, and rise back within two or three seconds. Straight to the bottom and staying there means too cool; sizzling furiously on the surface without sinking at all means too hot, and too hot gives you a dark coating around a raw centre. Only now, with the oil already at temperature, do you mix the batter. Sift the cake flour and cornflour together and put the bowl in the freezer for ten minutes if you have the time — COLD IS THE FIRST DEFENCE, because gluten forms more slowly at low temperature. Beat the egg yolk into the iced water in a separate bowl, then tip the cold flour in all at once and stir it with CHOPSTICKS, not a whisk, using ten to fifteen strokes and then stopping dead. THE BATTER MUST STILL LOOK WRONG. There should be visible lumps, and dry pockets of unmixed flour on the surface, and it should be thin enough that you can see the colour of a prawn through it once dipped. Every stroke past fifteen is developing gluten you cannot undo, and a smooth, glossy, well-mixed tempura batter fries into something heavy and bready that goes soft in three minutes. If it looks like pancake batter you have already lost. Fry in small batches, never covering more than a third of the oil's surface, because crowding drops the temperature and the coating absorbs oil instead of sealing against it. Dip a piece, let the excess run off for a second, and lay it into the oil away from you. Vegetables want a slightly cooler oil, around 165 to 170°C, because they need time to actually cook through; prawns and seafood want it hotter, 180 to 190°C, because they need speed. Sweet potato takes about three minutes, aubergine and mushroom two, beans ninety seconds, prawns ninety seconds to two minutes and not a second more. Here is the flourish worth learning: as each piece cooks, dip your fingertips in the batter and FLICK a few droplets over it in the oil. They set instantly onto the surface as spiky, brittle little flowers — the Japanese phrase is to make the flowers bloom — and that ragged, uneven surface is what gives good tempura its shattering texture and its silhouette. Skim the loose bits between batches or they burn and darken the oil. Lift each piece out and stand it on a rack, never on paper, and never stacked; a rack lets steam escape from underneath, and a piece lying flat on kitchen paper steams its own base soft within a minute. Salt it immediately, while it is hot enough for the salt to stick. And then serve it AT ONCE. This is a dish timed to the person eating rather than to the kitchen, which is precisely why a tempura counter cooks one piece at a time and hands it straight over, and why a large platter arriving all together has already begun to fail. Tentsuyu in a small bowl with the grated daikon and ginger stirred in; or simply salt, which for good prawns and sweet potato is arguably better.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~590–680 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 28g, Carbs 52g, Fat 34g, Sodium ~1050mg","storage":"Do not keep it. Tempura is a texture with a lifespan measured in minutes, and refrigerated tempura is a soft, oily thing that no oven fully rescues — the honest advice is to fry only what will be eaten. If you do have leftovers, do not reheat them as tempura: put them straight into the tendon or the noodle-bowl treatments below, where softening is part of the design rather than a fault. The frying oil, cooled and strained through a coffee filter, is good for two or three more sessions; keep it in a dark bottle and discard it once it darkens or starts to smell fishy. The tentsuyu keeps a week in the fridge.","chefNotes":"Everything bone dry and prepped before the batter exists. Oil to temperature first, batter second. Iced water, cold flour, chopsticks, fifteen strokes, and stop while it still looks lumpy and wrong. Never crowd the pan. Flick droplets on to make the flowers. Drain standing on a rack, salt at once, serve immediately.","pairsWith":"A bowl of plain rice, a small bowl of miso soup and something pickled — the standard set, and it wants nothing more elaborate. Cold beer or dry sake.","trivia":"Japan's most formal fried food began as street food, and it began there for a fire regulation. Edo in the seventeenth and eighteenth centuries was a city built almost entirely of wood and paper, and it burned down with grim regularity; deep-frying over an open flame indoors was a genuine hazard and was pushed out of buildings and onto the street. So tempura was sold from mobile stalls, on skewers, eaten standing up, dipped into a communal pot of sauce that everybody at the stall shared — closer to a boerewors roll on a street corner than to anything with a tablecloth. That it is now among the most rarefied cuisines in Japan, with counters where a chef fries one piece at a time in front of you and an apprenticeship runs a decade, is one of the more complete reversals in food. The name itself came from further away still: Portuguese missionaries at Nagasaki in the sixteenth century, and most likely the Latin quatuor tempora, the ember days on which meat was forbidden and battered fried fish was what you ate instead. A religious restriction became a fast-food snack became a formal art, which is a long journey for a piece of prawn.","howThisFeels":"The sound when it is right — not a crunch but a shatter, like something thin breaking — and the flick of the fingertips that makes those spiky little flowers bloom in the oil.","leftovers":["(easy) Tendon: put yesterday's tempura on hot rice and pour over a sauce of 2 parts each dashi and mirin to 1 part soy, simmered five minutes. Softening tempura is the entire point of the dish, so leftovers are the correct ingredient.","(easy) Drop cold tempura into a bowl of hot udon or soba broth for thirty seconds. Tempura udon is a menu item precisely because a batter that has gone soft in broth is a good thing.","(creative) The loose fried batter bits skimmed from the oil are tenkasu and they are worth keeping in a jar — scattered over rice, noodles or okonomiyaki they add crunch and a little fried richness for nothing."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Vegetable Tempura (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":23,"howThisFeels":"A pile of ordinary vegetables turned briefly extraordinary, and gone within five minutes of hitting the plate.","trivia":"Vegetable tempura is not the compromise it sounds like — pumpkin, sweet potato, aubergine, green pepper, onion, mushroom and shiso are the traditional set and several of them are better than prawn. The two adjustments that matter are temperature and thickness: vegetables need to cook through rather than merely heat through, so the oil comes down to about 165°C and everything is cut thin. The egg yolk comes out and cold sparkling water goes in, which does most of the same job — the bubbles keep the batter light and the yolk's contribution was mostly colour. Use the kombu-and-shiitake dashi for the tentsuyu and it is vegan throughout.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"4 large prawns, peeled with the tails left on"},{"item":"1 egg yolk"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g pumpkin, in 4mm slices"},{"item":"40g onion, in thick rings"},{"item":"30g green pepper, in wide strips"},{"item":"150ml iced sparkling water"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the batter with iced sparkling water and no egg yolk, mixed the same way and just as badly — fifteen strokes with chopsticks, lumps left visible. The bubbles do what the yolk was doing for lightness. Cut everything thinner than you would for prawn tempura, 4 to 5mm, and drop the oil to 165°C so the inside cooks before the coating darkens. Pumpkin and sweet potato take three to four minutes, onion rings and peppers two, mushrooms and shiso leaves under a minute. Use the kombu-and-shiitake dashi in the tentsuyu."}]}},{"name":"Classic Prawn & Vegetable","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":52,"howThisFeels":"That first prawn, straight from the rack, still too hot to hold properly and eaten anyway.","trivia":"Cake flour rather than plain is a real difference and not a fussy one: it has a lower protein content, which means less gluten available to form in the first place, so the batter is more forgiving of the extra stir you will inevitably give it. The cornflour has no gluten at all and dilutes the flour further. If you only have plain flour, replace a fifth of it with cornflour and be even stricter about the stirring. Sesame oil blended into the frying oil at about one part in five is what several old Tokyo tempura houses use, and it changes the smell of the whole dish.","delta":{}},{"name":"Kakiage & Cold Soba","icon":"🍜","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":46,"howThisFeels":"Breaking a shaggy golden disc apart with chopsticks over a tray of cold noodles on a hot afternoon.","trivia":"Kakiage is the one tempura that is an assembly rather than a single item: everything shredded fine, bound in a minimum of batter and fried as one shaggy fritter. It is where a Japanese kitchen puts the odds and ends — carrot ends, half an onion, a few small prawns — and it is deliberately loose and full of holes, because a densely packed kakiage fries into a solid puck. Served with cold soba on a bamboo tray it becomes ten-zaru, which is summer food: the noodles are dipped into a strong cold broth rather than sitting in it, and the last of the broth is traditionally topped up with the starchy water the soba was cooked in and drunk from the cup.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"120g dried soba noodles"},{"item":"50g carrot, in matchsticks"},{"item":"50g onion, thinly sliced"},{"item":"10g spring onion, sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Toss the shredded carrot, onion, spring onion and a few chopped prawns with a tablespoon of the dry flour until everything is lightly coated, then add only just enough batter to make it cling — far less than feels right. Slide a spoonful onto a flat perforated ladle or a square of baking paper and lower it into 170°C oil, then slip it off; it will hold its shape after twenty seconds. Do not press it down. Poke a few holes through it with a chopstick as it fries so the oil gets into the middle, and turn it once. Three to four minutes. Meanwhile boil the soba in plenty of unsalted water for the time on the packet, then rinse it under cold running water, rubbing the strands with your hands to wash off the surface starch, and drain it well. Make the dipping broth stronger than the tentsuyu — 3 parts dashi to 1 each of mirin and soy — and serve it cold in a cup beside the noodles, with the kakiage on the tray."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🧂"},{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🍖"},{"name":"Chawanmushi","target":"japan-chawanmushi","emoji":"🥚"}]},
{"id":"japan-dorayaki","name":"Dorayaki","nameAlt":"Honey pancakes filled with sweet red bean paste","aliases":["Dorayaki","Red Bean Pancakes","Japanese Pancake Sandwich","Anko Pancakes"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","snack","baking"],"diet":["vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","party"],"ingredients":"80g dried azuki beans · 70g caster sugar, for the anko · 1g fine salt · 1 egg · 30g caster sugar · 12g honey · 5ml mirin · 45g cake flour · 1.5g bicarbonate of soda · 12ml water · 3ml sunflower oil","method":"Two things happen here and they are almost unrelated: a bean paste that takes two hours and rewards patience, and a pancake that takes four minutes and rewards restraint. Make the anko first, ideally the day before, because it is better cold and better still on its second day. Rinse the azuki beans and put them in a pan with plenty of cold water. Bring them to the boil, let them roll for three minutes, and then POUR THE WHOLE LOT AWAY down the sink. This is called shibukiri, cutting the astringency, and it is not squeamishness — azuki skins carry tannins and saponins that make a paste taste faintly harsh and drying, and that first water takes most of them out. Some cooks do it twice. Cover the drained beans with fresh cold water by about 4cm, bring them back to a simmer, and cook them uncovered for sixty to ninety minutes, topping up with hot water whenever they threaten to emerge — they must stay submerged the whole time or the exposed ones toughen and stay hard for good. Test by pressing a bean between finger and thumb: it must crush to a paste with almost no pressure, with no chalky core whatsoever. Now, and ONLY NOW, does the sugar go in. THIS IS THE LAW OF THE WHOLE PASTE: SUGAR ADDED TO BEANS THAT ARE NOT YET FULLY SOFT WILL KEEP THEM HARD FOREVER. Sugar in the surrounding water draws water back out of the beans by osmosis and firms their skins, and once that has happened another hour of boiling will not undo it. Every gritty, disappointing homemade anko is this mistake. So when the beans crush easily, drain off most of the water, keeping a cupful, add the sugar in two or three additions with the salt, and cook it down over a medium heat, stirring across the bottom of the pan, for fifteen to twenty minutes. Stop while it is still LOOSER THAN YOU WANT IT — it thickens substantially as it cools, and a paste reduced to the right consistency while hot sets into something you could build with. For a chunky tsubuan, mash it roughly with the back of the spoon and leave it there. For a smooth koshian, push it through a sieve, which is genuinely tedious and genuinely better. Cool it completely. The pancakes are a different discipline. Whisk the egg, the 30g of sugar, the honey and the mirin together until pale and slightly thickened — a minute of proper whisking, no more. The honey and mirin are not there to sweeten it. They are hygroscopic, meaning they hold onto water, and they are the entire reason a dorayaki is still soft three days later where a plain pancake is stale within the hour; the mirin also helps it brown evenly. Sift in the cake flour and the bicarbonate of soda and fold it through until it is just combined. Then REST THE BATTER, covered, in the fridge for at least thirty minutes and up to a couple of hours. The rest lets the flour hydrate completely and gives the bicarbonate time to start working, and a rested batter spreads into a smooth round on its own where an unrested one sits in a lumpy blob. It will have thickened while it rested, so stir the 12ml of water through it now to bring it back to the consistency of thick cream — it should fall off the spoon in a ribbon that sinks back within about three seconds. Heat a non-stick pan over a MEDIUM-LOW heat and give it a few minutes to reach an even temperature; a pan that is hot in the middle gives you a ring. Wipe it with a little oil on kitchen paper and then WIPE IT ALMOST DRY. Visible oil in the pan fries the surface unevenly and gives a blotchy, mottled face, and a dorayaki's face is the whole point of it. Pour the batter from a spoon, from one fixed point and one fixed height, and DO NOT SPREAD IT — let it find its own circle, about 8cm. Spreading it with the back of a spoon gives a thin ragged edge that cooks before the middle does. Now watch it, and flip when the bubbles across the surface have broken and are STAYING OPEN rather than filling back in, which is the moment the top has set enough to hold. That is usually two to three minutes. The first side is the face and should come off an even, unbroken tan-brown with no pale centre and no dark ring. The second side needs only forty seconds or so; it stays pale, and it is the inside. Cook them all, and as they come out of the pan, stack them and cover them with a cloth. Assemble while they are still warm. Pair pale side to pale side, put a generous mound of anko on one, doming it high in the centre and leaving a clear 1cm margin all round, and press the second one gently on top so the paste spreads to the edge without escaping. Then WRAP EACH ONE IN CLING FILM WHILE IT IS STILL WARM and leave it for at least half an hour. The trapped steam softens the crumb and marries the two halves, and it is the difference between two pancakes with jam in them and a dorayaki.","cookTime":"Prep 20 min · Cook 1 hr 45 min (anko) + 15 min","kcal":"~420–480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 11g, Carbs 84g, Fat 5g, Sodium ~380mg","storage":"Wrapped individually in cling film, three days at room temperature and still soft on the third — that is the honey and the mirin doing their job, and it is why dorayaki became a shop cake rather than a home one. Do not refrigerate them: the fridge stales starch faster than a cupboard does and they go firm and dry. They freeze very well wrapped, for two months, and thaw at room temperature in about an hour. The anko keeps a week in the fridge in a sealed jar and freezes for three months; make far more than you need, because it is the base of half of Japanese confectionery and it is the part that takes the time.","chefNotes":"Boil the beans three minutes and throw the first water away. Sugar goes in only when a bean crushes to nothing between your fingers — early sugar keeps them hard for good. Stop the paste looser than you want it. Rest the batter thirty minutes. Wipe the pan almost dry. Pour, never spread. Flip when the bubbles stay open. Wrap them warm.","pairsWith":"Green tea, and specifically a plain sencha or a roasted hojicha rather than anything sweet or milky — the bitterness is doing structural work against the sugar.","trivia":"Sweet beans strike an unfamiliar palate as a strange idea, and the reason they exist is a genuinely interesting absence: Japan built its entire confectionery tradition with almost no dairy. No butter, no cream, no milk to speak of, for most of its history. In European sweets, fat is what carries sweetness and gives body — cream, butter, custard, ganache. Japanese wagashi had to solve the same problem with what was available, and the answer was azuki: a bean that cooks down to a smooth, starchy, faintly protein-rich paste that coats the mouth the way a fat does without being one. Sweet red bean is not an eccentric ingredient dropped into a Western dessert; it is a different and entirely coherent answer to the question of what makes a sweet thing satisfying. The beans carry weight beyond the kitchen too — red is a colour that wards off misfortune, which is why azuki turn up at births, weddings and New Year, cooked with rice into the celebratory sekihan. And there is a national split on texture that people hold real opinions about: tsubuan, left chunky with the skins in, against koshian, pushed through a sieve until it is completely smooth. Shops state which they use.","howThisFeels":"Peeling the cling film off one that has been sitting for an hour, and finding it has gone from two pancakes and a filling into a single soft thing.","leftovers":["(easy) Leftover anko on hot buttered toast is a Nagoya breakfast institution and it is exactly as good as it sounds.","(easy) Stir a spoonful of anko into a mug of hot milk, or into plain yoghurt, for something between a pudding and a drink.","(creative) Any pancakes that came out badly shaped are still perfectly good: tear them into a bowl, warm a spoonful of anko with a splash of water to loosen it into a sauce, and pour it over with a scoop of vanilla ice cream."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Quick Anko from Tinned Beans (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":8,"howThisFeels":"Twenty minutes from deciding you want something sweet to actually eating it.","trivia":"Tinned red kidney beans are not azuki and the paste will be a shade earthier and less delicate, but the method collapses from two hours to fifteen minutes and the pancakes are unchanged. The one thing that cannot be skipped is the rinsing: tinned beans sit in a salty, starchy liquid that will taste of tin in a sweet paste, so rinse them under running water until the water runs completely clear. Since the beans are already fully soft, the sugar law does not apply here — there is nothing left to toughen — which is the single genuine advantage of the shortcut.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"80g dried azuki beans"}],"addIng":[{"item":"240g tinned red kidney or adzuki beans, drained"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Rinse the tinned beans thoroughly under cold running water until it runs completely clear, then drain them well. Put them in a pan with the sugar, the salt and 80ml of water and cook over a medium heat for twelve to fifteen minutes, mashing with the back of a wooden spoon as you go, until it is a thick paste that holds a line when you drag the spoon across the bottom of the pan. Stop it loose, cool it completely, and it is ready. Everything from the pancake batter onward is unchanged."}]}},{"name":"Classic Dorayaki","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":13,"howThisFeels":"That first even, unblemished brown face lifting off the pan — and immediately wanting to make the rest better than that one.","trivia":"Cake flour matters here for the same reason it matters in tempura, from the other direction: lower protein means a softer, more tender crumb, and a dorayaki made with strong bread flour is noticeably chewy. If you only have plain flour, replace 10g of the 45g with cornflour. The bicarbonate of soda rather than baking powder is deliberate too — it needs an acid to react with, which the honey provides, and it also nudges the batter alkaline, which speeds browning and gives that even mahogany face that is the whole aesthetic of the thing.","delta":{}},{"name":"Matcha & Cream (Nama Dorayaki)","icon":"🍵","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"Cold cream against a warm pancake, and the small bitterness of the matcha arriving a second after the sweetness.","trivia":"Nama dorayaki is the modern bakery version — nama meaning fresh or raw, used in Japanese food names to mean something perishable that has to be eaten today. Whipped cream is layered with the anko rather than replacing it, and the two together do exactly what the tradition originally worked around: it is a dairy solution and a bean solution to the same problem, stacked. The matcha in the batter is not decorative either, since its tannic bitterness is the only thing standing between this version and being simply very sweet. Sift the matcha with the flour or it will refuse to disperse and leave green specks.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"100ml cream, whipped to firm peaks"},{"item":"4g matcha powder"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Sift the matcha in with the cake flour and the bicarbonate — added to the wet mixture it clumps and refuses to disperse, leaving bitter green specks. Whip the cream to firm peaks, firmer than you would for a dessert, because a soft cream will squeeze straight out of the sides under the weight of the top pancake. Let the pancakes cool completely before assembling this version, which is the one exception to assembling warm; warm pancake melts cream. Spread anko on the bottom pancake, pipe or spoon the cream in a dome on top of it, and press the second pancake on gently. It cannot be wrapped and kept, and it needs eating within a couple of hours."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Chawanmushi","target":"japan-chawanmushi","emoji":"🥚"},{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"},{"name":"Tempura","target":"japan-tempura","emoji":"🍤"}]},
{"id":"japan-onigiri","name":"Onigiri","nameAlt":"Omusubi — the rice ball you hold in your hand","aliases":["Onigiri","Omusubi","Japanese Rice Ball","Rice Triangle"],"course":"side","type":["side","snack","lunchbox","portable"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","lunchbox","picnic","quick"],"ingredients":"90g Japanese short-grain rice (sushi rice), raw · 135ml cold water · 70g salmon fillet, skin on · 3g coarse salt · 3g fine salt · 1 sheet nori, cut in half · 5g toasted sesame seeds","method":"This is the oldest portable food in Japan and it is made entirely with your hands, which means the technique lives in your palms rather than in any equipment, and there are exactly four things to get right. Start with the rice, because nothing later can rescue it. It must be Japanese short-grain — sushi rice on the SA shelf — and no long-grain substitute will work here, not because of snobbery but because of chemistry: short-grain rice is high in amylopectin, the branched starch that goes sticky, and long-grain is high in amylose, which stays separate. A ball of long-grain rice is not a firmer onigiri, it is a pile of rice on the floor. Rinse the raw rice in a bowl of cold water, swirling with your fingers, and pour the cloudy water off. Do this four or five times until the water runs almost clear. You are washing off loose surface starch, which would otherwise turn to glue on the outside of the grains while the inside stays hard. Then let the drained rice sit for twenty minutes before it meets its cooking water — dry grains crack when they hit water fast, and a rested grain absorbs evenly all the way to the centre. Cook it with 135ml water in a small pot with a tight lid: bring it to the boil, drop it to the lowest heat for 12 minutes, then take it off and LEAVE THE LID ON for a further 10 minutes. That off-heat rest is not optional and it is not patience for its own sake — the residual steam finishes the top layer of grains, which are always slightly behind the bottom ones, and a pot opened early gives you two different rices in one bowl. While it rests, cook the salmon. Salt the fillet on both sides with the coarse salt and leave it 15 minutes, then pat it dry — the salt pulls water out of the surface, firms the flesh and seasons it right through rather than just on top. Grill or pan-fry it, skin down first, over a medium heat for about 4 minutes, turn, and give it 2 more, until it flakes when you press it with a fork. Let it cool, then pull it into coarse flakes with your fingers, dropping any bones and skin as you go. Now the shaping, and here is where the whole dish is won or lost. THE RICE MUST BE WARM, NOT HOT AND NOT COLD. Hot rice will burn your palms and, worse, it steams inside the ball and turns to paste. Cold rice has already retrograded — the starch has recrystallised, the grains have stopped being tacky and they will simply refuse to bond, which is why the ball you made from last night's fridge rice fell apart on the way to work. Fan it or spread it on a plate until it is comfortably warm to the hand, around body temperature. Wet both hands under the tap and shake them almost dry, then take the fine salt and rub it across both palms. THE SALT GOES ON YOUR HANDS, NEVER IN THE RICE. This is tenoshio, and it does two jobs: it seasons the outside surface, which is the part your mouth actually meets first, so a small amount tastes like a lot; and it puts salt exactly where an unrefrigerated rice ball needs it, on the outside, which is why this dish travelled for centuries in a cloth without making anyone ill. Salt stirred through the rice tastes flat and does neither job. Take half the warm rice into one cupped palm, press a hollow into the middle with your thumb, put half the salmon flakes into the hollow, and draw the rice up over the filling so it is completely buried. A filling that shows on the surface will tear the nori and weep into it. Then shape it: cup one hand into a V, lay the other flat on top, and turn the ball a quarter-turn between three or four gentle presses, firming the corners and leaving the middle alone. PRESS ONLY ENOUGH TO HOLD. This is the counter-intuitive law of onigiri — a ball that holds together perfectly is too tight. You are aiming for something that survives being carried in one hand and collapses the moment it is bitten, which means the outside is compacted and the inside is still loose grains with air between them. Three or four presses, not ten. Repeat with the second one. Roll the finished balls in the sesame seeds if you like, and wrap each in half a sheet of nori — but wrap it at the moment you are going to eat it, never before. Nori is dry and crisp, warm rice is wet, and the two start trading moisture on contact: within twenty minutes the seaweed has gone limp and slightly slimy and you have lost the contrast the whole thing is built on. If these are going into a lunchbox, carry the nori separately in a fold of paper and wrap them at the table.","cookTime":"Rest rice 20 min · Cook 12 min · Steam 10 min · Shape 5 min","kcal":"~390 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 20g, Carbs 68g, Fat 6g, Sodium ~900mg","storage":"Best within a few hours of shaping, at room temperature, wrapped in cling film or greaseproof paper. Do NOT refrigerate them — the cold retrogrades the starch, the grains go hard and chalky and no amount of warming brings the texture back. If you must keep them overnight, freeze them instead, wrapped tightly and un-nori'd, for up to a month, and steam or microwave straight from frozen. Salmon and other cooked fillings should be treated as you would treat cooked fish generally: made and eaten the same day is the safe habit.","chefNotes":"Warm rice, wet salted hands, three or four gentle turns and no more, filling completely buried, nori on at the last second. If the ball crumbles the rice was too cold; if it is dense and gluey you pressed it too many times.","pairsWith":"A bowl of miso, a piece of tamagoyaki and something green and vinegared is the whole Japanese lunchbox in three moves. Also excellent alongside grilled skewers or a bowl of clear soup.","trivia":"This is very probably the oldest surviving prepared dish in Japan. Archaeologists working a Yayoi-period site in Ishikawa Prefecture found lumps of carbonised rice that had been deliberately shaped and then cooked — not spilled from a pot, but formed by hand roughly two thousand years ago, and the shape has essentially not changed since. What has changed is the packaging, and that change is a small marvel of engineering. Onigiri was a homemade thing until the late 1970s, because a convenience store could not sell one: wrap it in nori and the seaweed was ruined by the time it reached the shelf; sell it without nori and nobody wanted it. The solution, patented by a Japanese inventor in 1978 and taken up by the convenience-store chains, was a three-layer film that keeps the nori physically separated from the rice by a sheet of plastic until you pull a tab, at which point the wrapper splits and pulls itself out from between the two, delivering dry crisp seaweed onto warm rice in about a second and a half. It is the reason onigiri became the single biggest-selling item in Japanese convenience stores, and it is a rare case of a packaging invention creating an entire retail category rather than just serving one. The traditional filling tells you what the food was for. Umeboshi — a salt-pickled plum, ferociously sour and salty — was the standard for travellers and soldiers not because anyone especially loved it but because it is genuinely antibacterial and strongly preservative, and a rice ball with a plum in the middle kept longer in a cloth on a hot road. The triangle is not decorative either: it stacks flat, it travels in a box without rolling, and the mountain shape carried an older meaning, since shaped rice was offering food long before it was lunch.","howThisFeels":"Warm rice in your cupped palms, the small salt-sting on wet skin, and that first bite where the crisp seaweed gives way and the whole thing falls apart in your mouth exactly as it is supposed to.","leftovers":["(easy) Yesterday's onigiri, hard and unloved, becomes yaki onigiri: brush both flat faces with a thin mix of soy and mirin and grill or dry-fry them until the outside is lacquered and crunchy. Cold rice is actually the better starting point here, because a firm ball holds its shape on the heat where a fresh soft one slumps.","(easy) Break a leftover ball into a bowl, pour hot green tea or dashi over it, and add a pinch of sesame and a torn strip of nori. This is ochazuke, it takes ninety seconds, and it is one of the most comforting things in the entire Japanese repertoire.","(creative) Crumble two cold onigiri into a hot oiled pan with a beaten egg and whatever is in the fridge for a fast Japanese-style fried rice — the grains have dried out and separated overnight, which is precisely the condition fried rice wants and fresh rice never has."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Gomashio & Salted Rice (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":5,"howThisFeels":"The plainest version and somehow the one you keep making — rice, salt, sesame, and the surprise of how much that is.","trivia":"This is the original, and for most of the dish's history it was the only version. Gomashio — toasted sesame ground with salt — is still what a Japanese Buddhist temple kitchen puts on rice, and the ratio is roughly ten parts sesame to one part salt, which stretches a small amount of salt across a whole bowl and makes it taste of more than it is.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"70g salmon fillet, skin on"},{"item":"3g coarse salt"}],"addStep":[{"text":"With no filling, the shaping is the whole dish, so give the salt on your hands a little more attention — go slightly heavier, since the rice has nothing in the middle carrying flavour. Grind the toasted sesame coarsely with a pinch of extra salt in a mortar before rolling the balls in it: whole seeds bounce off the tongue and are largely swallowed intact, whereas a half-crushed seed releases its oil and tastes several times stronger for the same money."}]}},{"name":"Classic Salted Salmon (Shiozake)","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":16},{"name":"Yaki Onigiri — Grilled with Miso","icon":"🔥","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":9,"howThisFeels":"The smell of soy hitting a hot pan and the sound of a rice crust cracking under a fork — it tastes like the last thing eaten at a festival stall.","trivia":"Grilling a rice ball is the oldest way of reviving one, and the crust is a Maillard reaction on a food with almost no protein and no added sugar — the browning is coming from the miso and soy painted on rather than from the rice itself, which is why an unbrushed yaki onigiri toasts but never really tastes of anything.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"70g salmon fillet, skin on"},{"item":"3g coarse salt"},{"item":"1 sheet nori, cut in half"}],"addIng":[{"item":"15g white miso paste"},{"item":"10ml mirin"},{"item":"5ml soy sauce"},{"item":"5ml neutral oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Shape the balls a touch firmer than usual and then let them sit uncovered for ten minutes — a slightly dried surface is what browns, and a wet one just steams and sticks. Mix the miso, mirin and soy into a loose paste. Heat the oil in a non-stick pan over a medium heat and lay the balls in DRY, with nothing on them yet, for about 3 minutes a side until a pale crust forms and they release from the pan by themselves. Only now brush the miso mix onto the top face, flip, and give it 60 to 90 seconds, then brush and flip the other. Miso burns fast and bitterly because of the sugars in it, so it goes on at the end, onto a crust that already exists — painting it on at the start gives you a black ball with raw rice underneath."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Tamagoyaki","target":"japan-tamagoyaki","emoji":"🍳"},{"name":"Goma-ae","target":"japan-goma-ae","emoji":"🥬"},{"name":"Kare Raisu","target":"japan-kare-raisu","emoji":"🍛"}]},
{"id":"japan-tamagoyaki","name":"Tamagoyaki","nameAlt":"Dashimaki Tamago — the rolled egg","aliases":["Tamagoyaki","Dashimaki Tamago","Japanese Rolled Omelette","Atsuyaki Tamago"],"course":"side","type":["side","egg","breakfast","lunchbox"],"diet":["omnivore","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","breakfast","lunchbox","quick"],"ingredients":"3 large eggs · 45ml dashi, cold · 5ml soy sauce · 5ml mirin · 4g caster sugar · 1g fine salt · 8ml neutral oil · 30g daikon, finely grated, to serve","method":"A rolled egg looks like a party trick and is actually a piece of quiet engineering, and every single rule below exists to solve one problem: how to make eight or nine separate thin omelettes weld into one solid block that can be cut into a clean square. Begin by mixing rather than whisking, and understand the difference, because it decides everything. Crack the eggs into a bowl and cut through them with chopsticks or a fork in a back-and-forth dragging motion, keeping the tips on the bottom of the bowl. You are trying to break the yolks and combine them with the whites without lifting air in. A whisked, foamy egg is full of bubbles, bubbles become holes, and holes are gaps where two layers cannot touch each other — the finished roll delaminates into a stack of loose sheets the moment a knife goes through it. Then STRAIN THE EGG through a fine sieve, pushing it through with the back of a spoon. This takes twenty seconds and it is the difference between a professional slice and a homemade one. What you are removing is the chalazae, the two ropy white cords that anchor the yolk in the shell — they are pure structural protein, they never break down at any temperature you will use here, and in a pale yellow cross-section they show up as unmistakable white streaks. Stir in the cold dashi, soy, mirin, sugar and salt. The dashi is where the dish stops being an omelette: at 45ml to three eggs you are adding almost a third of the egg volume back in liquid, and it stays in there because setting egg protein forms a gel that traps water inside its own mesh. Tamagoyaki is closer to a rolled savoury custard than to anything fried, which is why it is soft, faintly wobbly and juicy rather than rubbery, and why the dashi version is the one restaurants are judged on. Use it cold — warm dashi begins to set the egg in the bowl. Now the pan. The proper tool is a makiyakinabe, a rectangular pan whose straight sides do the folding for you, but a small round non-stick frying pan works perfectly well and always has for people without one; you simply tuck the two curved ends in as you roll and trim the block square at the end. Set it over a medium-LOW heat and be honest about it: the pan should hiss faintly when a drop of egg lands, not spit. Egg over high heat browns, and in Japan a browned tamagoyaki is a fault rather than a rustic charm — the whole aesthetic is an even, uninterrupted yellow, and once the pan is hot enough to colour egg it is too hot to control layers. Fold a piece of kitchen paper into a pad, dip it in the oil, and wipe the pan. OIL BETWEEN EVERY SINGLE LAYER, with that same pad — this is not about sticking so much as about release, since each layer has to slide freely while you roll it or it will tear across the middle. Pour in a thin layer of egg, about a fifth of the mixture, and tilt the pan so it runs to every corner. As it sets, pop any bubbles that rise with a chopstick. Watch the surface, because here is the law the whole dish turns on: ROLL IT WHILE THE TOP IS STILL WET. The layer should be set underneath and just barely liquid on top, glossy and tacky rather than dry. A fully cooked surface is a sealed surface, and sealed surfaces do not bond to anything — the roll will look right and then fall into separate sheets under the knife. Starting at the far side, fold the egg over on itself in two or three rolls towards you, ending with the roll sitting nearest to you. Wipe the empty part of the pan with the oil pad again. Pour in the next layer, and now do the thing that makes it one object rather than a spiral: LIFT THE COOKED ROLL WITH YOUR CHOPSTICKS AND LET THE RAW EGG RUN UNDERNEATH IT. That thin film of wet egg trapped between the roll and the new layer is the weld. Let it set until it is again wet on top, then roll the whole bundle back towards the far side, over the new layer, so the roll grows by one skin. Wipe with oil, pour, lift, let it run under, set, roll. Repeat until the mixture is finished, and expect five or six layers from this quantity. Tip the finished roll out onto a bamboo sushi mat or a doubled sheet of foil, roll it up snugly and let it sit for five minutes — while it is warm it will take a shape and keep it, squaring off the sides and pressing out the last air. Unwrap, and cut into thick slices with a sharp knife, wiping the blade between cuts. Serve warm or at room temperature with a small mound of grated daikon beside it, squeezed gently of its liquid, with a few drops of soy on the daikon rather than on the egg. The radish is not garnish — its bite cuts the richness and its enzymes make the whole thing sit lighter.","cookTime":"Mix and strain 5 min · Roll 12 min · Shape 5 min","kcal":"~250 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 19g, Carbs 6g, Fat 17g, Sodium ~700mg","storage":"Keeps 2 days in the fridge, wrapped, and is genuinely good cold — this is a lunchbox staple precisely because it does not need reheating. Slice it only when you are ready to eat, as a whole block dries far more slowly than cut faces do. It does not freeze well: the trapped dashi turns to ice crystals, tears the protein gel and the thawed egg weeps water and goes spongy.","chefNotes":"Mix, never whisk. Strain. Cold dashi. Medium-low heat, oil between every layer, and roll while the top is still wet — that tacky surface is the glue. Lift the roll and let each new pour run underneath it.","pairsWith":"The other half of a Japanese breakfast or lunchbox: rice or onigiri, something green and sesame-dressed, and a bowl of miso. Also a standard piece on a sushi counter and a good foil for anything grilled and salty.","trivia":"At a traditional Edo-style sushi counter, the rolled egg is the item the whole shop is judged on, and there is a hard logic to it. Every other thing on that counter depends on the fish market — a chef with a good supplier and a fair knife can serve superb tuna without telling you much about himself. The egg tells you everything, because it is the only item made entirely in-house from cheap, universally available ingredients, which means nothing about it can be bought. It is pure technique, and connoisseurs traditionally order it first for exactly that reason, reading the shop from a slice of egg before committing to the rest of the meal. Apprentices in the old system could spend years on it before being allowed near a fish. The regional split is visible in the pans themselves: Tokyo cooks work in a square pan and lean sweeter and firmer, a style descended from a time when sugar was expensive enough that sweetness in a savoury dish was a statement of wealth, while Osaka and Kyoto cooks use a long rectangular pan and push the dashi ratio as high as the egg will physically hold, making something so soft it barely survives being lifted. The Kansai version is technically the harder one, since more liquid means a weaker gel and a roll far more willing to tear, and it is also the version that gave the dish its other name — dashimaki, literally 'dashi rolled up', which is an unusually honest description of what is going on inside it.","howThisFeels":"The faint hiss as each thin pour hits the oiled pan, the small satisfaction of a roll that comes back on itself cleanly, and a warm slice that gives like custard rather than bouncing like an omelette.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold slices go straight into a lunchbox beside rice and need nothing done to them — this is what half of Japan does with it, and the egg is arguably better on day two once the dashi has settled evenly through the layers.","(easy) Cut cold tamagoyaki into fingers and lay them on a rice ball or over a bowl of hot rice with a splash of soy and some spring onion for an instant tamago-don.","(creative) Dice it small and fold it through a cold noodle salad or a potato salad in place of boiled egg — it brings its own seasoning and dashi with it, so the dressing can be much lighter than it would otherwise need to be."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Plain Sweet Tamagoyaki (Budget · Vegetarian)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":7,"howThisFeels":"Sweeter, firmer, and the one that tastes like somebody's mother made it — the version that goes into a schoolchild's box.","trivia":"This is the Kanto style and the household default, and it is easier for a reason that has nothing to do with skill: with no dashi in it, the egg sets into a much stronger gel, so the layers are firmer, more forgiving and far less inclined to tear as you roll them. Anyone learning tamagoyaki should start here and add the dashi once the rolling is automatic.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"45ml dashi, cold"},{"item":"30g daikon, finely grated, to serve"}],"addIng":[{"item":"15ml water"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"4g caster sugar","to":"10g caster sugar"}],"addStep":[{"text":"With the dashi gone the mixture is much thicker, so pour slightly thinner layers than you think you need — a heavy layer sets before you can tilt it into the corners. The extra sugar browns far more readily than plain egg does, so drop the heat another notch: sweet tamagoyaki is the version most often ruined by colour, and a pale golden block is the target every time."}]}},{"name":"Classic Dashimaki Tamago","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":12},{"name":"Layered with Nori & Spring Onion","icon":"🌿","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":10,"howThisFeels":"Cut it and there are dark green spirals running through the yellow — the slice that makes a plain lunchbox look like somebody tried.","trivia":"Laying a sheet of nori into the roll is a home cook's trick rather than a restaurant one, and it does something useful beyond looking good: the dry seaweed absorbs a little of the moisture at each join, which makes the roll noticeably easier to keep together for anyone still finding the technique.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"45ml dashi, cold","to":"35ml dashi, cold"}],"addIng":[{"item":"1 sheet nori, cut into strips the width of the pan"},{"item":"15g spring onion, very finely sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Stir the spring onion through the strained egg. Then, as each layer sets and just before you roll it, lay a strip of nori across the surface nearest you — onto the wet egg, so it sticks immediately and does not shift during the roll. Use slightly less dashi than the classic: the nori and the onion both release water into the mixture as they cook, and at the full dashi ratio the roll becomes genuinely difficult to lift."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🍥"},{"name":"Onigiri","target":"japan-onigiri","emoji":"🍙"},{"name":"Goma-ae","target":"japan-goma-ae","emoji":"🥬"}]},
{"id":"japan-kare-raisu","name":"Kare Raisu","nameAlt":"Japanese Curry Rice — the navy's Friday dinner","aliases":["Kare Raisu","Japanese Curry","Curry Rice","Kare"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","comfort","batch"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","batch","weekend"],"ingredients":"150g beef chuck, cut into 3cm cubes · 120g onion, sliced thinly · 60g carrot · 90g waxy potato · 1 clove garlic, grated · 5g fresh ginger, grated · 30g apple, grated · 20g butter · 20g cake flour · 8g curry powder · 2g garam masala · 350ml beef stock · 10ml neutral oil · 5ml soy sauce · 5g honey · 2g salt · 180g cooked Japanese short-grain rice, to serve","method":"Japanese curry is thick, dark, faintly sweet and barely spicy, and every one of those qualities traces back to the fact that it is a British stew rather than an Indian one — it is thickened with a flour-and-butter roux, which no Indian curry on earth is, and that roux is where the technique lives. Start with the onions, and give them the time, because they are the entire sweetness of the dish and there is no sugar coming later to fake it. Slice 120g of onion as thinly as you can manage and put it into a heavy pot with the oil over a medium-low heat with a good pinch of salt. Now cook them for thirty to forty minutes, stirring every few minutes and scraping the base. They will go translucent, then straw, then a deep glossy amber. The Japanese name for this stage is ame-iro tamanegi, candy-coloured onions, and it is a slow Maillard-and-caramelisation job that simply cannot be hurried: a hot pan gives you browned edges and raw sulphurous middles, which tastes sharp rather than sweet, and every thin curry you have ever made was probably an onion that got twelve minutes instead of thirty-five. Add a splash of water any time they threaten to catch, and use it to lift the brown film off the base back into them. While they go, brown the beef. Pat the cubes dry, season them, and sear them hard in a separate pan in a film of oil until they have real colour on at least two faces, then set them aside. Dry meat and a hot pan — wet meat steams grey and you lose the entire savoury foundation. When the onions are amber, add the garlic and ginger for a minute, then the beef with any resting juices, the stock, the grated apple, the soy and the honey, and bring it to a bare simmer. Skim off the grey foam that rises in the first ten minutes; it is coagulated protein and it will make the sauce cloudy and slightly bitter if you stir it back in. Cover and leave it at a lazy blip for about ninety minutes, until the beef gives up completely to a fork. The apple and honey are kakushi-aji — 'hidden flavour', a genuine Japanese cooking concept meaning an ingredient added specifically so that nobody can identify it. Grated apple, a square of dark chocolate, a spoon of instant coffee and a spoon of honey are the classics, and the test of a kakushi-aji is that a guest tastes depth and cannot name the cause. Meanwhile, make the roux, which is the part everybody buys in a block and which takes twenty minutes to do better. Melt the butter in a small pan over a LOW heat, stir in the flour, and cook it, stirring more or less constantly, for 15 to 20 minutes until it smells of toasted biscuit and has gone the colour of peanut butter. You are cooking the raw floury taste out and partially breaking down the starch, which is why a properly browned roux thickens less per gram but tastes incomparably better. Now TAKE THE PAN OFF THE HEAT COMPLETELY and only then stir in the curry powder and garam masala. Ground spices in hot fat scorch in seconds, and scorched curry powder is acrid in a way that no amount of simmering afterwards will lift out. The residual heat in the pan is more than enough to bloom them. Now cut the vegetables and get the timing right, because this is where a good curry goes to mush. Cut the carrot with a rolling cut — a diagonal slice, then a quarter-turn of the carrot and another diagonal — which gives irregular chunks with far more cut surface than discs and lets them absorb sauce from every angle. Cut the potato into 3cm pieces and use a WAXY potato, not a floury one: a floury potato disintegrates into the sauce, clouds it and turns the whole pot to gluey mash, whereas a waxy one holds its edges and stays a distinct thing on the spoon. Add the carrot to the pot for the last 25 minutes and the potato for the last 15. And now the single law that separates a smooth curry from a lumpy one: THE ROUX NEVER GOES INTO A BOILING POT. Kill the heat under the curry entirely and let it stop moving for two minutes. Ladle about 150ml of the hot liquid into the pan of roux and whisk it smooth off the heat — it will look alarmingly thick, then loosen. Pour that slurry back into the pot, stirring as you go. A cold or stiff roux dropped straight into rapidly boiling liquid gelatinises on its own outside instantly and seals itself into lumps that will never disperse no matter how long you beat them, which is precisely what happens to everyone the first time. Once it is in, return the pot to the lowest possible heat for 10 minutes, stirring occasionally, so the starch fully swells and the sauce goes from cloudy to glossy. It should coat a spoon thickly and fall from it in a sheet, not a drip. Taste for salt. Then, if you possibly can, LET IT SIT OVERNIGHT. This is not folklore. The starch continues to hydrate, the fat emulsifies properly into the sauce as it cools, and the spice compounds — which are oil-soluble — spend twelve hours distributing themselves through that fat instead of sitting in patches. Day-two curry is measurably a different dish, and in Japan it is understood to be the point rather than a consolation. Serve it beside rather than over a mound of short-grain rice, so half the plate stays dry.","cookTime":"Onions 35 min · Simmer 90 min · Roux 20 min · Finish 10 min","kcal":"~780 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 38g, Carbs 88g, Fat 30g, Sodium ~1200mg","storage":"Improves in the fridge for 3 days and is expected to — make it the day before if you can. Reheat gently, adding a splash of water, since it thickens considerably as it chills. It freezes for 3 months, but freeze it WITHOUT the potato: potato cells rupture when frozen and thaw grainy and hollow, so either fish the pieces out first or add fresh ones when you reheat. Cool it to room temperature quickly, in a wide dish rather than a deep pot, before it goes near the fridge.","chefNotes":"Onions for thirty-five minutes, not twelve. Spices into the roux OFF the heat. Waxy potato, added late. And the roux goes into a pot that has stopped boiling, slackened first with a ladle of the hot liquid — never straight in. Make it a day early.","pairsWith":"Fukujinzuke or any sharp red pickle, and a mound of plain short-grain rice. A crumbed cutlet laid on top makes it katsu curry — that build lives on the Tonkatsu card. Something green and vinegared on the side keeps it from feeling heavy.","trivia":"Curry reached Japan from Britain, not from India, and the whole character of the dish follows from that one fact. It arrived in the late nineteenth century as Anglo-Indian naval stew — meat, root vegetables and curry powder, thickened with a flour roux in the standard British manner — which is why Japanese curry is a thick brown gravy rather than a spiced sauce, and why to this day it is filed in Japan under yoshoku, Western food, rather than as anything Asian. The Imperial Japanese Navy took it up in the 1880s, and there was a medical reason as well as a culinary one. Naval crews on a ration of polished white rice were being destroyed by beriberi; the naval doctor Takaki Kanehiro ran what amounts to one of the first controlled dietary trials in history, sending two ships on near-identical voyages with different rations, and demonstrated that a varied diet with barley, meat and vegetables all but eliminated the disease. A curry stew served over barley-cut rice fed an entire crew from one pot, kept well, and did exactly what the new ration required. It became a fixed item of navy cooking and spread from the navy into the whole country. The most charming part survives today: the Japan Maritime Self-Defense Force still serves curry every Friday, and the reason officially given is calendrical rather than culinary — submarine and long-patrol crews lose their grip on which day it is with no daylight and no weekend, so Friday curry is a clock. Individual ships guard their own recipes competitively, and the base towns run public curry competitions where you can eat the actual destroyer recipes ashore.","howThisFeels":"That thirty-minute stretch where the kitchen fills with the smell of onions going sweet, and then the moment the roux hits the pot and the whole thing turns glossy and dark in front of you.","leftovers":["(easy) Day-two curry is the intended state, not a leftover — but the great second act is kare udon: loosen a portion with dashi until it is a thick soup, and pour it over hot udon noodles with plenty of spring onion.","(easy) Spoon cold curry over rice in an oven dish, cover it with cheese and grill it until blistered. This is yaki kare, it is on menus all over Japan, and the flour-thickened sauce is unusually well suited to it because it does not split under a grill.","(creative) Wrap thick cold curry in a disc of bread dough or shortcrust, seal it, coat it in panko and fry it for kare pan — the curry bread sold in every Japanese bakery. Cold and stiff is the only workable state for the filling, so this is a leftovers-only dish by nature."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Vegetable & Chickpea (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":22,"howThisFeels":"Sweeter and rounder than the beef, and the version that proves the onions were always doing the heavy lifting.","trivia":"This is closer to the original navy dish than the beef version is. Meat was the scarce part of the ration and the pot was built to stretch it, so a curry carried mostly by onion, root vegetables and pulses is not a modern compromise — it is what most of the country actually ate for the first fifty years the dish existed.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef chuck, cut into 3cm cubes","to":"120g chickpeas, cooked or tinned and drained"},{"from":"350ml beef stock","to":"350ml vegetable stock"},{"from":"20g butter","to":"20ml neutral oil"},{"from":"5g honey","to":"5g brown sugar"}],"addIng":[{"item":"80g butternut, in 3cm pieces"},{"item":"60g mushrooms, quartered"}],"addStep":[{"text":"With no beef to brown there is no fond in the pot, so build the savoury depth elsewhere: brown the mushrooms hard and dry in the pot first, before the onions go in, until they squeak and colour, and leave them in. Push the onions a further five minutes darker than the classic — they are now carrying the whole base. The simmer drops from 90 minutes to about 25, since nothing here needs breaking down; add the butternut with the carrot and the chickpeas only in the last 10 minutes, because a chickpea that has boiled for an hour goes to powder."}]}},{"name":"Classic Beef Kare Raisu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":42},{"name":"Sapporo Soup Curry","icon":"🍲","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":48,"howThisFeels":"A thin, fierce, fragrant broth with a whole chicken leg and half a vegetable garden standing up out of it — nothing like the brown gravy and completely Japanese anyway.","trivia":"Soup curry was invented in Sapporo in the 1970s and it is a deliberate rejection of everything the roux stands for: no thickener at all, a much heavier hand with whole spices, and the vegetables roasted separately and stood in the bowl rather than stewed into submission. Hokkaido eats it in deep winter, and the local claim is that a thin, very hot, heavily spiced soup warms you more thoroughly than a thick one, which is at least physically plausible.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"20g butter"},{"item":"20g cake flour"},{"item":"90g waxy potato"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"150g beef chuck, cut into 3cm cubes","to":"1 whole chicken leg, about 250g"},{"from":"350ml beef stock","to":"500ml chicken stock"},{"from":"8g curry powder","to":"12g curry powder"}],"addIng":[{"item":"3g cumin seeds"},{"item":"3g coriander seeds"},{"item":"1 dried red chilli"},{"item":"150g mixed vegetables for roasting — potato, carrot, aubergine, green pepper, broccoli"}],"addStep":[{"text":"There is no roux in this one at all, so skip that step entirely and toast the whole cumin and coriander seeds in the dry pot for 40 seconds before the onions go in — with nothing thickening the liquid, the aroma has to carry the bowl. Brown the chicken leg skin-side down until the skin is properly crisp, then simmer it in the stock with the onions and spices for 45 minutes. Roast the vegetables separately in a hot oven with a little oil until they are caramelised at the edges and STILL FIRM — the entire point of the style is that each vegetable arrives as itself, so they must never see the inside of the pot. Ladle the thin broth into a deep bowl, sit the chicken leg in the middle and stand the roasted vegetables around it."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🍖"},{"name":"Onigiri","target":"japan-onigiri","emoji":"🍙"},{"name":"Goma-ae","target":"japan-goma-ae","emoji":"🥬"}]},
{"id":"japan-agedashi-tofu","name":"Agedashi Tofu","nameAlt":"Agedashi Dofu — fried, then given to the broth","aliases":["Agedashi Tofu","Agedashi Dofu","Fried Tofu in Dashi","Japanese Crispy Tofu"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","tofu","fried","small plate"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","entertaining","quick"],"ingredients":"200g silken tofu · 25g potato starch · 400ml neutral oil · 150ml dashi · 20ml soy sauce · 20ml mirin · 40g daikon, finely grated · 5g fresh ginger, finely grated · 10g spring onion, finely sliced · 2g katsuobushi","method":"The name of this dish is an instruction and almost nobody reads it: age means fried and dashi is the broth, and the grammar of it is that the tofu is fried and then GIVEN to the dashi. That is the whole idea, and it explains the one thing that confuses people about agedashi tofu, which is that it arrives crisp and is meant to stop being crisp while you eat it. Understand that and every decision below makes sense. Start by pressing the tofu, and take this seriously, because tofu is around eighty-five percent water and every gram of it that is still in there when the block meets hot oil is a gram trying to escape violently through your coating. Lay the block on a plate lined with a folded tea towel or several sheets of kitchen paper, put another layer on top, and rest something flat and moderately heavy on it — a small plate with a tin on it is right, a cast-iron pan is not. Silken tofu is fragile and you are draining it, not crushing it. Give it 30 minutes and change the paper once when it is soaked. You will draw off a surprising amount of liquid, and the block will have gone from wobbling to holding a shape. Then cut it into six cubes with a wet knife, sliding rather than sawing, and lay them on fresh dry paper. Now make the broth, because it must be ready and hot before anything is fried and there is no time to make it afterwards. Put the dashi, soy and mirin in a small pan — a roughly 8:1:1 ratio, which you can shift to 6:1:1 if you like it saltier — and bring it to a simmer for two minutes. That simmer is doing a real job: mirin carries alcohol, and alcohol left in a warm broth reads as a thin chemical sharpness behind everything else. Two minutes at a simmer drives it off and leaves the sugars behind. Keep it hot. Grate the daikon on the finest side of a box grater and let it sit in a small sieve, then squeeze it very gently — you want it damp, not dry, since the liquid it gives up is part of the dish. Grate the ginger the same way. Heat the oil in a small deep pan to 180°C. If you have no thermometer, a wooden chopstick stood in the oil should throw up a steady stream of fine bubbles from its tip, and a pinch of the starch should sizzle and rise immediately without browning. Temperature matters more here than in most frying: below about 170°C the coating takes too long to set, the tofu sits there absorbing oil and comes out heavy and greasy, and above 190°C the starch colours before the inside of the block has warmed through at all, which gives you a hot shell around a cold centre. Now the coating, and the timing of it is a law. COAT THE TOFU ONLY IN THE MOMENT BEFORE IT GOES INTO THE OIL. Put the potato starch in a shallow dish, roll one cube in it, pat off every loose grain, and put it straight into the oil. Then do the next one. Do not coat all six and line them up on a tray, because starch sitting on damp tofu draws water out of it, and within a few minutes what was a dry powdery jacket has turned into a wet paste that fries gummy and slides off in sheets. This is the single commonest way the dish fails at home and it happens during the tidy, well-organised version of the process. Use potato starch rather than cornflour if you can find it. Katakuriko fries into a thin, brittle, faintly translucent crust that shatters, because its granules are large and swell dramatically; cornflour will work and is a fair stand-in, but it sets denser and slightly chalky and it browns sooner. Plain flour is not a substitute at all here and will give you a soft pale skin. Fry the cubes two or three at a time so the oil temperature holds, turning them gently with chopsticks after about 90 seconds, for around 3 minutes total. And here is the second law: TAKE THEM OUT PALE. You are looking for a crust that is set, faintly golden at the edges and still nearly white across the faces, not a deep brown. A deeply browned agedashi is over-fried, the coating has gone hard and thick, and it will sit in the broth like a shell instead of doing what it is supposed to do. Lift them out and let them drain for a few seconds. Now assemble, and this is the law that the whole dish turns on: THE BROTH GOES INTO THE BOWL FIRST AND THE TOFU IS SET INTO IT. Never pour hot broth over the top of fried tofu. Ladle the hot dashi mixture into a shallow bowl to a depth of about a centimetre, then sit the cubes in it so that they stand with their lower third submerged and their tops dry and above the surface. Every piece then has three textures in it at once — a crisp dry top, a softening middle where the starch is dissolving into the broth and thickening it very slightly, and a bottom that has given up entirely. Pouring the broth over collapses all three into one soggy texture in about ten seconds and there is no way back from it. Pile the grated daikon on top with the ginger beside it and scatter over the spring onion and the bonito flakes, which will curl and move in the steam. Eat it immediately, and eat it top to bottom rather than politely from the side — the crispness is a countdown rather than a state, and it is supposed to be.","cookTime":"Press 30 min · Broth 5 min · Fry 10 min","kcal":"~320 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 16g, Carbs 24g, Fat 18g, Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"This one genuinely does not keep and there is no honest way to pretend otherwise — it is built to lose its texture within minutes, so it is made and eaten. If you must work ahead, press the tofu and make the broth up to 2 days in advance and keep them separately in the fridge, then coat and fry to order; that is how a restaurant does it too. Leftover fried tofu, drained of broth, will keep a day in the fridge and is worth having, but treat it as a soft braising ingredient from that point rather than trying to re-crisp it.","chefNotes":"Press it properly, coat one cube at a time in the second before it goes in, pull them out PALE, and pour the broth into the bowl first so the tofu stands in it. Crisp all over means over-fried.","pairsWith":"A first plate before almost anything — grilled skewers, a bowl of noodles, a donburi. Its job on a table is the cold-and-hot, soft-and-crisp contrast, so put it beside something dry and charred rather than beside another fried thing.","trivia":"It is worth noticing that this is a dish engineered to go soggy on purpose, which is close to unique in fried cookery. Almost everything else fried anywhere in the world is built to defend its crust — drained, racked, salted, served the instant it leaves the oil, kept as far from moisture as possible. Agedashi tofu does the opposite: it is deliberately fried in a starch that dissolves, deliberately pulled out pale so the crust stays thin, and then deliberately stood in hot liquid, because the coating's real job is not to stay crisp but to melt slowly into the broth, thickening it very slightly and creating a gradient of texture from the top of the cube to the bottom. The crispness is a stage in the dish rather than the goal of it. The recipe is also unusually well documented, because it appears in a book that was one of the first genuine food bestsellers in Japan. In 1782 a publisher in Osaka brought out Tofu Hyakuchin — 'One Hundred Delights of Tofu' — a whole cookbook devoted to a single cheap ingredient, ranked from everyday recipes up to a small number of extraordinary ones. It sold in numbers nobody had expected from a cookery book and set off a publishing craze: sequels followed, and then hundred-delight books on daikon, on sweet potato, on egg. Two and a half centuries later the tofu book is still in print, and agedashi tofu made the original list, which is to say the dish has been on Japanese tables in more or less this form since before most European cuisines had written their own recipes down.","howThisFeels":"The little hiss when the starch hits the oil, and then the sound at the table — a crisp top giving way, and underneath it something that is barely holding together.","leftovers":["(easy) Drained of its broth, leftover fried tofu is excellent dropped into a bowl of miso soup or hot noodles, where it behaves like the aburaage in a proper soup and soaks up everything around it.","(easy) Simmer the pieces gently in the remaining broth with a little extra soy and some sliced mushrooms for ten minutes and serve them over rice — the coating thickens the sauce into a light gravy by itself.","(creative) Any tsuyu left in the bowl is a finished seasoning liquid and should not be poured away: chill it and use it as the dressing for cold noodles or over a plate of blanched greens, where it needs nothing added to it at all."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Firm Tofu, Shallow-Fried (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":14,"howThisFeels":"Chewier and sturdier, and forgiving enough that you can talk to somebody while you cook it.","trivia":"Firm tofu is the ordinary domestic version of this dish and there is nothing second-rate about it — it is what most Japanese home kitchens actually use, because silken tofu breaks if you look at it and a shallow pan of oil is easier to justify on a weeknight than a deep one.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"200g silken tofu","to":"200g firm tofu"},{"from":"400ml neutral oil","to":"60ml neutral oil"},{"from":"150ml dashi","to":"150ml kombu and shiitake dashi"}],"removeIng":[{"item":"2g katsuobushi"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Firm tofu holds far less free water, so 15 minutes of pressing is enough and the cubes can be cut larger without collapsing. In a shallow pan you are frying four faces rather than all six at once: give each of the two flat sides about 2 minutes and then roll the cubes onto their edges for 30 seconds each, and keep the heat a little lower than for deep-frying since a thin layer of oil swings in temperature much faster. The crust will be sturdier and less lacy than the silken version, which means it dissolves more slowly in the broth — so serve this one in slightly less liquid, or it will still be crunchy at the last bite instead of yielding."}]}},{"name":"Classic Silken Agedashi","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":22},{"name":"Mushroom Ankake (Vegan)","icon":"🍄","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":19,"howThisFeels":"The broth clings instead of pooling, and the whole bowl stays hot right to the last piece.","trivia":"Ankake simply means a starch-thickened sauce, and it is a standard trick of Japanese temple and winter cooking for one very practical reason: a thickened liquid holds its heat dramatically longer than a thin one, because convection currents cannot move through it. A bowl that would have gone lukewarm halfway through stays hot to the end.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150ml dashi","to":"200ml kombu and shiitake dashi"}],"removeIng":[{"item":"2g katsuobushi"}],"addIng":[{"item":"80g mixed mushrooms — shiitake, oyster, brown — sliced"},{"item":"8g potato starch"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Simmer the sliced mushrooms in the seasoned dashi for 4 minutes until they have given up their liquid and taken some back. Then stir the slaked starch again right before you use it — starch settles into a solid pad at the bottom of a bowl within a minute and a half of standing, and pouring off the water above it is the reason a sauce sometimes refuses to thicken. Pour it into the simmering broth in a thin stream while stirring, and let it come back to a bubble for 30 seconds to cook the starch out; it will turn from milky to clear and glossy. Spoon it around the tofu rather than over it, exactly as with the thin broth."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🍥"},{"name":"Tempura","target":"japan-tempura","emoji":"🍤"},{"name":"Gyoza","target":"japan-gyoza","emoji":"🥟"}]},
{"id":"japan-goma-ae","name":"Goma-ae","nameAlt":"Horenso no Goma-ae — greens dressed in ground sesame","aliases":["Goma-ae","Gomaae","Sesame Spinach","Japanese Sesame Dressed Greens"],"course":"side","type":["side","vegetable","salad","lunchbox"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","lunchbox","quick","entertaining"],"ingredients":"150g spinach · 20g white sesame seeds, toasted · 8g caster sugar · 10ml soy sauce · 5ml mirin · 5g fine salt","method":"Three ingredients, four minutes of cooking, and two techniques that decide entirely whether this tastes like a restaurant side dish or like wet leaves in a puddle. Neither of them is the cooking. Start the water first, because the greens go in and out fast and there is no time to be organising anything once they are in. Bring a wide pan of water to a hard rolling boil and salt it properly — 5g is right for a litre, and it seasons the leaf from the outside in a way no dressing added later can. Wash the spinach and, if it is bunched spinach with roots, keep the stems together and aligned rather than letting it become a heap; you will want that alignment shortly. Now blanch it, and blanch it briefly. Hold the stems and dip them into the boiling water for about 15 seconds first, since they are thicker and slower, then let the whole bunch down and give it 30 to 45 seconds more. That is all. The leaves will collapse and go a startling bright green. THEN STRAIGHT INTO ICE WATER, and have it already sitting next to the stove. This is not a nicety. The green in a leaf is chlorophyll, which holds a magnesium ion at the centre of its molecule, and heat and the plant's own acids knock that ion out and replace it with hydrogen — at which point the pigment becomes pheophytin and the colour turns the drab olive of tinned peas. That reaction is irreversible and it accelerates the whole time the greens are hot, including in the three minutes they sit steaming in a colander while you look for the dressing. Ice water stops it dead. Leave them in for a minute, no longer, or they start taking water back on. Now the first of the two techniques that matter, and it is the one that separates a good goma-ae from a disappointing one: THE SQUEEZE. Lift the spinach out and gather it into a rough log with the stems all running the same way. Then squeeze it, and squeeze it much harder than feels reasonable. Work along the log in sections, from one end to the other, wringing each part between both palms until no more water comes. Then open it out, re-gather it and do it a second time. You will be astonished how much water comes out of what looked like drained spinach — leaves are mostly water and blanching adds more. Every gram you leave behind ends up in the bottom of the serving bowl, thinning the dressing off the leaves into a grey puddle, and that puddle is the entire difference between the version you eat out and the version you make at home. Once it is properly wrung, cut the log across into 3cm lengths, which is why the stems were kept aligned — you get neat bundles rather than a tangle. Now the second technique, which is the grind. Put the toasted sesame into a suribachi, the Japanese grooved mortar, or into any mortar you own, and work the pestle in a circular scraping motion rather than pounding. And here is the law: GRIND TO HALF-BROKEN, NOT TO PASTE. You are aiming for roughly two-thirds of the seeds cracked open and a third still whole, a coarse damp meal that just begins to clump when you press it. Both extremes fail, and they fail for opposite reasons. Whole seeds are sealed inside a hull that human digestion does not open, so an untouched seed contributes almost no flavour, releases none of its oil and passes straight through — a dressing of whole sesame seeds is decoration. Go too far the other way and the oil comes fully out, the meal turns to a smooth grey paste, and what you have made is tahini: perfectly nice, but it coats the leaves in a slick film and loses the faint granular catch on the tongue that is the whole texture of the dish. If you are using a suribachi you can feel the right moment, because the sound changes from a dry rattle to a soft rasp. It is worth saying what that tool actually is, because it is not a Western mortar with a different name. The inside of a suribachi is combed with dozens of fine radial grooves, and it works by SHEAR — dragging seeds across ridges to split them — where a smooth marble mortar works by impact and pressure. That is why you rotate rather than pound, and why a suribachi will crack a bowl of sesame in ninety seconds and a marble one takes five minutes and turns half of it to butter. A food processor is the poor option here for exactly the same reason: blades either miss the seeds entirely or overshoot into paste, with very little useful ground in between. Add the sugar, soy and mirin to the ground sesame in the mortar and stir it into a thick, sandy, barely-pourable dressing — it should hold a ribbon for a second, not run. The ratio worth remembering is roughly three parts sesame to one part sugar to one part soy by weight, and once you know it you never need the recipe again. Finally, DRESS IT AT THE LAST MINUTE. Fold the spinach through the dressing with your hands or two spoons, lifting rather than stirring, until every bundle is coated. The dressing is salty and sweet, which means it is osmotically hungry, and left standing it will pull water straight back out of the leaves you just spent all that effort wringing dry — twenty minutes in a bowl and it is weeping again. Serve it in a small heap, at room temperature or lightly chilled, never hot.","cookTime":"Blanch 1 min · Shock and squeeze 5 min · Grind and dress 5 min","kcal":"~200 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 8g, Carbs 14g, Fat 12g, Sodium ~800mg","storage":"Undressed, the squeezed and cut greens keep 3 days in a sealed box in the fridge and the dressing keeps a week in a jar, so the sensible habit is to store them apart and combine a portion at a time. Dressed, eat it the same day — it will be watery by the next morning even in the fridge. It does not freeze: blanched greens thaw limp and collapsed, and the dressing separates.","chefNotes":"Ice water waiting before the greens go in, squeeze twice and far harder than feels polite, grind to half-broken and stop, and dress only as it goes to the table.","pairsWith":"The green thing on a Japanese plate, and it earns its place beside anything rich, charred or fried — grilled skewers, a crumbed cutlet, curry, or in a lunchbox next to rice and rolled egg.","trivia":"Grinding sesame gave Japanese the word for a flatterer. Goma-suri, literally 'sesame-grinding', means a person who sucks up to their superiors, and the image behind it is exact rather than vague: ground sesame is sticky and clinging, it gets smeared around the grooves of the mortar and sticks to absolutely everything it touches, and so does the sort of person the word describes. It is one of the most common everyday insults in the language, and it comes from this bowl. The bowl itself deserves the attention. Sesame is among the oldest cultivated oilseeds on earth, but the reason it became structural to Japanese cooking rather than incidental is that Buddhist temple kitchens had a problem to solve. Shojin cooking permits no meat, no fish and no dairy, which leaves a cuisine with almost no fat and very little concentrated protein — and fat is what makes food satisfying and what carries aroma. Seeds and nuts were the answer, but only if you could get inside them, since an intact hull surrenders nothing. So the tools came with the cooking: the grooved mortar arrives in Japanese kitchens alongside temple cuisine, and a whole family of ground-seed dressings descends from that single problem — sesame dressing here, walnut dressing, and shira-ae, in which mashed tofu and ground sesame together do the work that cream would do somewhere else. What looks like a light vegetable side is really a solution to a nutritional constraint, which is why it is more filling than a plate of greens has any right to be.","howThisFeels":"The moment the leaves hit iced water and go almost luminous, and then the dry rasp of seeds cracking under the pestle — the two most satisfying sounds in a small dish.","leftovers":["(easy) Any dressing left in the jar is a finished sauce and should not be thrown out: it is superb on cold blanched green beans, on steamed broccoli, on cucumber, or thinned with a splash of water into a dressing for a plain leaf salad.","(easy) Fold leftover dressed greens through hot rice with a little extra soy for an instant sesame rice bowl — the residual dressing seasons the whole bowl and the wateriness stops mattering once it meets warm grains.","(creative) Chop leftovers finely and stir them into a beaten egg for a green rolled omelette, or through the filling of a rice ball, where the sesame does something quietly excellent against plain rice."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Cabbage & Green Bean (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":6,"howThisFeels":"Crunchier and sweeter than the spinach, and cheap enough to make a bowl of it without thinking about it.","trivia":"Goma-ae is a method rather than a recipe, and in Japanese home cooking it is applied to whatever is cheap and in season — cabbage, beans, broccoli, carrot, chrysanthemum leaves, even blanched bean sprouts. Naming it after spinach is a restaurant habit; a household simply dresses what it has.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"150g spinach","to":"100g white cabbage · 60g green beans"}],"addStep":[{"text":"These are firmer than spinach and need real blanching rather than a dip: give the beans 3 minutes and the cabbage 90 seconds, in the same salted water, and lift each out separately when it is tender but still has an audible snap. Both go into the ice water the same way. The squeeze changes here — cabbage holds a lot of water and should be wrung firmly, but beans hold almost none and only need patting dry, and squeezing them just crushes them. Because these vegetables are sweeter than spinach, cut the sugar in the dressing by about a third or the whole bowl tips over into dessert."}]}},{"name":"Classic Horenso no Goma-ae","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":11},{"name":"Shira-ae — Tofu & Sesame Dressing","icon":"🧂","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":13,"howThisFeels":"Pale, creamy and unexpectedly rich, and the first time you eat it you will not believe there is no dairy anywhere near it.","trivia":"Shira-ae means 'white dressed', and it is the older and more ceremonial sibling of goma-ae — a temple dish that turns tofu and sesame into something with the mouthfeel of a cream dressing using neither cream nor egg. It was for centuries a dish served at Buddhist memorial meals, which is why the white is not accidental.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"100g firm tofu"},{"item":"3g white miso paste"},{"item":"30g carrot, cut into fine matchsticks"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"10ml soy sauce","to":"5ml soy sauce"}],"addStep":[{"text":"The tofu has to be dried out properly first or the dressing is soup: wrap the block in kitchen paper, weight it for 20 minutes, then crumble it into a dry non-stick pan over a medium heat and stir it for 3 to 4 minutes until it stops steaming and looks matte rather than glossy. Cool it. Then press it through a sieve with the back of a spoon into the ground sesame — a sieve rather than a masher, because tofu mashed with a fork stays lumpy and the whole character of shira-ae is smoothness. Beat in the miso and the reduced soy. Blanch the carrot matchsticks for 60 seconds with the spinach so there is a second colour and a bit of bite in the pale dressing, and fold everything together very gently — this dressing is thick and will bruise the leaves if you stir it hard."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Yakitori","target":"japan-yakitori","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Tamagoyaki","target":"japan-tamagoyaki","emoji":"🍳"},{"name":"Onigiri","target":"japan-onigiri","emoji":"🍙"}]},
{"id":"japan-matcha-warabimochi","name":"Matcha Warabimochi","nameAlt":"Warabimochi — the jelly that is not mochi at all","aliases":["Matcha Warabimochi","Warabimochi","Warabi Mochi","Bracken Starch Jelly","Kinako Jelly"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","sweet","summer","quick"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","summer","entertaining","quick"],"ingredients":"30g cornflour · 25g caster sugar · 150ml cold water · 4g matcha powder · 15g kinako (roasted soybean flour) · 20g brown sugar · 20ml water","method":"Start with the name, because it is the only thing here that lies to you. Warabimochi contains no mochi, no glutinous rice and no pounding — it is not a mochi at all but a set starch jelly, and everything that makes it good comes from understanding that it is a gel rather than a dough. It is also, in the version you will actually make, not made of warabi. True hon-warabiko is bracken-root starch, and it is not on a South African shelf at any price worth paying: where it can be imported at all it runs R350 to R600 for 100 to 200 grams before shipping and customs. So this is the cornflour route, and it is written honestly rather than apologetically, because as the note below explains, most warabimochi eaten in Japan is not made of warabi either. Cornflour costs under R30 for 500g, it sets slightly more opaque and a little more matte, and it wants a fraction less liquid and a firmer hand on the stirring. If you can find tapioca starch, it is the closer texture match and there is a version below built on it. Sift the cornflour, the caster sugar and the matcha together into a cold pan. Sifting is not tidiness — matcha is a very fine, very static powder that clumps the instant it meets moisture, and a lump of dry matcha in a set jelly is a bitter green pocket that no amount of stirring will find later. Now pour in the cold water and whisk it smooth BEFORE THE PAN GOES NEAR THE HEAT. This is the first law and it is the one that catches everybody: STARCH IS DISSOLVED COLD, NEVER HOT. A starch granule dropped into hot liquid gelatinises on its own outside in under a second and seals itself into a lump with dry powder still inside it, and once that has happened the lump is permanent — it will not whisk out, it will not sieve out, and it will sit in the finished jelly as a chalky white bead. Whisk until you cannot feel any grain on the bottom of the pan with the whisk, then run it through a sieve into the pan if you have any doubt at all. It should look like thin, flat green paint. Now put it over a MEDIUM-LOW heat and start stirring, and do not stop stirring until it is finished — with a wooden spoon or a silicone spatula, dragging across the whole base of the pan including the corners, continuously. There is no window here in which you can turn away and fetch something. The mixture will do three distinct things and you need to recognise all three. For the first minute or two nothing happens at all. Then it will suddenly go thick and opaque and pale, all at once, and become genuinely hard work to move — this is the moment most people stop, and it is far too early. Keep going. Then, over another two or three minutes of real effort, it will change again: it goes GLOSSY and TRANSLUCENT, the pale opacity clears, and you can see the spoon through the mixture as it folds over itself. That is the second law and the only doneness test that matters: TRANSLUCENCY IS DONENESS, NOT THICKNESS. A mixture pulled at the thick-and-opaque stage still has raw starch in it, tastes faintly of flour and goes cloudy and grainy as it cools. Cook it until it is a glossy, stretchy, translucent mass that comes away from the sides of the pan in one piece and pulls into a thick ribbon off the spoon. Total time from cold is around five to six minutes. Tip it immediately into a shallow dish or tin lined with cling film or wetted with cold water, spread it roughly level with a wet spatula, and let it cool at ROOM TEMPERATURE. Twenty minutes is enough. And here is the third law, which is the one that will actually ruin your work if you break it: NEVER PUT WARABIMOCHI IN THE FRIDGE. Not to speed it up, not overnight, not for an hour. Starch gels retrograde — the amylose chains that are holding all that water in a loose mesh slowly recrystallise back into tight bundles and squeeze the water out — and cold accelerates that reaction enormously. A refrigerated warabimochi goes hard, cloudy, rubbery and slightly weeping, and the change is irreversible; no amount of standing at room temperature brings it back. This is the same chemistry that turns yesterday's cold rice into something that will not hold together, and it bites harder here than it would with real bracken starch, because hon-warabiko is very low in amylose and cornflour is around a quarter amylose. The substitution is honest and it works, but it buys you a shorter life. Make it on the day, and make it in the hour if you can. Toast the kinako while it cools: two or three minutes in a dry pan over a low heat, moving it constantly, until it smells nutty and darkens by a shade. It is sold already roasted, but a fresh toast wakes it up completely and takes almost no time. Tip it onto a plate to stop it cooking on in the hot pan. Make the syrup by melting the brown sugar with the 20ml of water over a low heat, swirling rather than stirring, until it is a dark, loose, pourable syrup — about two minutes, and stop while it still runs freely, because it thickens a great deal as it cools. Now cut and serve. Dust a board generously with about half the kinako, turn the set jelly out onto it, and dust the top as well. Cut it into rough 3cm squares with a WET knife, or better, with a plastic scraper or the edge of a spoon — a dry blade drags and tears where a wet one slides. Do not aim for neat cubes; warabimochi is meant to look soft-edged and slightly irregular, and a squared-off grid looks like jelly rather than like this. Toss the pieces in the remaining kinako until every face is coated, pile them in a shallow bowl, and pour the syrup over just before it reaches the table. Eat it within the hour, at room temperature, with a small fork or a toothpick.","cookTime":"Mix 5 min · Cook 6 min · Set 20 min","kcal":"~290 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 5g, Carbs 62g, Fat 2g, Sodium ~10mg","storage":"This is genuinely a same-day sweet and the honest advice is to make only what will be eaten. At cool room temperature, covered, it holds its texture for about six hours and is at its best in the first one. Do NOT refrigerate it under any circumstances — the starch retrogrades and it turns hard, cloudy and weeping, and there is no way back. It does not freeze for the same reason, only faster. The kinako keeps for months in a sealed jar and the syrup keeps a fortnight in the fridge, so if you want to work ahead, prepare those two and make the jelly itself in the ten minutes before you serve it.","chefNotes":"Dissolve the starch in COLD water and whisk it perfectly smooth before any heat — a lump formed in hot liquid is permanent. Then cook past thick and opaque all the way to glossy and translucent, because translucency is doneness and thickness is not. And never, ever refrigerate it.","pairsWith":"Green tea, and the sharper the better — a plain sencha or a roasted hojicha, served hot even in summer, because the whole plate is sugar and starch and it wants bitterness against it. It also sits well at the end of a rich meal like curry or a crumbed cutlet, where something cold, clean and barely sweet is exactly what is needed.","trivia":"Almost no warabimochi contains warabi, and this is true in Japan as much as anywhere else — which makes the substitution below a continuation of the tradition rather than a departure from it. Real bracken starch is one of the lowest-yielding ingredients in any cuisine: the starch is washed out of the roots of the bracken fern, and the commonly cited figure is that ten kilograms of dug root render somewhere around seventy grams of usable hon-warabiko. That is a yield of well under one percent, from a plant that has to be dug rather than harvested, and it is why genuine warabimochi is a specialist confectioner's item at a specialist's price even in Kyoto. Everything else — the supermarket tubs, the convenience-store cups, the vast majority of what is served in cafes — is made from sweet potato starch, tapioca starch or potato starch, usually blended, and the packet will often say warabimochi-ko, which means warabimochi flour and is not the same word as warabiko. It is a rare case of a dish whose name survived the disappearance of its defining ingredient without anybody much minding. The dish is old enough to have a court anecdote attached: it is traditionally associated with Emperor Daigo in the tenth century, who is said to have liked it enough to grant it a rank, which is the sort of detail that is impossible to verify and too good to leave out. What is better documented is what the bracken was for. Warabi root was famine food — a starch you could dig out of a hillside when the rice failed, processed through days of washing and settling — and the fact that it eventually became an expensive summer delicacy served in lacquered bowls is one of the more complete reversals in Japanese food, the same journey tempura made from street stall to tasting counter.","howThisFeels":"The moment the pan goes from stiff pale paste to clear glossy green in about ten seconds, and it stops feeling like cooking and starts feeling like a chemistry trick you have got away with.","leftovers":["(easy) There is not usually any, but if there is, do not chill it — leave it covered at room temperature and eat it within a few hours, dusted with fresh kinako, since the first coating will have gone damp and disappeared into the surface.","(easy) Leftover toasted kinako is far too good to waste: it is superb stirred into hot milk with a spoon of honey, dusted over vanilla ice cream, or whisked into a bowl of plain yoghurt where it tastes remarkably like a peanut butter without any peanuts in it.","(creative) Leftover syrup and kinako together make an instant dessert out of almost anything plain — pour them over a bowl of cold rice pudding, over sliced banana, or over a plain steamed sponge, and the toasted-bean-and-molasses combination does all the work."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Kinako & Simple Syrup (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":9,"howThisFeels":"Plain, pale and honest, and the version that makes you realise the matcha was never the reason you liked it.","trivia":"This is the original and still the standard: for most of its history warabimochi was starch, water, a little sugar, kinako and syrup, and nothing else. The matcha is a modern addition and largely a visual one. Kinako is the ingredient actually carrying the dish, and it is worth knowing what it is — roasted soybeans, ground to a fine flour, and nothing more. It is one of the oldest processed foods in Japan, it is almost pure protein and fibre with a deep toasted flavour somewhere between peanut and malt, and a Japanese kitchen uses it the way a European one uses cocoa powder.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"4g matcha powder"}],"addStep":[{"text":"With the matcha gone there is nothing to sift for colour, but sift the cornflour and sugar together anyway — cornflour clumps on its own and the cold-mix law is unchanged. The jelly will set almost clear with a faint grey cast, which is exactly right and is what it is supposed to look like. Since there is no matcha bitterness balancing the sugar, go a shade heavier on the kinako and make the syrup slightly darker by giving it an extra thirty seconds on the heat, so the toasted and molasses notes carry the plate instead."}]}},{"name":"Classic Matcha Warabimochi","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":17},{"name":"Tapioca Starch — the Teahouse Texture","icon":"🍵","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":21,"howThisFeels":"Bouncier, clearer and far more elastic — you can stretch a piece between two fingers before it gives, which is the thing cornflour cannot quite do.","trivia":"If the cornflour route is the accessible one, this is the accurate one. Tapioca starch is very low in amylose, which is the same property that makes real bracken starch behave the way it does, and the practical result is a jelly that stays soft and stretchy instead of firming up — it resists retrogradation far better, so it holds its texture for longer and is more forgiving if the timing slips. It is also clearer, so a matcha version comes out a much more convincing green. It is sold in most Asian grocers and in the baking aisle of larger supermarkets, sometimes labelled tapioca flour, which is the same thing. Sweet potato starch behaves almost identically and is what a great many Japanese manufacturers actually use.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"30g cornflour","to":"35g tapioca starch"},{"from":"150ml cold water","to":"170ml cold water"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Tapioca takes more liquid than cornflour and it behaves differently on the heat, so watch for the change rather than the clock. It thickens later and then moves much faster — there is a shorter window between opaque paste and glossy translucence, sometimes only twenty or thirty seconds, so keep the heat a notch lower and stir harder through the transition. It also goes considerably stickier at the end and will fight the spatula; wet the setting dish and the spatula rather than oiling them. The finished jelly is noticeably bouncier and clearer than the cornflour version, and because it retrogrades far more slowly it will still be good several hours later — though the no-fridge law stands regardless."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dorayaki","target":"japan-dorayaki","emoji":"🥞"},{"name":"Goma-ae","target":"japan-goma-ae","emoji":"🥬"},{"name":"Agedashi Tofu","target":"japan-agedashi-tofu","emoji":"🍢"}]},
{"id":"japan-mitarashi-dango","name":"Mitarashi Dango","nameAlt":"Grilled rice dumplings in a sweet-soy glaze","aliases":["Mitarashi Dango","Dango","Rice Dumpling Skewers","Sweet Soy Dango","Yaki Dango"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","sweet","street-food","skewer","grilled"],"diet":["vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","street-food","entertaining","festival"],"ingredients":"60g rice flour · 40g glutinous rice flour · 70ml hot water · 30ml soy sauce · 30g caster sugar · 15ml mirin · 60ml water · 8g cornflour","method":"Everything good about dango comes from the fact that it is made of two flours rather than one, and everything disappointing about a homemade dango comes from somebody using only what they had. So begin there. Rice flour — plain, non-glutinous, milled from ordinary short or medium grain rice, sold as joshinko in Japan — gives structure, a clean bite and the ability to sit on a skewer without sagging. Glutinous rice flour, sold as shiratamako or mochiko or simply as glutinous rice flour in an Asian grocer, gives the stretch and the soft chew. Use only the plain and you get a firm, slightly crumbly dumpling that cracks when you skewer it and goes chalky in an hour. Use only the glutinous and you get something so soft and sticky it slumps off the stick and melts in the pan rather than colouring. The blend, roughly three parts plain to two glutinous, is the whole dish, and it is not a matter of taste — it is what lets you grill something made mostly of starch without it collapsing. Whisk the two flours together dry in a bowl, then add the hot water in three or four goes, mixing with a spoon at first and then with your hand. HOT water, not boiling and not cold: around 70 to 80°C, hot enough that you would not hold your finger in it. The heat begins gelatinising the surface starch as you mix, which is what makes the dough cohesive and workable instead of sandy — this is the same reason a good gnocchi wants warm potato. Hold back the last of the water. Flours vary enormously in how much they absorb depending on the mill and the humidity in your kitchen, and a wet dough cannot be rescued, only diluted with more flour until the ratio is wrong. Knead it in the bowl and then on a clean surface for two or three minutes until it is completely smooth and no longer tacky on your palm. Now the test, which is the traditional one and is genuinely more reliable than any measurement: THE DOUGH SHOULD FEEL LIKE YOUR EARLOBE. Pinch your earlobe, then pinch the dough. Soft, yielding, with a very slight resistance and springing back slowly. If the dough is firmer than that it will boil up dense and the skewer will split it; wet your hands and knead the water in. If it is softer, dust in a little more plain rice flour. Divide the dough and roll it into balls about 20 grams each, which comes out at roughly the size of a large marble — you want twelve for three skewers of four, or fifteen for three of five. Roll each one properly round between your cupped palms, then check for cracks; a crack in a raw dango opens up into a fissure in the boiling water. Smooth any you find with a damp fingertip. Bring a wide pan of water to a rolling boil, salt it not at all, and drop the balls in. They will sink. Leave them alone. After three or four minutes they will rise and bob at the surface, and this is the first signal but not the finish — GIVE THEM TWO MORE MINUTES AFTER THEY FLOAT. Floating means the outside has cooked and trapped enough steam to lift them; the centre is still raw flour paste at that point, and an undercooked dango has a distinct chalky core you cannot mistake for anything else. Lift them straight out with a slotted spoon and drop them into a bowl of ICE WATER for a full minute. This is not merely to cool them for handling. The shock firms the surface, stops the residual heat cooking them further, and gives you the taut skin that browns properly on the grill instead of sticking and tearing. Drain them and pat them dry, thoroughly, on a clean cloth — the drier the surface, the better the char. Thread four or five onto each skewer, pushing gently and turning rather than forcing, and leave a small gap after the first one if you want the traditional arrangement. Now make the tare. Put the soy sauce, sugar, mirin and the 60ml of water in a small pan and bring it to a simmer over a medium heat, stirring until the sugar has gone. Slake the cornflour separately in two teaspoons of cold water — cold, whisked completely smooth, and only then poured into the simmering pan in a thin stream while you stir. Cornflour dropped dry into hot liquid seizes into permanent lumps, and there is no rescuing a lumpy tare. It will thicken almost immediately; cook it thirty seconds longer so it loses the raw starch edge, and pull it off when it is glossy and coats the back of a spoon while still pouring freely. It thickens considerably as it cools, so err on the loose side. Now grill, and here is the law that separates a real mitarashi dango from a boiled dumpling with sauce on it: GRILL THEM DRY, AND GRILL THEM FIRST. Get a griddle pan, a heavy frying pan or a grill properly hot, brush it with the barest film of oil or none at all, and lay the skewers on with NO GLAZE. Turn them every minute or so until each dumpling has taken real colour — patchy golden brown with a few genuinely blackened blisters, three or four minutes in total. Those scorched spots are not damage, they are the point. The tare is sweet and salty and one-dimensional on its own, and it needs bitterness and smoke to push against; a dango glazed before it is grilled simply candies in the pan, the sugar burns before the dumpling colours, and you end up with something cloying and slightly acrid. Char first, then glaze. Take them off the heat, brush or spoon the warm tare generously over each skewer, turning to coat, and either serve immediately or give them a further thirty seconds back on the heat to set the glaze to a lacquer. Eat them warm, the same day, ideally within the hour.","cookTime":"Mix 10 min · Boil 6 min · Grill 4 min","kcal":"~230 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 4g, Carbs 50g, Fat 1g, Sodium ~780mg","storage":"Best within an hour of grilling and genuinely not the same thing the next day. Dango staling is starch retrogradation, exactly as it is in bread and in cold rice, and the fridge accelerates it — so keep them covered at room temperature, where they hold for around six hours, and never chill them. If you must work ahead, boil and shock the dumplings, dry them, and keep them covered at room temperature for up to four hours, then grill and glaze at the last minute; that stage is the one that does not wait. Uncooked dough will sit wrapped in the fridge overnight but wants twenty minutes back at room temperature before you can roll it. The tare keeps a fortnight in a sealed jar in the fridge and reheats gently.","chefNotes":"Two flours, never one — plain rice flour for structure, glutinous for chew, and the blend is what lets a starch dumpling survive a skewer and a grill. Then char them naked before the glaze goes anywhere near them; the tare has nothing to push against without those blistered black spots.","pairsWith":"Green tea, unsweetened and hot, which is not a suggestion so much as the way it is always eaten — the tare is sweet and salty at once and a bitter tea cuts straight through it. Roasted hojicha works beautifully because the smokiness echoes the char. It also sits well alongside anything grilled and savoury, which is why you find it at the same festival stalls as skewered chicken.","trivia":"The name is a shrine, and the dumplings are a body. Mitarashi is the stone font at the entrance to a Shinto shrine where visitors rinse their hands and mouth before approaching, and this particular one belongs to Shimogamo Shrine in Kyoto, where a spring feeds a pond called Mitarashi. The story that has attached itself to the dish, and which the neighbouring teahouse has traded on for centuries, is that the dumplings represent the bubbles that rise from the bottom of that spring — and that the arrangement on the skewer, one dumpling set slightly apart and then four together, is a human figure offered up to the gods, the single one being the head and the remaining four the arms and legs. That five-and-a-gap arrangement is still how they are threaded in Kyoto, while much of the rest of Japan long ago settled on four in a row, and the usual explanation given for the change is thoroughly unromantic: in the Edo period coins came in denominations that made four dumplings for four mon far easier to sell than five. A sacred offering was reorganised for the convenience of small change. The other thing worth knowing is that the glaze came late. Dango are ancient — rice-flour dumplings appear in Japanese records well over a thousand years old, and for most of that history they were eaten plain, salted, or dusted with kinako. Sugar was an expensive import for centuries, and a sweet soy glaze of the kind on these skewers only became an everyday possibility once domestic sugar production made it cheap enough to pour over street food. The dumpling is old; the thing everyone associates with it is comparatively new.","howThisFeels":"The smell that comes off the pan the second the glaze hits hot charred dough — soy and caramel catching at once — which is the exact smell of a festival street after dark.","leftovers":["(easy) Day-old dango have gone firm, and the answer is heat rather than the bin: put them back on a dry hot pan for two minutes a side and the starch softens again completely, then re-glaze with any tare you have left. They will not be quite as good as they were, but they will be a great deal better than they look.","(easy) Leftover tare is one of the most useful things in the fridge. It is essentially the same sauce family as a teriyaki glaze, so brush it over grilled chicken or aubergine, stir a spoon into a bowl of plain noodles, or use it as a dipping sauce for anything fried.","(creative) Cut cold leftover dumplings in half, pan-fry the cut faces until crisp and golden, and serve them in a bowl of hot clear broth — they behave rather like a gnocchi and turn a plain soup into something substantial."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Sugar & Soy Only (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":11,"howThisFeels":"Sharper and saltier without the mirin's roundness, and closer to what these actually tasted like before sugar got cheap.","trivia":"Mirin is the first thing to go when a Japanese kitchen is economising, and it has an honest substitute that costs nothing: a little extra sugar. Mirin is sweet rice wine, and what it brings beyond sweetness is a faint alcoholic depth and a glossiness that comes from its own sugars caramelising, so replacing it with plain sugar gets you most of the way there. The one thing to know is that mirin's sweetness is gentler than white sugar's — roughly speaking a tablespoon of mirin sweetens about as much as a teaspoon of sugar — so you add far less than you removed, not more.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"15ml mirin"}],"addStep":[{"text":"With no mirin, add just 5g more caster sugar to the tare and nothing else — mirin is much less sweet than it tastes, and matching it spoon for spoon with sugar makes the glaze sickly. The sauce will be slightly less glossy and a shade sharper, which is not a fault; it lets the soy come forward. If you want some of the roundness back without buying mirin, a teaspoon of rice vinegar added at the very end lifts it and stops the sweetness sitting flat on the tongue."}]}},{"name":"Classic Mitarashi Dango","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":14},{"name":"Isobe Dango — Grilled, Soy-Brushed, Nori-Wrapped","icon":"🌊","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":19,"howThisFeels":"Entirely savoury, and the moment the hot dumpling hits the nori and the seaweed goes soft and smells of the sea is worth the whole exercise.","trivia":"Isobe means a rocky shore, and in Japanese cooking any dish with isobe in the name involves nori — the word is doing the work that the word marinara does in Italian, telling you where the flavour comes from rather than what it is. Isobe-yaki is the older and plainer sibling of mitarashi dango and it makes the point that dango were never inherently a sweet: the same dumpling, grilled and brushed with soy, is a savoury snack, and this is how they were eaten for most of their history before cheap sugar arrived. The nori is not decoration. Wrapped around a dumpling straight off the heat it softens and clings, and the glutamates in seaweed and the glutamates in soy sauce amplify each other — the same effect that makes dashi work, arriving here without a pot of stock in sight.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"30g caster sugar"},{"item":"60ml water"},{"item":"8g cornflour"}],"addIng":[{"item":"1 sheet nori, cut into strips"}],"addStep":[{"text":"There is no cooked tare here at all, which makes this the quickest version by some distance. Warm the soy sauce and mirin together in a small pan for thirty seconds, no thickening and no sugar, and keep it loose. Grill the skewers dry exactly as before until properly blistered, then brush them with the warm soy and put them back on the heat for fifteen seconds a side so it dries onto the surface rather than sitting wet on it — that flash back on the pan is what gives the savoury version its lacquer. Cut the nori sheet into strips a little wider than a dumpling, and wrap one around each skewer while everything is still hot so the seaweed softens and grips. Eat immediately; the nori goes leathery once it cools."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Yakitori","target":"japan-yakitori","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Matcha Warabimochi","target":"japan-matcha-warabimochi","emoji":"🍧"},{"name":"Dorayaki","target":"japan-dorayaki","emoji":"🥞"}]},
{"id":"japan-takoyaki","name":"Takoyaki","nameAlt":"Osaka octopus balls — crisp shell, molten centre","aliases":["Takoyaki","Octopus Balls","Tako Yaki","Osaka Street Balls"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","street-food","snack","sharing","griddle"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","street-food","entertaining","party"],"ingredients":"50g cake flour · 1 egg · 200ml dashi · 60g octopus, cooked and in 1.5cm pieces · 2 spring onions, sliced · 20g pickled red ginger (beni shoga), chopped · 10g tenkasu (tempura scraps) · 15ml neutral oil · 30ml okonomiyaki sauce · 20ml Japanese-style mayonnaise · 2g aonori (dried green seaweed flakes) · 4g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)","method":"You need a takoyaki pan — a heavy plate of half-spherical wells, cast iron or non-stick, sold cheaply anywhere with an Asian grocery and increasingly in ordinary kitchen shops. There is no honest substitute, and an aebleskiver pan or a Dutch poffertjes plate will do the job perfectly because they are the same object with a different accent. You also need a pick: a bamboo skewer, a thin metal cake tester, or the proper takoyaki picks that come with most pans. Everything below depends on having something thin and pointed in your hand rather than a spatula. Whisk the cake flour into the dashi first, in a jug, with the egg — flour into liquid rather than liquid into flour, because you are aiming for something far thinner than you will believe and starting from a paste means fighting lumps the whole way. And this is the first law, the one that people override on instinct and then wonder why their takoyaki are stodgy: THE BATTER IS SUPPOSED TO BE FAR TOO THIN. It should pour like single cream, or like full-cream milk with a little body — thinner than a crepe batter, very much thinner than a pancake batter. It will look wrong in the jug. It will feel like a mistake. It is the entire dish. A thick batter sets into a solid dumpling with a uniform bready crumb, which is a perfectly pleasant thing and is not takoyaki; the thin batter cooks into a crisp browned shell around a centre that stays almost liquid, and that violent contrast between a crackling outside and a molten inside is the only reason anybody queues for these. Season it with a good pinch of salt and leave it in the jug. Get everything else within arm's reach before you light the pan, because from the moment the batter goes in you will not have a spare hand: the octopus in a bowl, the spring onions, the beni shoga and the tenkasu each in their own, the oil, and your pick. Takoyaki is not a dish you assemble as you go. Now heat the pan over a medium-high heat until it is properly, seriously hot — a drop of batter should hiss and set on contact. Oil it generously, and generously means more than feels reasonable: brush or spoon oil into every well and across the flat surface between them, and top it up as you cook. These are not baked, they are closer to shallow-fried in the wells, and the oil is what gives the shell its colour and its crunch and what stops the whole plate welding itself to the pan. Now the second law, which looks like an accident and is entirely deliberate: FLOOD THE PLATE ON PURPOSE. Pour the batter into the wells until each is full, and then keep pouring until the batter runs across the entire surface of the pan and joins every well to every other one in a single thin sheet. Do not try to fill them neatly. That flooded webbing between the holes is not spillage and it is not waste — it is the raw material you are about to fold back in, and it is where the extra layers of each sphere come from. Working quickly, drop two or three pieces of octopus into each well, then scatter the spring onion, the beni shoga and the tenkasu across the whole pan, webbing included. The tenkasu matters more than its size suggests: they are the crisp scraps of fried batter left over from making tempura, and inside a takoyaki they absorb liquid, hold pockets of fat and keep the centre loose and rich rather than letting it set solid. Let it all cook, untouched, for two or three minutes, until the batter at the edges of the wells has firmed enough to hold a shape and the surface has gone from wet to matt. Now take your pick, and here is the third law and the technique the whole dish is named for: IT IS A QUARTER-TURN, NEVER A FLIP. First, run the pick around the rim of each well to cut it free from the webbing, then drag the surrounding sheet of set batter towards that well and tuck it in. Then slide the pick down the side of the ball and rotate it just NINETY DEGREES — a quarter turn, so that the cooked shell swings down into the well and the open, uncooked side comes up. Do not turn it over. A half-turn at this stage tips the still-liquid centre straight out and you are left with a hollow bowl. When you rotate ninety degrees, the raw batter inside runs out into the space you have just vacated and immediately begins to cook against the hot metal as a NEW layer of shell. That is the trick, and it is the whole reason the technique exists: each quarter-turn builds another lamina, so a sphere is assembled in four or five passes rather than formed in one. Go around the pan giving every ball a quarter-turn, then go around again a minute later, and again, and again — five or six passes over about six to eight minutes, adding oil to the pan as you go. By the third pass they will be spinning freely in their wells and you can start turning them for colour rather than for structure. They are ready when they are evenly deep golden brown all over, feel light for their size, and spin without resistance. The inside should still be soft enough to wobble. Pile them onto a plate, zigzag the okonomiyaki sauce and then the mayonnaise over the top, and finish with the aonori and a generous heap of katsuobushi. Serve at once, and warn people: the middle is genuinely molten and holds its heat far longer than the outside suggests. Eat with the picks.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 10 min","kcal":"~420 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 22g, Carbs 42g, Fat 18g, Sodium ~1150mg","storage":"Takoyaki is a same-minute food and everything good about it — the crisp shell against the molten centre — is gone within twenty minutes of leaving the pan. Cooked and cooled, they keep a day covered in the fridge, and they reheat far better than you would expect in a hot dry pan or an air fryer at 190°C for four or five minutes, which re-crisps the shell; a microwave turns them into soft dumplings and should be avoided entirely. Never sauce them until the moment of eating, since the sauce and mayonnaise soften the shell within minutes. Uncooked batter keeps overnight in the fridge in a covered jug and wants a good whisk before use, as the flour settles hard to the bottom. The cooked octopus keeps two days.","chefNotes":"Make the batter far thinner than instinct allows — it should pour like single cream, and that thinness is what buys you a molten centre inside a crisp shell. Then quarter-turns only, never a flip: ninety degrees lets the raw batter run out and cook as a new layer, while a half-turn dumps the middle out and leaves you a hollow shell.","pairsWith":"Cold beer, which is what they are sold beside on every festival street in Osaka and is not a coincidence — the sauce and mayonnaise are rich and sweet and want something bitter and cold against them. A dry sparkling wine does the same job. If you are building a meal around them, keep everything else sharp and clean: a vinegared cucumber salad or blanched greens in sesame, and nothing else fried.","trivia":"The dish is named for the octopus and the original had none in it. Takoyaki descends directly from radio-yaki, a batter snack sold in Osaka from around 1933 and named — with the straight-faced optimism of the period — after the radio, then the most glamorous object a household could aspire to owning. Radio-yaki contained no octopus at all. It was filled with konnyaku and beef sinew, the cheap chewy scraps that a working city ate, and it was cooked in the same wells and turned with the same picks. The change is credited to Tomekichi Endo, who ran a stall called Aizuya, and to a customer from Akashi who happened to remark in 1935 that in his home town they made a similar dumpling with octopus in it. Endo tried it, added dashi to the batter while he was at it, and a regional aside became the food that Osaka is now known for worldwide. The dumpling that customer was describing still exists and is a genuinely different thing: akashiyaki, from the port town of Akashi, is far richer in egg, so soft it barely holds together, and it is not sauced at all but dipped into a bowl of warm clear dashi like a savoury quenelle. Osaka took the octopus and kept its own sauce; Akashi kept the broth. The version below lets you make the older one. The other thing worth knowing is what those crisp scraps in the filling are: tenkasu are the stray drips of batter that fry loose in the oil while tempura is being cooked, and for centuries they were swept up and given away or thrown out. An entire category of Osaka street food is built on somebody deciding that the by-product was worth eating.","howThisFeels":"The first time a quarter-turn actually works — the shell swings down, the raw middle runs out into the empty half of the well and starts setting instantly, and you can feel the ball becoming round in your hand rather than being made round.","leftovers":["(easy) Reheat them dry and hot — a pan or an air fryer at 190°C for four or five minutes brings the shell back properly. Sauce them only after reheating, never before, and never use a microwave, which steams the crust off entirely.","(easy) Leftover batter is one of the most useful things you can have in the fridge. It is a thin savoury dashi batter, so pour it into a hot oiled frying pan and make thin savoury pancakes, or use it to coat sliced onion and sweet potato for a fast, rough approximation of a fritter.","(creative) Cold takoyaki, halved and pan-fried cut-side down until crisp, then dropped into a bowl of hot clear broth with some greens, becomes a genuinely good soup — the shell holds and the middle melts into the stock and thickens it slightly."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Radio-yaki — Konjac, the Ancestor (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":24,"howThisFeels":"Springier and cleaner in the middle, and once you have eaten it you stop thinking of the octopus as the point.","trivia":"This is not a modern substitution, it is the older dish. Radio-yaki was what Osaka ate for two years before anybody thought to put octopus in, and konnyaku was the filling that made it work. Konjac is made from the corm of a plant sometimes called devil's tongue, ground and set with a mineral alkali into a firm grey block, and it is remarkable stuff: it is roughly ninety-seven percent water and almost entirely soluble fibre, so it carries virtually no calories and no flavour of its own at all. What it has is bite. It squeaks slightly against the teeth and stays resiliently springy no matter how long it cooks, which is precisely the quality octopus was later admired for — the swap was never a compromise in texture, and arguably the octopus was the substitute.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"1 egg"},{"item":"60g octopus, cooked and in 1.5cm pieces"},{"item":"20ml Japanese-style mayonnaise"},{"item":"4g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)"}],"addIng":[{"item":"60g konjac, in 1.5cm cubes"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"200ml dashi","to":"200ml kombu and shiitake dashi"},{"from":"50g cake flour","to":"60g cake flour"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Two adjustments make this work rather than merely make it vegan. Without the egg the batter loses some of what binds and enriches it, so the flour goes up by ten grams — you are still aiming for single-cream thinness, so add the extra flour and judge by how it pours rather than by the measurement. And the dashi must be the kombu and shiitake kind, made by steeping a piece of dried kelp and two or three dried shiitake in cold water for a few hours: ordinary dashi and instant granules both carry bonito, so this is not an optional refinement. Konjac needs one piece of preparation the octopus does not. It comes packed in alkaline water and smells faintly of it, so drain it, cube it, and boil it in plain water for two or three minutes, then drain it again — this removes the smell entirely and firms the texture. Check that your okonomiyaki sauce carries no bonito, as some brands do."}]}},{"name":"Classic Osaka Takoyaki","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":38},{"name":"Akashiyaki — Egg-Rich, Dipped in Dashi","icon":"🥚","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"So soft they barely survive the journey from pan to bowl, and then they dissolve in hot broth like something between a dumpling and a custard.","trivia":"Akashi has been making these since well before Osaka borrowed the octopus, and locally they are not called akashiyaki at all but tamagoyaki, meaning simply the egg-grilled thing — which tells you where the emphasis sits. The batter is closer to a loose savoury custard than to a pancake mix, traditionally bound with a proportion of jinko, a fine wheat starch, and the result is so fragile that it is served on a small angled wooden board rather than a plate so the dumplings can be tipped straight into the broth without being handled. Nothing is poured over them. There is no sauce, no mayonnaise, no seaweed and no bonito on top, which makes it the clearest possible demonstration that all of that is Osaka's contribution rather than the dish's.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"30ml okonomiyaki sauce"},{"item":"20ml Japanese-style mayonnaise"},{"item":"2g aonori (dried green seaweed flakes)"},{"item":"4g katsuobushi (dried bonito flakes)"},{"item":"10g tenkasu (tempura scraps)"},{"item":"20g pickled red ginger (beni shoga), chopped"}],"addIng":[{"item":"150ml dashi, to dip"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"1 egg","to":"4 eggs"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Four eggs into the same amount of flour and dashi makes a batter that is barely a batter at all — it pours like thin custard and it will feel alarming. Oil the pan more heavily than for the classic version and keep the heat a shade lower, because there is far more protein here and it catches and toughens where the flour version would simply brown. Expect them to be much harder to turn: the quarter-turn technique is identical but the shell takes longer to form and tears more easily, so be patient through the first pass and do not force one that is not ready. They will never firm up the way Osaka takoyaki do, and they are not meant to. Take them out pale gold rather than deep brown. Warm the dipping dashi separately, season it with the barest splash of soy and a pinch of salt so it stays clear and light, and pour it into small individual bowls. Serve the dumplings alongside and let people dip them; they will half-collapse into the broth, which is the correct and intended outcome."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Okonomiyaki","target":"japan-okonomiyaki","emoji":"🥞"},{"name":"Tempura","target":"japan-tempura","emoji":"🍤"},{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🍲"}]},
{"id":"japan-zaru-soba","name":"Zaru Soba","nameAlt":"Cold buckwheat noodles with a dipping broth","aliases":["Zaru Soba","Cold Soba","Mori Soba","Buckwheat Noodles","Seiro Soba"],"course":"main","type":["main","noodles","cold","summer","quick"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","summer","quick","lunch"],"ingredients":"100g soba noodles · 120ml dashi · 30ml Japanese soy sauce · 30ml mirin · 1/4 sheet nori · 1 spring onion, sliced · 5g wasabi · 30g daikon, finely grated","method":"Make the dipping broth first and let it cool, because a warm tsuyu on cold noodles is the single most common way this dish is spoiled. Put the dashi, soy sauce and mirin into a small pan — that is a four-to-one-to-one ratio and it is worth remembering, because it scales to any quantity and it is the backbone of a dozen other dishes. Bring it just to a simmer, no further, and hold it there for thirty seconds. You are not reducing it; you are burning off the alcohol in the mirin, which otherwise sits sharp and slightly medicinal against cold buckwheat. Take it off, pour it into a jug and get it properly cold — the fridge for half an hour, or the freezer for ten minutes if you are in a hurry. Tsuyu for cold soba should be noticeably stronger and saltier than you would want to drink, because it is a dip and not a soup, and it will be diluted twice: once by the water clinging to the noodles, and again at the very end by the soba-yu. Season it now for that, not for how it tastes on a spoon. Now boil the noodles, and here is the first law, which trips up everyone who learned to cook pasta: PUT NO SALT IN THE WATER. None at all. This is not a stylistic preference. Soba dough itself is unsalted, unlike almost every Italian pasta and unlike udon, which is made with a heavily salted dough — so there is no salt gradient to protect and nothing to be drawn out. But the real reason is what happens to the water. Buckwheat sheds a great deal of starch as it cooks and the pot turns cloudy and pale grey, and that cloudy water is not waste: it is soba-yu, and you are going to drink it at the end of the meal. Salt it and you have thrown that away before you began. Use a genuinely large pot with far more water than looks necessary — at least two litres for a single portion — because a small pot goes gluey and the noodles clump the moment they go in. Bring it to a hard rolling boil, drop the soba in, fan it out with chopsticks so no two strands are touching, and stir once. Dried soba usually takes four to five minutes and fresh takes ninety seconds; go by the packet, and start tasting a full minute before it says. Soba wants a definite bite at the centre and it goes from correct to soft in about twenty seconds, faster than any wheat noodle, because there is very little gluten holding it together. When the water threatens to boil over, and it will, add a splash of cold water rather than turning the heat down — this drops the temperature at the surface without stopping the roiling that keeps the strands separate. The moment they are done, drain them, and RESERVE THE COOKING WATER in a jug or a small teapot. Do not pour it away. Now the second law, and the one that transforms the dish: WASH THEM IN COLD WATER AND RUB THEM UNTIL THEY SQUEAK. Tip the drained noodles into a bowl under a running cold tap, or into a bowl of cold water changed two or three times, and work them with your hands — really work them, gathering them up and rubbing the strands against each other between your palms as though you were washing a piece of cloth. Cloudy grey water will pour off them. Keep going, changing the water, until it runs almost clear and, more importantly, until the noodles change feel: they go from slippery and slightly tacky to genuinely squeaking against your fingers. That squeak is the finish line and it is unmistakable once you have felt it. The surface starch you have just removed is what makes badly made soba gummy, what glues the strands into a single mass on the plate, and what dulls the flavour by coating every strand in paste; underneath it, buckwheat is nutty and clean and faintly sweet, and washing is the only way to reach it. Finish with thirty seconds in a bowl of ice water to firm the strands completely, then drain them very thoroughly and shake them dry — water clinging to the noodles will flood your carefully strong tsuyu and make it insipid. Serve them piled on a zaru, which is the flat bamboo draining tray the dish is named for, or on any plate or rack that lets the last of the water fall away rather than pooling underneath. Shred the nori into fine strips with scissors and scatter it over the top at the last possible moment; nori is dry and crisp and turns to slime within minutes of meeting damp noodles. Put the cold tsuyu in a small deep cup, and the spring onion, wasabi and grated daikon in little dishes alongside so each person can add them to their own cup. Eat by lifting a small bundle of noodles, dipping only the bottom third into the tsuyu, and taking them in one go. Dipping the whole bundle drowns them; the restraint is the point, and slurping is genuine practice rather than a stereotype, because drawing air in with cold noodles is what carries the buckwheat aroma up into the nose. And now the third law, which is the part most people outside Japan never learn: KEEP THE SOBA-YU AND DRINK IT. When the noodles are finished, pour the reserved cooking water into whatever tsuyu is left in your cup, stir, and drink it like a broth. It is cloudy, faintly nutty, and it turns the concentrated leftover dip into exactly the right strength of light soup. It is also the reason the dish ends rather than simply stopping, and in a soba restaurant a small lacquered pot of it arrives at your table unasked.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 5 min · Chill 30 min","kcal":"~410 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 15g, Carbs 78g, Fat 2g, Sodium ~1450mg","storage":"Cooked soba does not keep well and this is genuinely a dish to make and eat. Washed and drained noodles hold for a few hours covered in the fridge, but they firm and dry at the edges; a fast rinse in cold water loosens them again. Do not dress or top them until serving. The tsuyu is the opposite — it keeps a fortnight in a sealed jar in the fridge and improves over the first few days, so it is well worth making a large batch, and the same four-to-one-to-one mixture diluted with three parts dashi becomes the broth for hot noodles. Soba-yu does not keep at all and is drunk at the meal. Grated daikon loses its bite within an hour of grating, so do it last.","chefNotes":"No salt in the water, ever — soba dough is unsalted and the cloudy cooking water is soba-yu, which you drink at the end. Then wash the boiled noodles in cold water and rub them between your hands until they audibly squeak; that surface starch is the whole difference between gummy soba and clean, nutty soba.","pairsWith":"Tempura, which is the classic partner and for good reason — hot, oily and crisp against cold, clean and lean is the whole appeal, and a couple of prawns or a few vegetable pieces on the side turns this into a full meal. Otherwise keep the table light: a small dish of blanched greens in sesame, or pickles. Cold barley tea, or a dry sake served chilled.","trivia":"Nearly every culture with a long noodle reads it as long life and takes care not to break it. Soba does the opposite, and that is the point of it. Toshikoshi soba, the bowl eaten on the last night of the year, is chosen precisely because buckwheat noodles are fragile and snap easily — the breaking is the symbolism, severing the hardships and misfortunes of the year that is ending so that none of them follow you across midnight. It is one of the very few noodle traditions in the world that celebrates a noodle falling apart, and it is still so widely observed that Japanese supermarkets sell out of soba on the thirty-first of December. There is a second custom built on a pun. Hikkoshi soba was what you gave your new neighbours when you moved into a district, because soba sounds like the word for nearby or alongside — a bowl of noodles that says, in effect, I live beside you now. The habit has largely faded, but it survives in the language. The plant itself is the real surprise. Buckwheat is not wheat and is not a cereal at all: it is not a grass, it belongs to the family that contains rhubarb and sorrel, and what is milled is a seed rather than a grain. That is why it contains no gluten, and why almost all soba you can buy is not pure buckwheat but nihachi soba, the two-eight ratio, eighty percent buckwheat cut with twenty percent wheat flour purely so the dough will hold together at all. Pure ten-wari soba exists, costs a great deal more, and is famously difficult to make and even harder to keep intact — which brings the whole thing neatly back to why it is what you eat when you want a year to break cleanly.","howThisFeels":"The moment under the cold tap when the noodles stop being slippery and start to squeak against your palms, and you can feel the starch actually let go.","leftovers":["(easy) Leftover tsuyu is the most useful thing here and it improves in the fridge. Diluted with three parts dashi it becomes the broth for hot noodles; used neat it is a dipping sauce for tempura or a dressing for cold blanched vegetables; a spoonful stirred into a stir-fry at the end seasons the whole pan.","(easy) Cold cooked soba, rinsed loose, makes an excellent salad rather than a sad one — toss it with sesame oil, rice vinegar, the leftover spring onion and any raw vegetable cut into fine strips, and it holds up all day in a lunchbox in a way that wheat noodles do not.","(creative) Pan-fry a nest of leftover soba in a little oil, pressing it flat and leaving it alone until the underside forms a crisp golden cake, then flip it. Top with a fried egg and a spoon of the tsuyu and you have a completely different and rather better meal than the one you started with."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Kake Soba — Hot Broth, Nothing On It (Budget · Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":19,"howThisFeels":"Plain to the point of austerity, steam coming off a pale brown bowl, and somehow the version you go back to on a cold night.","trivia":"Kake means simply poured over, and this is soba stripped to its absolute minimum: noodles, hot broth, a scatter of spring onion, nothing else. It is the cheapest thing on the menu in any soba shop and it is also the honest test of one, because there is nowhere for a bad broth or a badly washed noodle to hide. Standing soba bars on Japanese railway platforms, where a bowl is assembled in under a minute and eaten in three, exist almost entirely on the strength of this version. Worth knowing for the vegan route: ordinary dashi and every instant granule carry bonito, so the kombu and shiitake version is not a refinement here but a requirement — and it is the older method anyway, since dried kelp and dried mushrooms both predate the industrial bonito flake by centuries.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"5g wasabi"},{"item":"30g daikon, finely grated"},{"item":"1/4 sheet nori"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"120ml dashi","to":"350ml kombu and shiitake dashi"}],"addStep":[{"text":"The broth changes from a dip to a soup, so the concentration must come right down: use the full 350ml of kombu and shiitake dashi against the same 30ml each of soy and mirin, which takes you from four-to-one-to-one to roughly twelve-to-one-to-one. It should taste like something you would happily drink a bowl of, because that is exactly what you are about to do. Make the dashi by steeping a 10cm piece of dried kombu and two or three dried shiitake in cold water for at least four hours or overnight, then warming it to just below a simmer and lifting the kombu out before it boils — boiled kelp turns slippery and bitter. Serve the noodles hot rather than washed cold: boil them exactly as before, rinse and rub them under cold water to get the starch off, then dip the drained noodles back into the hot pot for ten seconds to reheat before they go into the bowl. The washing step is not optional just because the dish is hot; skipping it clouds the broth and makes it gluey. Pour the hot broth over, scatter the spring onion, and eat it immediately."}]}},{"name":"Classic Zaru Soba","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":26},{"name":"Kamo Nanban — Duck and Leek","icon":"🦆","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":41,"howThisFeels":"Duck fat spreading across the surface of the broth in gold coins, and the leeks coming out of the pan almost sweet.","trivia":"Nanban means southern barbarian, which was the Edo-period term for Portuguese and Spanish traders, and it attached itself in the kitchen to anything involving alliums cooked in fat — a technique Japanese cooks associated with those foreigners, who ate onions and leeks in quantities that seemed remarkable at the time. So the name of this dish records a sixteenth-century first impression of European cooking, preserved in a noodle bowl. Duck and soba is one of the oldest pairings in the repertoire and the reasoning is straightforward once you cook it: buckwheat is lean, faintly bitter and clean, and duck is the richest of the common poultry, so the fat rendered into the broth does the work that a pork bone does in ramen without any of the hours. The leek is not a garnish either — scorched in duck fat, its sweetness is what balances the soy.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"80g duck breast, skin on"},{"item":"60g leek, in 3cm lengths"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"120ml dashi","to":"400ml dashi"}],"addStep":[{"text":"This one is served hot, so build the broth as a soup: the full 400ml of dashi against the same 30ml each of soy and mirin. Score the duck skin in a criss-cross without cutting into the meat, salt it, and lay it skin-side down in a cold dry pan. Bring the heat up slowly to medium — starting cold is what renders the fat out properly instead of seizing the skin and trapping it — and give it six to eight minutes until the skin is deep brown and crisp and there is a pool of clear fat in the pan. Turn it, give it ninety seconds on the meat side only, and lift it out to rest; duck breast wants to stay pink and it will carry on cooking as it sits. Now put the leeks into that rendered duck fat and let them colour hard, three or four minutes, until they are properly scorched at the edges and collapsing — this is where the sweetness comes from and pale leeks will taste of nothing. Pour the broth into the same pan to lift everything off the base, and simmer it two minutes. Slice the rested duck thinly across the grain. Boil, wash and rub the noodles exactly as in the main method, reheat them briefly in hot water, and put them in the bowl; pour the leeks and broth over, lay the duck slices on top so they warm through without cooking further, and finish with the spring onion. Skip the wasabi, which fights the duck."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Tempura","target":"japan-tempura","emoji":"🍤"},{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🍲"},{"name":"Shoyu Ramen","target":"japan-shoyu-ramen","emoji":"🍜"}]},
{"id":"japan-nikujaga","name":"Nikujaga","nameAlt":"Meat and potatoes, simmered under a drop lid","aliases":["Nikujaga","Niku Jaga","Japanese Beef and Potato Stew","Meat and Potato Simmer"],"course":"main","type":["main","stew","comfort","one-pot","winter"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","winter","budget"],"ingredients":"120g beef chuck, thinly sliced · 300g waxy potato, in 4cm chunks · 120g onion, cut into wedges · 60g carrot, in rough chunks · 60g shirataki noodles, drained · 300ml dashi · 15g white sugar · 20ml mirin · 30ml Japanese soy sauce · 10ml neutral oil · 30g green beans, halved","method":"The name tells you the priority and almost everybody gets it backwards. Niku is meat and jaga is potato, and it is tempting to read that as a beef stew with potatoes in it. It is not. This is a potato dish that happens to contain a little beef, the beef is there to season the potatoes rather than the other way round, and the whole recipe makes sense the moment you accept that the 120g of meat is a flavouring for 300g of potato. Cook it as a beef stew and you will simmer the beef to death trying to make it the star. Choose waxy potatoes and this matters more here than in almost any other dish. A floury potato — the kind you want for roasting or mashing — disintegrates in a shallow simmer and turns the liquid into a thick beige sludge with lumps in it. A waxy potato holds its shape through forty minutes and absorbs the broth without collapsing, which is the entire texture of nikujaga: chunks that keep their edges but have gone amber and translucent all the way to the middle. Peel them, cut them into generous 4cm chunks, and round off the sharp corners with your peeler if you are feeling careful — those corners are the first thing to break off and cloud the pot, and the technique of trimming them has a name in Japanese kitchens because it matters that much. Rinse the cut potatoes under cold water for thirty seconds to wash off loose surface starch, then drain them well. Prepare the shirataki: they come packed in liquid that smells faintly mineral, so drain them, rinse them, and give them a minute in boiling water before draining again. They are made from konjac, the same fibrous corm behind the springy filling in the older kind of Osaka street food, and in a simmered dish they are prized because they absorb broth greedily while contributing no flavour and almost no calories of their own. Cut them roughly with scissors so they are not one continuous tangle. Now heat the oil in a wide, heavy pot over a medium-high heat and sear the beef, spread out in a single layer, for barely a minute — you want colour on the surface and nothing more. And here is the first law: TAKE THE BEEF OUT AGAIN. Lift it onto a plate the moment it has lost its raw colour and set it aside. Thinly sliced beef simmered for half an hour goes grey, stringy and tough, and it gives up everything it had to the broth while keeping none of it; the point of searing it first is to leave the browned fat and juices in the pot, where they will season the potatoes for the whole cook. The beef comes back at the very end, for two minutes, and that discipline is the difference between nikujaga and a pot of sad grey meat. Add the onion wedges to the fat left in the pot and cook them for two minutes until they are translucent at the edges but still holding together, then add the potato, carrot and shirataki and turn everything over in the fat for another minute. Pour in the dashi — it should come roughly two-thirds of the way up the ingredients, not cover them, because this is a shallow braise rather than a boil and the liquid is meant to reduce onto the vegetables rather than dilute them. Bring it to a simmer and skim off any foam that rises in the first few minutes. Now the seasoning, and the second law, which is the one worth carrying into every other Japanese dish you ever cook: SUGAR GOES IN BEFORE SOY, AND THE ORDER IS NOT NEGOTIABLE. Add the sugar and the mirin first, on their own, and let them simmer for three or four minutes before the soy sauce goes anywhere near the pot. There is real chemistry behind this and it is explained in the note below, but the short version is that sugar is a large, slow molecule and salt is a small, fast one; if the soy sauce goes in first the salt races into the potato, firms its surface and draws water outwards, and the sugar can then never get in. You end up with vegetables that are salty on the outside and bland in the middle. Sugar first, given a few minutes alone, and it penetrates properly; the soy follows and seasons everything on top of that sweetness. Now add the soy sauce, and put on the drop lid — the third law, and the tool that makes this style of cooking work at all: USE AN OTOSHIBUTA. An otoshibuta is a lid that sits directly ON the food and the liquid rather than on the rim of the pot, and it solves a very specific problem. With the liquid only two-thirds of the way up, the exposed tops of the potatoes would cook in steam while their bottoms cook in broth, and the obvious fix — stirring — is exactly what you must not do, because stirring breaks waxy potato chunks and clouds the pot. The drop lid makes the simmering liquid rise around its edges, wash across the top of the ingredients and fall back, so everything is continuously bathed without a spoon ever going in. It also holds the pieces down so they do not knock against each other. A traditional one is a wooden disc, but a circle of baking paper cut to the width of the pot with a small hole in the centre works perfectly, and so does a smaller pan lid laid straight onto the food. Simmer gently under the drop lid for twenty to twenty-five minutes, until a skewer slides into the potato with no resistance and the liquid has reduced by about half. Do not stir at any point; if you must move things, take the pot by both handles and shake it once. In the last three minutes, add the green beans and return the beef to the pot, tucking it in among the vegetables so it warms through and takes on the glaze without cooking further. Take the drop lid off for the final couple of minutes to let the liquid tighten. Turn the heat off and — if you have the time and this is the single best thing you can do for it — LET IT SIT FOR AT LEAST TWENTY MINUTES BEFORE SERVING, or make it several hours ahead. Nikujaga absorbs as it cools, not as it cooks: the potatoes draw the broth into themselves while the temperature drops, and a bowl eaten straight off the heat is noticeably less seasoned than the same pot left to stand and gently reheated. It is one of very few dishes that is genuinely better the second day. Serve it warm rather than hot, in a bowl, with the remaining broth spooned over and plain rice alongside.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 30 min · Rest 20 min","kcal":"~480 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 26g, Carbs 58g, Fat 15g, Sodium ~1350mg","storage":"This is one of the rare dishes that genuinely improves overnight, because the potatoes keep drawing broth into themselves as they sit — make it a day ahead if you can. It keeps three days covered in the fridge and reheats gently on a low heat or in short bursts in a microwave; add a splash of dashi or water, since it thickens considerably as it stands. It does not freeze well: waxy potato turns grainy and slightly hollow after freezing, and the shirataki go rubbery. If you want to freeze a portion, lift the potatoes out first and freeze only the meat and broth.","chefNotes":"Sear the thin beef, then take it straight back out and only return it in the last two minutes — simmered for half an hour it goes grey and stringy for nothing. And use a drop lid so the shallow liquid washes over everything without you ever stirring, because a spoon in this pot breaks the potatoes.","pairsWith":"Plain short-grain rice and a bowl of miso soup, which is the standard and complete Japanese home dinner around this dish. Something sharp on the side stops it becoming heavy — pickled cucumber, or blanched greens dressed in sesame. Beer or a cup of warm barley tea; a light red wine works surprisingly well against the sugar and soy.","trivia":"There is a mnemonic every Japanese home cook learns, and this dish is the clearest demonstration of it: sa-shi-su-se-so. The five syllables are the order in which seasonings go into a pot — sa for satō, sugar; shi for shio, salt; su for vinegar; se for shōyu, soy sauce, spelled with the old se character; and so for miso. It looks like a piece of folk memory and it is in fact a piece of food science compressed into a nursery rhyme. Sucrose is a large molecule and it diffuses into a piece of potato or a piece of meat very slowly. Sodium chloride is a much smaller one and moves several times faster, and while it moves in, it also draws water out and firms the proteins and cell walls at the surface. Put the salt or the soy in first and you effectively close the door: the surface tightens, water flows outward against the direction you want sugar to travel, and the sweetness never reaches the middle no matter how long you simmer. Sugar first, alone, for a few minutes, and it gets all the way in — then everything else follows. The vinegar, soy and miso come last for a different reason again, which is that their aromatics are volatile and boil away, so they go in late to be tasted rather than early to be lost. The order also quietly explains why this dish tastes the way it does at all. Nikujaga is not spiced and contains almost nothing: dashi, sugar, mirin, soy. Everything it has comes from getting those four into the potato in the right sequence. It has a reputation to match — for decades it was the dish that turned up at the top of magazine polls as the home cooking people most associate with a mother's kitchen, ofukuro no aji, the taste of the person who raised you, and it became such a cliché of domestic competence that it was half a joke by the nineteen-nineties. Two former naval port towns, Maizuru and Kure, still argue publicly over which of them invented it, each with municipal signage to press the claim.","howThisFeels":"Lifting the drop lid after twenty minutes and finding every potato gone amber right through to the middle, still square at the edges, not one of them broken.","leftovers":["(easy) Genuinely better the next day, so this barely counts as leftovers — reheat it gently with a splash of dashi to loosen it, since it thickens overnight as the potatoes keep drinking.","(easy) Mash the leftover potatoes roughly with any meat and vegetables still in the pot, let the mixture cool completely so it firms up, then shape it into patties, crumb them and shallow-fry until deep gold. This is the standard second life for nikujaga in a Japanese kitchen and it is arguably better than the first.","(creative) Thin what is left with extra dashi, bring it to a simmer, and slide in a beaten egg in a thin stream around the edges of the pot without stirring — you get a soft-set egg over the whole surface and a completely different bowl, closer to a rice-topper than a stew, especially good spooned over hot rice."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Butajaga — Pork, the Eastern Version (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":27,"howThisFeels":"Softer and rounder than the beef version, the fat going sweet against the sugar rather than savoury against it.","trivia":"This is not a downgrade, it is a regional line on a map. Ask for nikujaga in Kansai — Osaka, Kyoto, Kobe — and it arrives with beef, because western Japan has been a beef-eating region for as long as Japan has eaten beef at all. Ask in Kanto, around Tokyo, and a great many households will make exactly the same dish with thinly sliced pork belly and call it nikujaga without a second thought, though butajaga is the name when anyone wants to be precise. The split runs through other dishes too and is old enough that meat-supply companies still describe the country as divided along it. Pork is the cheaper route almost everywhere, and in this particular dish it is arguably the better one: pork belly renders more fat into the broth than lean sliced beef does, and that fat is what carries the sugar and soy into the potato.","delta":{"swapIng":[{"from":"120g beef chuck, thinly sliced","to":"120g pork belly, thinly sliced"}]}},{"name":"Classic Nikujaga","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":34},{"name":"Vegan Nikujaga — Shiitake and Kombu","icon":"🌱","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"Cleaner and somehow deeper, and the mushrooms come out of the pot tasting more like meat than the meat did.","trivia":"Take the beef out of nikujaga and you are left needing two things it was providing: fat, and glutamates. The mushrooms handle the second and handle it emphatically, because dried shiitake are one of the richest natural sources of guanylate in any kitchen — and guanylate multiplies rather than adds when it meets the glutamate in kombu, which is precisely why kombu-and-shiitake dashi tastes so much more savoury than either ingredient suggests it should. That combination is also the oldest broth in Japan and long predates the bonito flake, so this is not a modern adaptation of the dish so much as a return to an older larder. It matters here for a practical reason too: ordinary dashi and every instant granule carry bonito, so the record's default version is not vegan and this fork is not optional dressing.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"120g beef chuck, thinly sliced"}],"addIng":[{"item":"40g dried shiitake mushrooms"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"300ml dashi","to":"300ml kombu and shiitake dashi"},{"from":"300g waxy potato, in 4cm chunks","to":"350g waxy potato, in 4cm chunks"},{"from":"10ml neutral oil","to":"20ml neutral oil"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Soak the dried shiitake in the cold water you are going to make the dashi from — at least four hours, or overnight in the fridge — and then use that soaking liquid as the dashi itself, together with a 10cm piece of dried kombu steeped alongside. Never throw the soaking water away; it is the single most valuable thing in this version and it is where the savoury depth comes from. Warm it to just below a simmer and lift the kombu out before it boils, since boiled kelp turns slippery and faintly bitter. Squeeze the rehydrated mushrooms out, trim off the woody stems, and slice the caps thickly. There is no beef to sear, so start by frying the mushrooms hard in the doubled oil for three or four minutes until the edges catch and colour — that browning is doing the job the seared beef did, and skipping it leaves the whole pot tasting flat and watery. Take the mushrooms out exactly as you would the beef and return them in the final three minutes, or they go slack. Everything else, including the sugar-before-soy order and the drop lid, is unchanged."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Kare Raisu","target":"japan-kare-raisu","emoji":"🍛"},{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🍲"},{"name":"Goma-ae","target":"japan-goma-ae","emoji":"🥬"}]},
{"id":"japan-korokke","name":"Korokke","nameAlt":"The croquette that became a Japanese street food","aliases":["Korokke","Japanese Croquette","Japanese Potato Croquette","Beef Korokke","Kroket"],"course":"starter","type":["starter","snack","fried","street-food","budget"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","party","budget"],"ingredients":"200g floury potato, in 4cm chunks · 60g beef mince · 40g onion, finely chopped · 8g butter · 15ml milk · 5ml soy sauce · 2g fine salt · 1g black pepper · 20g cake flour · 1 egg, beaten · 45g panko breadcrumbs · 500ml sunflower oil · 40g cabbage, shredded as fine as hair · 10ml tonkatsu sauce","method":"Everything about a good korokke comes down to one decision made before you cook anything, so make it deliberately: THIS IS THE ONE JAPANESE POTATO DISH THAT WANTS A FLOURY POTATO. Nikujaga wants waxy potatoes that hold their edges; korokke wants the opposite. You are trying to build a filling that is dry, fluffy and slightly mealy, because a wet filling turns to steam in hot oil, the steam has nowhere to go, and the croquette bursts. Nearly every korokke failure in a home kitchen is a moisture failure. Peel the potatoes, cut them into even 4cm chunks so they cook at the same rate, and boil them in salted water until a skewer slides in with no resistance at all — around fifteen to eighteen minutes. Undercooked potato leaves lumps that break the crumb coating from the inside. Drain them properly, then return the empty pot to a low heat, tip the potatoes back in and shake the pan for a full minute. You will see the surfaces go chalky and matte as the residual water steams off. That minute is not optional and it is the single most useful thing in this recipe. Mash them while they are still hot, because cold potato goes gluey the moment you work it, and mash coarsely — leave some texture. A completely smooth purée eats like wallpaper paste inside a crumb. Beat in the butter and the milk while the mash is hot so they are absorbed rather than sitting in pockets, then set it aside to cool. Meanwhile fry the onion and the beef. Get a pan properly hot, add a film of oil, and cook the onion for three or four minutes until it is soft and just beginning to take colour at the edges — colour here is sweetness, and it is most of the flavour of the finished thing. Add the mince, break it up, and fry it hard until it is browned and the liquid it releases has completely cooked away, which usually takes longer than people expect: keep going until the pan is sizzling rather than simmering. WET MINCE IS THE SECOND MOISTURE FAILURE. Season with the soy sauce, the salt and a good amount of black pepper, then let the mixture cool. Fold the meat into the mash and, now, THE STEP EVERYONE SKIPS: CHILL THE MIXTURE PROPERLY. At least an hour in the fridge, uncovered for the first twenty minutes so steam escapes rather than condensing back into it. Warm filling is soft filling, soft filling cannot be shaped tightly, and loosely shaped filling has air pockets that expand in the fryer and split the crust. If you have the time, chill the shaped croquettes too. Divide the cold mixture in two and shape each half into the flat oval with slightly domed faces that is the classic Japanese shape — press firmly as you go, compacting rather than moulding, and slap each one gently from palm to palm a few times to knock out trapped air. Now bread them in the standard three-stage order, and use one hand for the dry stations and the other for the egg, or you will end up with breadcrumb gloves. Dust with flour first and pat off every loose speck, because a thick flour layer turns to paste and slides the coating off in the oil. Then the beaten egg, letting the excess run off. Then the panko, pressing it on firmly all over including the edges, which is where splits start. Panko rather than ordinary breadcrumbs matters: it is made from a crustless bread baked electrically rather than in a hot-walled oven, so it dries into long jagged flakes rather than sandy grains. Those flakes absorb less oil and stay crisp far longer, which is the entire texture you are chasing. Heat the oil to 180°C. If you have no thermometer, drop in a pinch of panko: it should sink very slightly and rise immediately in a steady bubble, browning in about thirty seconds. Below 170°C the coating drinks oil and goes greasy; above 190°C it darkens before the inside is hot. Fry two at a time so the temperature does not crash, for two to three minutes, turning once, until they are the deep even gold of a biscuit. THE FILLING IS ALREADY COOKED — you are only crisping a shell and warming a middle, so the moment the colour is right, they are done. Lift them onto a rack, never onto paper, because a korokke resting on absorbent paper steams its own bottom soft within a minute. Serve them hot with a mound of raw cabbage shredded as finely as you can manage and a squiggle of tonkatsu sauce. The cabbage is not garnish; the sweetness and the crunch of raw brassica against hot fried starch is why the plate works.","cookTime":"Prep 25 min · Chill 60 min · Cook 15 min","kcal":"~470 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 17g, Carbs 52g, Fat 22g, Sodium ~780mg","storage":"Uncooked, shaped and crumbed korokke are the real prize: open-freeze them on a tray until solid, then bag them, and fry from frozen at a slightly lower 170°C for four to five minutes so the middle warms before the crust darkens. They keep three months and are better than anything you can buy. Cooked korokke keep two days in the fridge but the crust softens; revive them in a hot oven or air fryer for six or seven minutes rather than a microwave, which turns the panko to sponge. Do not freeze them already fried — you get the worst of both, a crust that has been soft twice.","chefNotes":"Dry the mash out in the hot empty pot for a full minute after draining, and chill the filling before you shape it. Every burst korokke in history was either too wet or too warm.","pairsWith":"Finely shredded raw cabbage is not optional in Japan and should not be here either — it cuts the fat and resets the palate between bites. Rice and a bowl of miso soup make it a full meal; a cold beer is the standard drink. It is also famously good pushed into a soft white roll with sauce as a korokke sandwich, which is exactly as good an idea as it sounds.","trivia":"Korokke has been made famous three separate times, and not once by a cook. The first was a song. In 1917 a comic number called Korokke no Uta became a nationwide hit — the joke being a wife who serves her husband croquettes every single day — and the dish went from an expensive restaurant curiosity to something ordinary households wanted to try. The second was a school lunch. In 1953 frozen korokke were developed for the national school meal programme, which required industrial-scale production, which in turn made the freezer aisle version cheap and everywhere, and quietly established the habit of buying korokke rather than making them. The third happened on the internet. In the early 2000s somebody on the message board 2channel mentioned they were laying in korokke ahead of an approaching typhoon; the remark caught, and buying korokke before a big storm is now a genuine and widely observed Japanese social-media ritual, complete with supermarkets running features on it. Three accidents of media, none of them culinary. The dish itself arrived from France, where a croquette is bound with béchamel, and it lost the béchamel almost immediately: sauce made from butter and milk was an expensive proposition in a country that had barely any dairy industry, while potatoes were cheap and grew everywhere. What began as a substitution turned out to be a better idea, because a potato croquette holds its shape at room temperature far better than a cream one does, which is precisely why korokke and not croquette became the thing sold at butcher's counters, packed into lunch boxes and eaten standing on the street.","howThisFeels":"The crack of the crust as the first one gives way, and the small cloud of steam that comes off a properly dried mash.","leftovers":["(easy) Split a leftover korokke, flatten it slightly, and put it inside a soft white roll with shredded cabbage and plenty of sauce. This is a real thing sold in Japanese bakeries and it is arguably the best possible use of a day-old one.","(easy) Crumble the leftovers into a pan with a beaten egg, a splash of dashi and a sliced onion, cook until the egg is just set, and slide the whole soft mess over hot rice. The crust dissolves into the sauce and thickens it.","(creative) Break them into rough chunks, warm them through in a curry sauce and serve over rice — the crumb thickens the sauce and the potato drinks it, which is why curry korokke is on every Japanese menu that sells both."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Yasai Korokke — All Vegetable (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":20,"howThisFeels":"Sweeter and lighter than you expect, and the flecks of carrot and pea make it look like something from a lunch box.","trivia":"This is the wartime version and it has a name that tells you exactly how it was regarded: korokke-modoki, mock korokke, printed in newspaper recipe columns in 1944 when meat had essentially vanished from ordinary shops. Cooks bulked the mash out with pumpkin for sweetness and body, and the versions that survived the shortage did so on merit rather than nostalgia. It is also the easiest korokke to make properly, because the thing that makes vegetable korokke burst — wet vegetables — is much easier to control than wet mince. Use the egg-free coating below and the whole thing is vegan without any substitution or apology.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"60g beef mince"},{"item":"8g butter"},{"item":"15ml milk"},{"item":"1 egg, beaten"}],"addIng":[{"item":"30g carrot, in 5mm dice"},{"item":"20g peas"},{"item":"60ml water, for the coating slurry"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"200g floury potato, in 4cm chunks","to":"260g floury potato, in 4cm chunks"},{"from":"20g cake flour","to":"35g cake flour"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Boil the diced carrot with the potato for the last six minutes so it softens without going to mush, and drop the peas in for the final minute. Dry the mash in the hot empty pot exactly as in the main method — this version needs it more, not less, because carrot and peas both carry water. There is no butter and no milk, so mash while very hot and beat hard for thirty seconds; the starch alone will bind it once it cools. Season more assertively than you think you need to, since there is no meat carrying salt. For the coating, whisk the extra flour with the water into a smooth batter about the thickness of pouring cream and use it exactly where the egg would go: potato, dry flour dust, then the batter, then panko. It grips better than egg does and browns a shade lighter, which is worth knowing even when you are not cooking vegan."}]}},{"name":"Classic Beef Korokke","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30},{"name":"Curry Korokke","icon":"🍛","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":33,"howThisFeels":"The smell hits before the plate does, and the first bite is warmer in the throat than the crust lets on.","trivia":"Every Japanese kitchen that has made a pot of curry has, within two days, made curry korokke, and this is the most efficient recipe in the country for a very specific reason: Japanese curry is thickened with a flour-and-fat roux rather than reduced, so a leftover pot sets almost solid in the fridge. Solid curry is exactly what a korokke filling wants, where a thin sauce would be a disaster. The result is a dish that exists because of the physics of the previous meal. Add the curry cold, straight from the fridge, and do not warm it to make it easier to mix — you will be putting the moisture back that the fridge took out.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"8g curry powder"},{"item":"6g butter, extra"},{"item":"6g cake flour, extra"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"5ml soy sauce","to":"3ml soy sauce"}],"addStep":[{"text":"There are two routes here and the better one costs nothing. IF YOU HAVE LEFTOVER JAPANESE CURRY IN THE FRIDGE, use about 70g of it and skip the curry powder, extra butter and extra flour entirely — that is what this version was invented for. Take it straight from the fridge, where it will have set to something closer to a paste than a sauce, and chop any large pieces of meat and vegetable small so the filling shapes cleanly. Do not warm it to make it easier to mix; you would be putting back exactly the moisture the fridge took out. Otherwise build the curry from scratch, which takes four minutes: melt the extra butter in the pan once the beef and onion are done, stir in the extra flour and cook it gently for two minutes until it smells of biscuits rather than raw flour, then TAKE THE PAN OFF THE HEAT BEFORE THE CURRY POWDER GOES IN. Spices scorch in hot fat in seconds and burnt curry powder is bitter through the whole batch. Stir it in off the heat, then back on a low flame for thirty seconds to wake it up. Either way, fold the curry into the cooled mash last, with a light hand — over-mixing turns the whole thing an even brown and you want streaks. Because curry is already salted and spiced, cut the soy sauce back and taste before adding any more salt. Chill the mixture for the full hour, and shape these a touch smaller than usual: curry filling is softer than plain mash and a large one is more likely to split. Serve with sauce as usual, or with nothing at all — there is enough seasoning inside."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Nikujaga","target":"japan-nikujaga","emoji":"🥔"},{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🐖"},{"name":"Kare Raisu","target":"japan-kare-raisu","emoji":"🍛"}]},
{"id":"japan-kinpira-gobo","name":"Kinpira Gobo","nameAlt":"Root vegetables cut to matchsticks and cooked twice","aliases":["Kinpira Gobo","Kimpira Gobo","Kinpira","Braised Burdock","Japanese Braised Root Vegetables"],"course":"side","type":["side","vegetables","quick","make-ahead","budget"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","lunchbox","budget"],"ingredients":"70g carrot, cut into fine matchsticks · 70g parsnip, cut into fine matchsticks · 5ml sesame oil · 10ml Japanese soy sauce · 8ml mirin · 5ml sake · 5g white sugar · 1 dried red chilli, deseeded and sliced into rings · 3g toasted sesame seeds","method":"Kinpira is not a recipe, it is a two-stage technique, and once you have it you own about fifteen side dishes rather than one. The word describes a specific sequence: fry hard in oil first, then season and let the seasoning cook away to nothing. Everything else is which vegetable you happened to have. THE CUT IS THE DISH, so give it the time it deserves. You want matchsticks around 5cm long and no thicker than 3mm, all roughly the same, because thickness decides how much crunch survives and mismatched sticks give you some raw and some collapsed in the same mouthful. In Japan the traditional cut for burdock is sasagaki — you hold the root like a pencil and shave it away from you with a knife, turning it as you go, so the shavings fall like sharpened pencil curls. It is worth learning, and it is much easier than it sounds, but a julienne on a mandolin or steady knife work is entirely honest here. Cut the carrot and the parsnip separately and keep them separate for now, because parsnip is softer and goes in later. Then, a step you cannot skip with parsnip and would not skip with burdock: DROP THE PARSNIP STICKS INTO COLD WATER FOR FIVE MINUTES. Both roots brown quickly once cut, and both carry a faint bitterness in that surface layer; a short soak takes the edge off and keeps the colour pale. Five minutes, no longer, or you leach out the sweetness you are cooking for. Drain them and dry them thoroughly on a cloth — wet vegetables steam instead of frying, and steamed kinpira is limp kinpira. Now heat the sesame oil in a wide frying pan over a medium-high heat, add the chilli rings, and give them fifteen seconds in the oil before anything else goes in. That fifteen seconds moves the heat from the chilli into the fat, where it can reach every strand of vegetable; add the chilli later and you get a dish that is bland in most bites and startling in one. Add the parsnip first and fry for two minutes, then the carrot, and keep everything moving for another two to three minutes. You are looking for the sticks to lose their raw squeak and go glossy while staying firm enough that they still rattle against the pan. Sesame oil is unusual as a frying medium because it burns at a much lower temperature than most oils and it is here for its aroma rather than its heat, so keep the pan moving and do not let it smoke. Now the seasoning, all at once: soy sauce, mirin, sake and sugar. It will hiss and instantly seem like far too little liquid to matter. That is correct, and it is the second half of the technique: KINPIRA IS FINISHED WHEN THE PAN IS DRY. Keep the heat up and keep turning the vegetables through the sauce for two to three minutes until the liquid has almost entirely gone and what is left clings to the sticks as a glaze rather than pooling underneath. If liquid remains in the pan the dish is a simmer, it will taste watery and salty at the same time, and it will not keep. Cook it out. The moment the pan goes dry and the sizzle changes pitch to something drier and sharper, take it off the heat. Scatter the sesame seeds over and turn everything once. Toast them yourself in a dry pan for a minute beforehand if they are not already toasted — the difference is not subtle. Serve it at room temperature rather than hot, in a small mound, in a bowl of its own. This is bento food and picnic food and fridge food, and it tastes better an hour after it is made than it does straight from the pan, because the glaze goes on soaking in as it cools. HONEST NOTE ON THE ROOT: the classic version uses gobo, burdock root, which is a long thin brown root that looks exactly like a stick and tastes earthy, faintly sweet and slightly nutty. It is not stocked in ordinary shops here, though Asian grocers sometimes have it fresh or frozen and it is worth grabbing if you see it — scrub it rather than peel it, since the flavour sits just under the skin, and soak the cut sticks for five minutes as above. Parsnip is the closest thing on an ordinary shelf: same shape of sweetness, same fibrous bite, browns the same way. Celery cut into sticks, or carrot alone, are both entirely traditional kinpira in their own right. The technique is the dish.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 8 min","kcal":"~150 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 2g, Carbs 20g, Fat 6g, Sodium ~620mg","storage":"This is one of the great make-ahead dishes and it is meant to be: because the pan is cooked completely dry, there is no liquid left to go sour, and it keeps happily for five days in a sealed container in the fridge. The flavour improves for the first two. Serve it cold or at room temperature straight from the container — reheating is unnecessary and softens the crunch. It freezes acceptably in a lunchbox portion for up to a month, though the carrot loses some of its bite.","chefNotes":"Cook it until the pan is completely dry. Kinpira with liquid left in it is a stir-fry that failed, and it will not keep the way a properly dried one does.","pairsWith":"It exists to sit next to rice, and a Japanese home dinner will often have a small bowl of this alongside a grilled fish or a piece of ginger pork. It is the standard corner-filler in a bento box, where it earns its place by being good cold. Anything rich wants it opposite — pork, fried food, a fatty fish. Green tea, or barley tea.","trivia":"The dish is named after a comic-book strongman, several centuries before comic books. Kinpira is Sakata no Kinpira, the hero of a rowdy sub-genre of Edo-period puppet theatre called kinpira-joruri: chanted, shamisen-backed stories of a superhumanly strong young man smashing his way through demons and giants. He was written as the son of Sakata no Kintoki, who is himself the historical seed of Kintaro, the folk-tale Golden Boy who wrestles bears. The plays were loud, violent and hugely popular, and the audiences were exactly the people who ate this dish. So when a stir-fry of burdock turned up — tough, fibrous, chewy, spiked with chilli, the sort of food that was believed to give you strength — somebody attached the strongman's name to it and it stuck for three hundred years. It is the eighteenth-century equivalent of naming a snack after a superhero, and it worked for the same reason. There is a second thing worth knowing about the original root, which is that almost nobody outside East Asia eats it. In Europe and America burdock is a weed — a tall thistly plant whose seed heads are covered in tiny hooked burs that catch on animal fur, which is where the plant gets its name and how it spreads. In 1941 a Swiss engineer named George de Mestral came back from a walk, looked at the burs stuck to his dog and his own trousers under a microscope, and spent the next decade turning what he saw into Velcro. The vegetable in this dish and the fastener on your shoes are the same plant.","howThisFeels":"The pan going quiet and dry all at once, and the smell of sesame oil and soy sauce arriving a second later.","leftovers":["(easy) Stir a couple of spoonfuls through hot rice with a little extra sesame — it seasons the whole bowl and turns plain rice into a meal on its own.","(easy) Pack it cold into a lunchbox next to anything fried. It is genuinely designed for this and it is better on day two than day one.","(creative) Fold it into an omelette or a rolled egg just before the last turn, so the matchsticks run through the layers. The sweetness of the egg and the salt of the glaze are a very old Japanese pairing and it looks striking cut across."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Kinpira Ninjin — All Carrot (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":9,"howThisFeels":"Almost shockingly orange in the bowl, and sweeter than a dish with this much soy sauce in it has any right to be.","trivia":"This is not a compromise version, it is a named dish in its own right and it turns up in bento boxes across Japan for a practical reason: carrot is the one root that is always in the house. Kinpira as a technique has been applied to lotus root, celery, bamboo shoots, potato, sweet potato, aubergine, pumpkin, radish skins and the tough outer leaves of cabbage — it is fundamentally a method for making something good out of a firm vegetable and four bottles from the cupboard, which is why it survived. Carrot alone leans sweet, so hold back a little on the sugar and be slightly braver with the chilli than you would be otherwise.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"70g parsnip, cut into fine matchsticks"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"70g carrot, cut into fine matchsticks","to":"140g carrot, cut into fine matchsticks"},{"from":"5g white sugar","to":"3g white sugar"}],"addStep":[{"text":"With only one vegetable there is no staggered timing — everything goes in together after the chilli has had its fifteen seconds in the oil. Carrot holds its crunch better than almost any other root, so give it a full four minutes of dry frying before the seasoning goes in, and let it take a little colour on the edges; those browned edges are where the flavour is. Because carrot brings its own sweetness, the sugar comes down and the finished glaze wants to be a touch saltier to balance it. Everything else, including cooking the pan completely dry, is unchanged."}]}},{"name":"Kinpira Gobo — Carrot and Parsnip","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":16},{"name":"Kinpira with Pork Belly","icon":"🥓","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":23,"howThisFeels":"Rendered pork fat catching in the glaze, so every strand of vegetable comes out shining.","trivia":"Adding thinly sliced pork to kinpira is standard practice rather than an invention, and it changes the dish from a side into something close to a main with rice. It also solves a small technical problem elegantly: sesame oil is a poor frying medium because it scorches at a low temperature, so cooks who want a harder, hotter fry render pork fat into the pan first and use that instead, adding the sesame oil only at the very end for aroma. The pork does the frying, the sesame oil does the smelling, and both jobs get done properly. It is worth stealing that trick for other dishes.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"60g pork belly, sliced very thin"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"5ml sesame oil","to":"3ml sesame oil"},{"from":"10ml Japanese soy sauce","to":"15ml Japanese soy sauce"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cut the pork into short strips about the length of the vegetable matchsticks. Start the pan dry over a medium heat, with no oil at all, and lay the pork in — it will stick for the first thirty seconds and then release itself as the fat renders, which is the signal to start moving it. Fry it for three or four minutes until the edges are properly browned and there is a decent pool of clear fat in the pan, then lift the pork out and set it aside. Add the chilli to the rendered pork fat and carry on with the method exactly as written, using that fat instead of the sesame oil for the frying. Return the pork with the seasonings, and add the reduced amount of sesame oil in the last thirty seconds off the heat, purely for the smell. There is more soy sauce here because there is more to season and because pork fat blunts salt."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Goma-ae","target":"japan-goma-ae","emoji":"🥬"},{"name":"Onigiri","target":"japan-onigiri","emoji":"🍙"},{"name":"Nikujaga","target":"japan-nikujaga","emoji":"🥔"}]},
{"id":"japan-buta-no-shogayaki","name":"Buta no Shogayaki","nameAlt":"Thin pork, ginger, and a sauce made in the pan","aliases":["Buta no Shogayaki","Shogayaki","Shougayaki","Ginger Pork","Japanese Ginger Pork"],"course":"main","type":["main","pork","quick","weeknight","pan-fried"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","lunchbox","quick"],"ingredients":"150g pork loin, sliced very thin · 8g fresh ginger, finely grated · 20ml Japanese soy sauce · 20ml mirin · 10ml sake · 5g white sugar · 60g onion, sliced thinly with the grain · 10ml neutral oil · 60g cabbage, shredded as fine as hair · 10g Japanese-style mayonnaise","method":"This is a ten-minute dish and every one of the ways it goes wrong happens in the first two minutes, so read the whole thing before the pan goes on. GET THE PORK PROPERLY THIN. In Japan you buy it pre-sliced at around 3mm, and the whole character of the dish depends on that: thin pork cooks through in under a minute, which means it never has time to dry out, and it takes on the glaze across a huge surface area relative to its weight. Here you will usually be slicing it yourself. Put the loin in the freezer for twenty to thirty minutes until it is firm at the edges but not frozen solid, then slice across the grain with your longest sharp knife — semi-frozen meat holds still and lets you go thinner than you ever could with soft pork. If a piece comes out thicker than you wanted, lay it between two sheets of plastic and give it a few taps with a rolling pin. Now the ginger, and the reason it is here at all. Grate it finely on a microplane rather than chopping it, so you get pulp and juice rather than pieces; you want it dispersed through the sauce, not lurking in fibrous knots. Ginger contains an enzyme called zingibain which breaks down protein — the same enzyme that will stop a jelly setting if you put fresh ginger in it, and the reason ginger genuinely tenderises meat rather than just flavouring it. That is useful and it is also a warning: SHORT MARINADE OR NONE AT ALL. Ten minutes is plenty. Leave thin pork sitting in grated ginger for an hour and the enzyme keeps working, the surface goes mealy and slightly mushy, and no amount of good frying will bring the texture back. Mix the soy sauce, mirin, sake, sugar and about two-thirds of the grated ginger in a small bowl and set it beside the stove, because from here on there is no time to be measuring anything. Toss the pork with the remaining ginger and leave it while you cut the onion and shred the cabbage. Cut the onion with the grain rather than across it, in slices about 5mm thick, so it holds together in the pan instead of collapsing into strings. Shred the cabbage as finely as you possibly can and drop it into ice water for five minutes, then spin or shake it dry — cold water makes shredded cabbage crisp up and curl, and this is the difference between a garnish and the thing that makes the plate work. Pile it on one side of the serving plate now, with the mayonnaise beside it, so the plate is ready before the meat is. Heat a wide frying pan over a high heat, add the oil, and when it shimmers add the onion. Fry for two minutes, moving it, until the edges are translucent and just starting to catch gold. Push it to one side of the pan or lift it out. Now the pork, laid out in a single layer with space between the pieces — CROWD THE PAN AND THE MEAT STEAMS, and steamed pork will never brown or glaze. Work in two batches if you need to. Give each side barely forty to sixty seconds; you are looking for the edges to go opaque and a little colour to appear underneath. Here is the timing that decides the dish, and it is the one thing the recipe cards usually get wrong: ADD THE SAUCE WHILE THE PORK IS STILL FAINTLY PINK IN THE MIDDLE. Not after it is cooked. Pour the sauce mixture in around the edges of the pan, return the onion, and let everything bubble hard together for thirty to forty-five seconds, turning the pork through it. The residual heat and the boiling sauce finish the cooking, and the pork finishes in a liquid rather than in dry heat, which is exactly why it stays tender. Wait until the meat is cooked and then add the sauce and you have overcooked pork wearing a sauce; do it this way and the two things happen at once. Watch the sauce rather than the meat now. The mirin and sugar mean it will go from thin to syrupy quickly, and you want to stop while it still coats the back of a spoon and slides — another twenty seconds and it goes tacky and then bitter. Lift the pork and onion onto the plate beside the cold cabbage and pour the remaining sauce from the pan over the top. Eat it immediately, with rice, using the cabbage between mouthfuls the way you would use a palate cleanser, because that is precisely what it is.","cookTime":"Prep 15 min · Cook 6 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 34g, Carbs 18g, Fat 32g, Sodium ~1180mg","storage":"Keeps two days covered in the fridge and is genuinely good cold, which is why it is one of the most common things in a Japanese lunchbox — the sauce sets slightly and clings. If you are reheating it, use a pan over a low heat with a splash of water for under a minute, or the thin pork toughens. It freezes for a month in its sauce; defrost overnight in the fridge rather than in a microwave, since thin slices go stringy when thawed fast. The cabbage does not keep — shred that fresh.","chefNotes":"Add the sauce while the pork is still slightly pink and let it finish cooking in the bubbling glaze. That single piece of timing is the whole difference between tender ginger pork and dry pork with sauce on it.","pairsWith":"Hot rice and miso soup, and the raw cabbage on the plate rather than in a separate bowl — that trio is the standard lunch set it was invented to be. A sharp pickle or a few slices of tomato work well against the sweetness. Cold beer, barley tea, or a dry white with enough acid to stand up to the soy.","trivia":"It tastes like something a Japanese grandmother has always made, and it is younger than the ballpoint pen. Shogayaki is generally traced to a Ginza restaurant in the 1940s, where it was developed as a way of turning out a hot meat dish very fast and in volume for delivery orders — an efficiency solution that happened to be delicious, went home with the customers, and became one of the two or three most-cooked pork dishes in the country within a generation. It sits alongside curry rice, hambagu and chicken teriyaki as a piece of twentieth-century restaurant cooking that the whole nation adopted as home cooking. There is a longer reason it needed inventing. Japan barely ate four-legged animals for around twelve hundred years: a Buddhist-influenced imperial edict in the seventh century restricted the eating of meat, and the prohibition was renewed, ignored, and renewed again for over a millennium until the Meiji government reversed it in the eighteen-seventies as a deliberate act of modernisation, with the Emperor himself publicly eating beef to make the point. So when pork became ordinary, Japanese cooks were working with an unfamiliar ingredient and were sharply aware of one thing about it: it smells. Ginger was the answer, and it is the answer in Chinese and Korean pork cookery too — a strongly aromatic rhizome that masks the particular funk of pork fat while cutting its richness. The name says so plainly. Shoga is ginger, yaki is grilled. The meat is not even mentioned, because the ginger was the point.","howThisFeels":"The hiss when the sauce hits a hot pan of pork, and the whole kitchen smelling of ginger and soy inside three seconds.","leftovers":["(easy) Cold from the fridge in a lunchbox with rice, exactly as intended — the sauce firms up overnight and clings to the meat rather than running.","(easy) Chop it roughly and stir it through hot rice with a spoonful of the sauce and a raw egg yolk, which turns it into a very good five-minute rice bowl.","(creative) Pile it into a soft roll with the shredded cabbage and a stripe of mayonnaise. The cold sauce soaks into the bread and it makes a better sandwich than most things designed to be sandwiches."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Nasu no Shogayaki — Aubergine (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":26,"howThisFeels":"Aubergine going glossy and almost meaty in the glaze, collapsing the moment your teeth reach it.","trivia":"Aubergine is the standard vegetable substitute for pork in Japanese home cooking and the reason is structural rather than sentimental: an aubergine is mostly air. Its flesh is a sponge of cells with large gaps between them, so it absorbs oil and sauce to a degree almost no other vegetable manages, and a slice that has drunk a ginger-soy glaze delivers flavour in a way that a courgette or a mushroom simply cannot. The trade is that it will drink as much oil as you give it, so the technique below salts it first — drawing out water collapses some of that sponge structure in advance, which means it absorbs the sauce rather than the fat.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"150g pork loin, sliced very thin"},{"item":"10g Japanese-style mayonnaise"}],"addIng":[{"item":"200g aubergine, in 1cm slices"},{"item":"2g fine salt"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"10ml neutral oil","to":"20ml neutral oil"},{"from":"10ml sake","to":"15ml sake"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Slice the aubergine into 1cm rounds or half-moons, salt them lightly on both sides and lay them in a colander for fifteen minutes. Beads of water will appear on the surface — blot them off firmly with a cloth, taking the salt with them. This is not about bitterness, which modern aubergines barely have; it is about collapsing some of the sponge so it stops drinking oil. Fry the slices in the doubled oil over a medium-high heat for three minutes a side, until they are deep gold and have gone from squeaky to yielding. Do not move them about in the first ninety seconds or they will absorb rather than sear. Add the onion for the last two minutes. Then add the sauce with the extra sake exactly as in the main method and let it bubble down for a minute, turning the slices through it — the aubergine will drink most of it and go dark and shining. There is no mayonnaise here, so squeeze a little lemon over the cabbage instead, or leave it plain."}]}},{"name":"Classic Buta no Shogayaki","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":36},{"name":"Miso Shogayaki","icon":"🍶","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":39,"howThisFeels":"Darker and nuttier, the sauce clinging thicker, more like a winter version of the same dish.","trivia":"Miso in the sauce is a recognised variation rather than a liberty, and it does something specific: miso is a fermented paste already loaded with free glutamates and with proteins broken down by koji mould over months, so it brings savoury depth that soy sauce alone cannot, plus body — it thickens the glaze without any starch. It also brings a hazard. Miso contains a lot of sugar and protein and it catches and burns far faster than soy sauce does, which is why it goes in at the very end and off a fierce heat. Every miso-glazed dish in Japanese cookery follows the same rule for the same reason.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"20g miso paste"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"20ml Japanese soy sauce","to":"10ml Japanese soy sauce"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Whisk the miso into the sauce mixture in the bowl before it goes anywhere near the pan, along with the reduced soy sauce — miso dropped into a hot pan seizes into lumps that never disperse. Loosen it with the mirin first if it is a stiff paste. Cook the pork exactly as in the main method, but turn the heat down to medium before the sauce goes in, and give it forty seconds rather than a minute. Miso scorches quickly and burnt miso is acrid rather than bitter, unmistakably a mistake. Take the pan off the heat while the sauce still looks slightly too loose; it thickens noticeably as it cools on the plate."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Tonkatsu","target":"japan-tonkatsu","emoji":"🐖"},{"name":"Goma-ae","target":"japan-goma-ae","emoji":"🥬"},{"name":"Onigiri","target":"japan-onigiri","emoji":"🍙"}]},
{"id":"japan-chirashizushi","name":"Chirashizushi","nameAlt":"Scattered sushi, made in a bowl instead of by hand","aliases":["Chirashizushi","Chirashi Zushi","Chirashi","Scattered Sushi","Barazushi"],"course":"main","type":["main","rice","celebration","seafood","no-cook"],"diet":["omnivore","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["celebration","family","spring","guests"],"ingredients":"90g Japanese short-grain rice (sushi rice), raw · 20ml rice vinegar · 10g white sugar · 2g fine salt · 8g dried shiitake mushrooms · 30g carrot, cut into fine matchsticks · 30g green beans, sliced on the diagonal · 100ml dashi · 10ml Japanese soy sauce · 10ml mirin · 1 egg · 3ml sunflower oil · 4 prawns, peeled and deveined · 10g pickled red ginger (beni shoga) · 1/4 sheet nori, cut into fine strips","method":"Chirashizushi is the easiest sushi to make and the hardest to make well, because there is no rolling and no knife skill to hide behind — it is the rice, and then a series of small separate jobs done properly. Do them in the order below and nothing overlaps badly. START THE SHIITAKE FIRST, because everything else waits on them. Cover the dried mushrooms with 150ml of cold water and leave them for at least four hours, or overnight in the fridge. Cold and slow, never hot and fast: hot water pulls the flavour out into the liquid in a rush and leaves the mushroom itself flabby, while a long cold soak rehydrates the flesh and builds a soaking liquid that is one of the most savoury things you can make from a packet. Keep that liquid. Squeeze the mushrooms out, cut off the tough stems, and slice the caps thin. Now the rice, which is the actual dish and deserves the attention. Rinse it in a bowl of cold water, swirling with your hand — the water will go milky immediately. Drain and repeat three or four times until the water runs almost clear, then let the drained rice sit for thirty minutes before cooking. That rest lets the grains absorb the surface water evenly so they cook through at the same rate. Cook it with 110ml of water, covered, brought to a boil then dropped to the lowest heat for twelve minutes, then off the heat and left covered and untouched for another ten. NO LIFTING THE LID AT ANY POINT. While it cooks, warm the vinegar, sugar and salt together just enough to dissolve — do not boil it, or you drive off the vinegar's aroma and are left with sour. This is sushi-zu, and it is the reason the dish is called sushi at all. Simmer the vegetables next: put the mushroom slices, the carrot and the shiitake soaking liquid into a small pan with the dashi, soy sauce and mirin, and cook at a bare simmer for eight minutes, adding the green beans for the last two so they keep their colour and squeak. Then lift everything out with a slotted spoon and let it drain and cool on a plate. DRAIN THEM PROPERLY AND DO NOT SKIP THIS — wet toppings turn seasoned rice to porridge, and the whole texture of the dish depends on rice that is glossy but distinct. Now the egg, which is where the dish gets its colour. Beat it with a pinch of salt and push it through a sieve, which sounds fussy and takes fifteen seconds and removes the ropy chalazae that would otherwise fry into pale threads. Heat a wide pan over a low heat with the barest smear of oil, pour in a thin layer of egg and tilt the pan to spread it out. Cook it until it is just set and still matte on top — under a minute — and do not let it colour at all. Slide it out flat, let it cool, then roll it up like a carpet and slice it into the finest ribbons you can. This is kinshi tamago, golden threads, and thin is the entire point. Poach the prawns for ninety seconds in the vegetable simmering liquid, or in salted water, until they curl and go opaque. Now dress the rice, and here is where most home cooks lose it: TIP THE HOT RICE INTO A WIDE, FLAT DISH, NOT A DEEP BOWL. Rice in a deep bowl steams itself, goes sticky and starchy, and cannot be dressed evenly. Wide and shallow — a large mixing bowl on its side, a roasting dish, anything. Pour the vinegar mixture over the back of a spatula so it disperses rather than landing in one spot, then fold it in with a CUTTING, SLICING motion, dragging the spatula edge through the rice and turning it over. Never stir and never press. Every stirring motion crushes grains and releases starch, and crushed rice is gluey rice. Traditionally you fan the rice while you cut it in, and there is a real reason behind the theatre: cooling it fast as the vinegar goes in stops the grains absorbing steam, and gives sushi rice its particular sheen. Keep cutting and cooling for about three minutes, until the rice is at body temperature and every grain is shining and separate. Fold the drained mushrooms, carrot and beans through the rice at this point — this is what makes it chirashi rather than rice with things on top. Spread it out in a shallow serving bowl and scatter the egg ribbons over the whole surface, then the prawns, then the pickled ginger and finally the nori strips, which go on at the very last second because they soften on contact with anything damp. Serve at room temperature. Never chilled — cold ruins sushi rice, hardening the starch back into something dry and chalky, which is why a fridge is the wrong place for this bowl.","cookTime":"Soak 4 hr · Prep 30 min · Cook 25 min","kcal":"~520 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 24g, Carbs 82g, Fat 8g, Sodium ~1250mg","storage":"This is a make-and-eat-today dish and it is worth being blunt about why: refrigerating cooked rice makes its starch recrystallise, and sushi rice comes out of a fridge dry, hard and mealy no matter how good it was going in. Keep it covered with a damp cloth at cool room temperature and eat it within about four hours. If you must refrigerate it, do so for no more than a day, take it out a full hour before eating, and accept it will not be what it was. It does not freeze. The simmered vegetables, made separately, keep three days in the fridge and can be prepared entirely in advance — that is the part to make ahead, not the rice.","chefNotes":"Fold the vinegar into the rice with a cutting motion in a wide flat dish, never a stir in a deep bowl. Stirring crushes the grains and releases the starch that turns seasoned rice gluey, and no amount of good topping recovers from it.","pairsWith":"A clear soup rather than a heavy one — clear dashi with a little mushroom, or a light miso. Something pickled and sharp on the side. It is a celebration dish, so it is usually the centre of the table rather than a companion to anything. Green tea, cold barley tea, or a dry sparkling wine, which is unorthodox and works beautifully against the vinegar.","trivia":"This bowl exists because somebody found a loophole in a sumptuary law. In the early Edo period, Ikeda Mitsumasa, the first lord of the Bizen-Okayama domain, issued an austerity decree instructing commoners to limit themselves to ichiju-issai — one soup and one side dish — with rice. The people of Okayama read the wording carefully and concluded that if the fish and the vegetables were chopped up and mixed into the rice, they were part of the rice rather than a second dish. What came out of that reasoning was barazushi: a technically compliant single bowl containing an entire feast from the Seto Inland Sea. There is a companion story, told with obvious relish in Okayama, that inspectors would stop people in the street and look inside their lunch boxes, so cooks packed the good things at the bottom of the box, covered them with plain-looking vinegared rice, and simply turned the whole thing upside down onto a plate when they arrived. The dish is now the region's proudest local speciality, and in 2004 a Hiroshima sushi-ingredient company successfully petitioned to have 27 June — the anniversary of Ikeda Mitsumasa's death — registered as Chirashizushi Day, which is a very Japanese way of settling an argument with a feudal lord four hundred years later. There is a second point buried in all this that most people outside Japan have backwards. Sushi does not mean raw fish. It means the vinegared rice, and always has. The word comes from an old adjective meaning sour, and the earliest form, narezushi, was a preservation technique in which fish was packed in cooked rice and left to ferment for months; the rice was the pickling medium and was thrown away, and only the fish was eaten. Chirashizushi is the clearest proof of what the word actually points at. There need not be a single piece of raw fish in the bowl for it to be, unarguably, sushi.","howThisFeels":"Lifting the cloth off a wide bowl of pale pink rice scattered with gold and green, and nobody at the table quite wanting to be the one to disturb it.","leftovers":["(easy) Press leftovers firmly into a small bowl lined with cling film, turn it out and you have a neat domed portion for a lunchbox — but eat it the same day and keep it out of the fridge if you possibly can.","(easy) Wrap a handful in a half-sheet of nori as a hand roll, adding a strip of cucumber for crunch to make up for rice that has lost a little of its bounce.","(creative) Turn day-old chirashi into yaki-onigiri: shape it into firm patties, brush them with soy sauce and mirin, and grill or pan-fry them until the outside is crisp and lacquered. Rice that has gone slightly dry is actually better for this than fresh rice, so the fault becomes the feature."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Gomoku Chirashi — All Vegetable (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":19,"howThisFeels":"Quieter and browner than the festival bowl, and the shiitake taste of it fills the room while it simmers.","trivia":"Gomoku means five things, and this is the everyday form of the dish across most of Japan — the version an ordinary household actually eats, made from a cupboard rather than a fishmonger. It is worth knowing that it is not a lesser bowl but an older one: chirashi built on simmered dried mushroom, gourd, carrot and egg predates the Edo-style seafood version, and the ingredients were chosen because every one of them keeps without refrigeration. Note the dashi swap below, which is not optional if this needs to be vegetarian. Ordinary dashi and every instant granule are made with dried bonito, which is a fish; kombu-and-shiitake dashi is the traditional temple-kitchen answer and is if anything the better broth here, since the dish is already built on shiitake.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"4 prawns, peeled and deveined"}],"addIng":[{"item":"40g carrot, extra, cut into fine matchsticks"},{"item":"30g green beans, extra, sliced on the diagonal"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"100ml dashi","to":"100ml kombu and shiitake dashi"},{"from":"8g dried shiitake mushrooms","to":"15g dried shiitake mushrooms"}],"addStep":[{"text":"With no prawns carrying the savoury weight, the mushrooms have to do it, so use the larger quantity and give them the full overnight cold soak rather than four hours. Make the dashi from a 10cm piece of dried kombu steeped in cold water alongside them, warmed to just below a simmer and lifted out before it boils — boiled kelp turns slippery and faintly bitter. Simmer the extra carrot and beans with the mushrooms exactly as written, and reduce the simmering liquid a little harder at the end so what clings to the vegetables is more concentrated. Everything else, including the rice, the vinegar and the egg ribbons, is unchanged. Be generous with the pickled ginger at the end; without seafood the bowl wants something sharp on top."}]}},{"name":"Chirashizushi with Prawn and Egg","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":38},{"name":"Hinamatsuri Chirashi — the Doll's Festival Bowl","icon":"🎎","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":72,"howThisFeels":"Pink, gold, green and white all in one bowl, and the distinct sense that you are looking at spring rather than lunch.","trivia":"On 3 March, Hinamatsuri, Japanese households with daughters set out tiered displays of ornamental dolls and eat this bowl, and the reason is almost entirely visual. The festival is about the arrival of spring and the wish for a girl's health and future, and the food is chosen as a colour scheme: pink for peach blossom, green for new growth, white and gold for the light coming back. Individual ingredients then carry their own meanings, which are mostly puns and observations of the sort that turn up in celebratory food everywhere. Prawns are curved like an old person's back, so they stand for long life. Lotus root is full of holes you can see through, so it stands for a clear view of what is coming. Beans are mame, which is also a word for diligence and good health. It is a bowl assembled as a sentence.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"60g salmon fillet, skin off"},{"item":"20g cucumber, in fine strips"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"4 prawns, peeled and deveined","to":"6 prawns, peeled with the tails left on"},{"from":"20ml rice vinegar","to":"25ml rice vinegar"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Two decisions before you start. First, if you are using the salmon raw, it must be fish sold as safe to eat raw and it must be very fresh — ask, do not assume, and if there is any doubt at all, poach it instead: slide the piece into barely trembling salted water for four or five minutes until it flakes, then cool it and break it into large rosy flakes, which looks just as good and worries nobody. Second, poach the prawns with their tails on and butterfly them by slicing almost through along the back and opening them out, so they sit flat and fan across the surface. Build the bowl in colour blocks rather than scattering evenly: the egg ribbons over the whole surface first as a golden base, then the salmon and prawns in loose clusters, then the cucumber and green beans threaded between, and the pickled ginger last as the pink note. The extra vinegar is deliberate — there is more richness in this bowl and the rice needs to cut through it. Assemble it within half an hour of serving and keep it covered with a damp cloth, out of the fridge."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Tamagoyaki","target":"japan-tamagoyaki","emoji":"🍳"},{"name":"Onigiri","target":"japan-onigiri","emoji":"🍙"},{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🍲"}]},
{"id":"japan-nasu-dengaku","name":"Nasu Dengaku","nameAlt":"Aubergine grilled under a lacquer of sweet miso","aliases":["Nasu Dengaku","Miso Glazed Aubergine","Miso Glazed Eggplant","Dengaku","Nasu no Dengaku"],"course":"side","type":["side","vegetables","grilled","vegan","quick"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","guests","summer"],"ingredients":"200g aubergine, halved lengthways · 30g miso paste · 15ml mirin · 10g white sugar · 5ml sake · 15ml neutral oil · 3g white sesame seeds, toasted","method":"This is a two-part dish and the parts barely interact until the last three minutes, which is why it is nearly impossible to get wrong once you understand what each half is for. Halve the aubergine lengthways, straight through the stem so each half keeps its handle, and then do the one piece of knife work that decides the whole thing: SCORE THE CUT FACE IN A DIAMOND LATTICE, about 1cm apart and roughly 1cm deep, without ever cutting through the skin. Take your time and keep the blade shallow. That lattice is not decoration. It multiplies the surface area enormously, it lets heat travel into the middle of a dense vegetable instead of crawling in from the edges, and — the real reason — it gives the glaze somewhere to sit. Score it properly and the miso settles into every groove and you get sweet-salty lacquer all the way through the flesh. Skip it and the glaze slides off a smooth face into the pan. Now salt is the question everyone asks and the answer here is no. Modern aubergines are not bitter and this method cooks them fast enough that the sponge problem is handled by technique instead. Brush the cut faces generously with the oil, and use more than feels comfortable — an aubergine is structurally a foam, mostly air trapped in cell walls, and it will drink whatever you give it. The oil is what conducts heat into that foam and turns it from squeaky and raw to silky. An underoiled aubergine goes leathery on the outside and stays cottony in the middle. Lay them cut side down in a heavy pan over a medium heat, or on a baking tray under a hot grill, and cook them for six to eight minutes until the cut face is properly browned — not tan, browned. Turn them over, add a splash of water to the pan, cover it, and let them steam for another eight to ten minutes until a skewer or a knife tip slides into the thickest part with absolutely no resistance and the flesh has gone from opaque white to translucent. THIS IS THE ONE THING TO GET RIGHT: AN UNDERCOOKED AUBERGINE IS UNPLEASANT IN A WAY THAT NO SAUCE CAN RESCUE. Squeaky, spongy, faintly bitter. Cook it further than you think. It should be collapsing. While that happens, make the dengaku miso, which takes four minutes and keeps for a month. Put the miso, mirin, sugar and sake in a small pan over a low heat and stir continuously with a spatula. It will loosen almost immediately as the alcohol warms, then slowly thicken again as that alcohol cooks off. You are looking for the point where it falls off the spatula in a thick ribbon that holds its shape for a second on the surface — roughly the consistency of thick honey. Three to four minutes on a low heat. LOW HEAT, AND DO NOT STOP STIRRING. Miso is loaded with sugars and amino acids, which is exactly why it browns and tastes so good, and exactly why it scorches faster than almost anything else in a Japanese kitchen. Burnt miso is not a deeper miso; it is acrid, and it will taint the whole batch. If it catches even slightly, throw it out and start again, because you cannot cover it. Now bring the two halves together. Turn the aubergines cut side up, spoon the miso glaze over the scored faces and spread it into the grooves, then put them under a hot grill for two to three minutes. Watch them the entire time and do not walk away — the glaze goes from glossy to blistered to black in the space of about forty seconds, and blistered is what you want: little dark bubbles rising across the surface with the paste still soft underneath. That short blast of fierce heat is doing Maillard chemistry on the miso's sugars and amino acids and it is where the whole flavour of the dish lives. Scatter the sesame seeds over while it is still hot so they stick. Serve immediately, one half per person, with a spoon rather than chopsticks if you like, because at this point the flesh is soft enough to eat like a baked potato.","cookTime":"Prep 10 min · Cook 20 min","kcal":"~240 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 5g, Carbs 22g, Fat 15g, Sodium ~1100mg","storage":"The glazed aubergine keeps two days in the fridge and is very good cold, in the way that a cold roast vegetable is good — the flesh firms and the glaze sets to something closer to a paste. Reheat under a grill rather than in a microwave if you want the blistered surface back. The dengaku miso itself is the thing worth making extra of: doubled or tripled, it keeps a month in a sealed jar in the fridge and turns any grilled vegetable, any piece of fish and any block of tofu into a finished dish in three minutes. Make a jar of it and you will use it weekly. It does not freeze usefully, and neither does the cooked aubergine, which goes watery and collapses.","chefNotes":"Cook the aubergine well past the point where you think it is done — squeaky aubergine is the one failure the glaze cannot hide. And keep the miso on a low heat, stirring, because burnt miso is acrid rather than deep and there is no recovering it.","pairsWith":"Rice and a bowl of soup make it a full plate, and it sits particularly well beside anything plain and grilled. Something crunchy and raw alongside stops the richness closing in — shredded cabbage, a cucumber pickle. It is also a very good thing to put in front of guests before a meal, since it can be built ahead and blistered at the last minute. Green tea, or a cold beer.","trivia":"The dish is named after a dance, and the resemblance is exact once you know what you are looking at. Dengaku began as a rustic performance tied to rice planting, and its most recognisable figure was a performer who balanced and hopped on a single tall stilt — a pole with a crosspiece — in a white robe. When Japanese cooks started skewering blocks of tofu on a stick, painting them with miso and grilling them, somebody noticed that a pale rectangle on a wooden pole looked precisely like a dengaku dancer, and the name transferred from the dance to the food. It stuck so completely that dengaku now simply means anything grilled with a sweet miso coating, and almost nobody making it thinks about dancers. There is a second thing here worth knowing, which is what miso actually is. It is a ferment — soybeans and usually rice or barley, inoculated with the mould Aspergillus oryzae, then left to age for anything from a few weeks to three years. The mould's enzymes spend that time taking the beans apart, breaking proteins down into free amino acids and starches into simple sugars, which is why miso tastes savoury in a way that plain soybeans do not, and why it browns so readily under a grill. It is also why the paste comes in colours: white miso is young, low in soy, high in rice, sweet and mild; red miso has aged far longer, and the darkness is the same browning reaction happening slowly at room temperature over years rather than in forty seconds under a flame. This dish traditionally uses the sweet white kind, and the grill is essentially running the ageing process again, at enormous speed, on the surface.","howThisFeels":"The miso blistering under the grill in the last thirty seconds, going from flat to bubbling all at once, and the smell that comes with it.","leftovers":["(easy) Eat it cold, straight from the fridge, cut into thick slices. The glaze sets firmer overnight and it is arguably better this way than warm.","(easy) Chop the leftover flesh roughly, glaze and all, and stir it through hot rice or noodles — it collapses into a sauce and seasons the whole bowl.","(creative) Scoop out the flesh, mash it with the glaze and a little extra miso, and use it as a dip or a spread on toasted bread. It is essentially a Japanese version of a smoky aubergine dip and it disappears alarmingly fast."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Simple Miso Dengaku (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":13,"howThisFeels":"Plainer and sweeter, the kind of thing you make on a Tuesday without thinking about it.","trivia":"Miso is one of the few genuinely expensive-tasting ingredients that is cheap by weight, and this version leans on that: less paste, more sugar, no sake, and it still tastes like the dish. That is not a compromise so much as how it was eaten for most of its history — dengaku was temple and street food long before it was restaurant food, made from the cheapest possible protein and the cheapest possible seasoning. The sake is doing a real job in the full version, cooking off and leaving behind aromatic compounds that round the paste out, but a splash of water and an extra thirty seconds of stirring gets you most of the way there.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"5ml sake"},{"item":"3g white sesame seeds, toasted"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"30g miso paste","to":"20g miso paste"},{"from":"10g white sugar","to":"14g white sugar"},{"from":"15ml mirin","to":"10ml mirin"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the glaze with a tablespoon of water in place of the sake, and expect to stir it for an extra thirty seconds or so — there is less liquid to loosen it and it will thicken faster, which means it will also catch faster. Keep the heat lower than you think you need. With less miso and more sugar the glaze is sweeter and browns more readily under the grill, so cut the blistering time to about ninety seconds and stand over it. Everything else, including the diamond lattice and cooking the aubergine well past done, is unchanged."}]}},{"name":"Nasu Dengaku","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":19},{"name":"Niku Miso Dengaku — with Pork","icon":"🍖","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":30,"howThisFeels":"The glaze turned into something closer to a ragu, sitting in the scored grooves in dark savoury clumps.","trivia":"Niku miso — meat miso — is a staple of its own in Japanese kitchens and it turns this from a side into a main. The technique is worth learning separately, because a jar of it makes about six meals: mince fried hard until dry, then bound with a sweet miso glaze until it is a thick, spoonable, intensely savoury paste that keeps for a week. It goes on rice, on noodles, into lettuce cups, on cucumber. Here it does a specific job, which is to replace the fat that the aubergine has drunk out of the pan and give the dish enough body to stand as dinner rather than an accompaniment.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"80g pork mince (20% fat)"},{"item":"4g fresh ginger, grated"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"15ml mirin","to":"20ml mirin"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Fry the mince in a dry pan over a high heat, breaking it up hard with a spatula, until the water it releases has completely boiled away and the meat is properly browned and separate rather than clumped and grey — this takes six or seven minutes and is longer than feels necessary. Add the grated ginger for the last thirty seconds. Then tip the miso, sugar, sake and the increased mirin straight into the pan with the meat and stir on a low heat for three or four minutes until the whole thing is thick and glossy and holds a shape on the spoon. It will catch even more readily than plain glaze because there is fat and browned protein in the pan, so lower heat, constant stirring. Spoon it over the cooked aubergine and grill for two minutes rather than three, since the meat is already browned and will dry out. Serve with rice."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Agedashi Tofu","target":"japan-agedashi-tofu","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Goma-ae","target":"japan-goma-ae","emoji":"🥬"},{"name":"Onigiri","target":"japan-onigiri","emoji":"🍙"}]},
{"id":"japan-miso-soup","name":"Miso Soup","nameAlt":"The bowl that finishes every Japanese meal","aliases":["Miso Soup","Misoshiru","Miso Shiru","Tofu Miso Soup","Japanese Miso Soup"],"course":"side","type":["side","soup","quick","everyday","budget"],"diet":["omnivore","vegan"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["everyday","family","breakfast","budget"],"ingredients":"300ml dashi · 20g miso paste · 40g firm tofu, in 1cm cubes · 2g dried wakame · 5g spring onion, finely sliced","method":"There are five ingredients and one rule, and the rule is the entire recipe: NEVER BOIL MISO. Everything else here is assembly. Start the wakame, because it needs a few minutes and nothing else does. Put the dried wakame in a small bowl of cold water and leave it. It will expand to something between five and ten times its dry size, which is genuinely startling the first time and is the reason the quantity above looks impossibly small — a heaped teaspoon of dry flakes becomes a generous handful of soft green ribbons. Over-measure it and you get a bowl of seaweed with some soup in it. Drain it after three or four minutes and, if the pieces are large, cut them into bite-sized lengths. Heat the dashi in a small pan until it is steaming and just trembling but not bubbling — around 80°C, well under a simmer. Slide in the tofu cubes and the drained wakame and let them warm through for two minutes. Handle the tofu gently and stir with the side of a spoon rather than a stirring motion; firm tofu is more fragile in liquid than it looks and a broken cube clouds the whole bowl. Now take the pan OFF the heat entirely before the miso goes anywhere near it. This is the moment the dish is made or ruined. Put the miso into a ladle or a small sieve, dip it into the hot dashi so that the liquid comes halfway up, and whisk it with chopsticks or a fork until the paste has completely dissolved into that small pocket of liquid before releasing it into the pot. Drop miso straight into a pan and you will spend a minute chasing lumps around, and you will still be finding them in the bowl. There are two reasons the pan must be off the heat, and both matter. The first is flavour: miso's aroma comes from volatile compounds produced over months of fermentation, and they boil off in seconds — a boiled miso soup smells of almost nothing and tastes flat and one-dimensionally salty. The second is texture: boiling causes the soy proteins to separate out and settle, so the soup goes grainy and the solids sink into a sludge at the bottom of the bowl instead of staying in that beautiful suspended cloud. Everything you like about miso soup is destroyed by ninety seconds at a rolling boil. Once the miso is in, do not put the pan back on the heat. If it has cooled too far, warm it very gently and take it off the moment steam rises. Taste it now, because miso brands vary enormously in salt — a white miso may need half again as much as a red one to reach the same strength. Adjust with more paste rather than salt. Pour into a bowl, scatter the spring onion over the top, and serve immediately. The soup should look faintly cloudy and slightly alive, with the miso hanging evenly through the liquid rather than settled. If you let a bowl stand for ten minutes the solids will settle out on their own, which is normal and is why in Japan the bowl is often given a gentle swirl before drinking. Drink it from the bowl and use chopsticks for the solid pieces — that is the actual etiquette, and there is no spoon.","cookTime":"Prep 5 min · Cook 8 min","kcal":"~90 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 7g, Carbs 5g, Fat 4g, Sodium ~980mg","storage":"Best made fresh and it takes eight minutes, so there is little reason not to. If you do keep it, it holds a day in the fridge, but reheat it very gently and never let it boil, or you will undo everything the method protects. It separates as it stands — that is the miso settling and it is not a fault; stir it back together. It does not freeze. The genuinely useful make-ahead is the dashi, which keeps three days in the fridge and turns this into a two-minute job. Wakame keeps indefinitely dry in the cupboard, which is the point of it.","chefNotes":"Take the pan off the heat before the miso goes in, and dissolve the paste in a ladleful of hot dashi first rather than dropping it into the pot. Boiled miso loses its aroma and goes grainy, and that single step is the difference between a good bowl and a salty one.","pairsWith":"It is not really a starter — in Japan it arrives with the meal and is drunk throughout, alongside rice, in the pairing that gives the whole cuisine its shape. It goes with everything, which is the point of it, but it is especially good against anything fried, and it is a standard part of a Japanese breakfast with rice, grilled fish and a pickle. Green tea, or nothing.","trivia":"This bowl is half of a phrase that describes the entire structure of a Japanese meal: ichiju-issai, one soup and one side. That phrase turns up as a Zen temple discipline, as a description of ordinary peasant eating, and — most memorably — as a sumptuary law, when a feudal lord in Okayama tried to legislate it and the population invented a whole style of sushi to get around it. The soup in that formula is almost always this one. What makes it interesting is how recently the components became affordable. Miso arrived from the Asian mainland well over a thousand years ago and for centuries was a luxury and a form of currency — samurai received it as part of their stipend, and it was valued because a barrel of fermented soybean paste is dense, salt-preserved protein that keeps for years without refrigeration, which made it a strategic military ration. Warlords maintained miso storehouses. Takeda Shingen is said to have encouraged its production in his territory specifically as a campaign food. It only became genuinely everyday in the Edo period, when commercial production reached ordinary townspeople. There is one modern footnote worth knowing, which is that miso is a live ferment full of Aspergillus oryzae and lactic acid bacteria — the same reason it should not be boiled is the reason unpasteurised miso is treated as a living food. Most supermarket miso is pasteurised, and the difference is real: the unpasteurised kind will slowly keep fermenting in your fridge and its flavour will deepen over months.","howThisFeels":"The moment the dissolved miso clouds through clear dashi and the whole bowl turns from water into food.","leftovers":["(easy) Pour it over a bowl of leftover rice for the simplest Japanese breakfast there is — the rice loosens into the soup and it becomes something closer to a porridge.","(easy) Use it as the broth for noodles: warm it gently, add cooked noodles and anything green, and it becomes a full bowl in three minutes.","(creative) Reduce leftover miso soup hard in a wide pan until it is thick and syrupy, then use it as a glaze for grilled vegetables or fish. It is the only situation where boiling it is allowed, because at that point you have stopped making soup and started making sauce."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Wakame and Spring Onion (Budget, Vegan)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":9,"howThisFeels":"Cleaner and greener, and you notice the dashi itself rather than what is floating in it.","trivia":"Strip the tofu out and this is the most common miso soup in Japan, not the least — wakame and spring onion is the everyday default and the tofu is the addition. The dashi swap is the load-bearing part. Ordinary dashi and every instant granule are built on katsuobushi, dried fermented bonito, so a bowl described as vegan cannot use it; kombu-and-shiitake dashi is the temple-kitchen answer, and it comes from shojin ryori, the Buddhist vegetarian cooking that developed exactly these techniques centuries before anybody used the word vegan. It is arguably the better broth here, since kombu's glutamate and shiitake's guanylate multiply rather than add.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"40g firm tofu, in 1cm cubes"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"300ml dashi","to":"300ml kombu and shiitake dashi"},{"from":"2g dried wakame","to":"3g dried wakame"},{"from":"5g spring onion, finely sliced","to":"10g spring onion, finely sliced"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Make the dashi by steeping a 10cm piece of dried kombu and a couple of dried shiitake in cold water for at least four hours, or overnight in the fridge, then warming it to just below a simmer and lifting the kombu out before it boils — boiled kelp turns slippery and faintly bitter. Use the mushroom soaking water as part of the broth; it is the most valuable thing in the pot. With no tofu there is nothing to warm through, so the soup goes together in about three minutes once the dashi is made: heat, off the heat, dissolve the miso in a ladle, add the soaked wakame and the extra spring onion. Be slightly more generous with the miso than you would be otherwise, since there is less in the bowl to carry it."}]}},{"name":"Tofu and Wakame Miso Soup","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":17},{"name":"Tonjiru — Pork and Root Vegetable","icon":"🐖","diet":["omnivore"],"costPP":32,"howThisFeels":"Thick enough to stand a spoon in, steaming, and much more a meal than a bowl of soup.","trivia":"Tonjiru is what happens when miso soup is asked to be dinner, and it is the standard winter answer across Japan — a pork and root vegetable soup that is properly filling and is often cooked in enormous quantities for school events, festivals and construction sites. The technical difference from ordinary miso soup is that it is genuinely cooked rather than assembled: the vegetables simmer in the dashi until soft, which takes twenty minutes, and only then does the same off-the-heat miso rule apply at the very end. A splash of sesame oil at the finish is standard and is worth not skipping.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"80g pork belly, sliced very thin"},{"item":"60g carrot, in thin half-moons"},{"item":"80g potato, in 2cm chunks"},{"item":"40g onion, sliced thinly"},{"item":"5ml sesame oil"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"300ml dashi","to":"400ml dashi"},{"from":"20g miso paste","to":"35g miso paste"},{"from":"40g firm tofu, in 1cm cubes","to":"60g firm tofu, in 1cm cubes"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Cut the pork into short strips and fry it in a dry pan over a medium-high heat until the fat renders and the edges brown, four or five minutes — do not skip this, because the browned fat is most of what separates tonjiru from vegetable soup with pork in it. Add the onion, carrot and potato and turn them through the rendered fat for two minutes, then pour in the increased dashi. Simmer gently for eighteen to twenty minutes, skimming off any foam that rises in the first few minutes, until the potato is completely soft. Add the tofu and the soaked wakame for the last two minutes. Then take the pan OFF THE HEAT and dissolve the larger quantity of miso in a ladle exactly as in the main method — the rule does not relax just because this is a heartier soup. Finish with the sesame oil off the heat and the spring onion in the bowl. Better the next day, reheated gently and never boiled."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Dashi","target":"japan-staple-dashi","emoji":"🍲"},{"name":"Onigiri","target":"japan-onigiri","emoji":"🍙"},{"name":"Agedashi Tofu","target":"japan-agedashi-tofu","emoji":"🍢"}]},
{"id":"japan-anmitsu","name":"Anmitsu","nameAlt":"Seaweed jelly, sweet beans and black sugar syrup","aliases":["Anmitsu","An Mitsu","Mitsumame","Japanese Agar Jelly Dessert","Kanten Dessert"],"course":"dessert","type":["dessert","cold","summer","no-bake","vegan"],"diet":["vegan","vegetarian"],"cuisine":"east-asia","country":"Japan","occasion":["summer","family","guests","everyday"],"ingredients":"4g agar agar powder · 350ml water · 30g dried azuki beans · 45g brown sugar · 40g glutinous rice flour · 60g tinned peaches, in pieces · 40g pineapple, in pieces","method":"Anmitsu is assembly rather than cooking, and every component can be made days apart, which is why it belongs in a hot kitchen in February. Work backwards from the jelly, since it needs to set. START THE BEANS THE NIGHT BEFORE. Soak the azuki in plenty of cold water overnight, then drain, cover with fresh water and bring to the boil. Drain that first water away entirely and start again with fresh — this first-boil discard is standard in Japanese kitchens and it removes the harsh, faintly astringent compounds in the skins that would otherwise sit under everything. Now simmer them gently in fresh water for about an hour, topping up so they stay just covered, until a bean crushes between your fingers with no hard core at all. And here is the law that governs every sweet bean dish in Japan: THE SUGAR GOES IN ONLY AFTER THE BEANS ARE COMPLETELY SOFT. Sugar draws water out of the beans and firms their skins, and a bean that meets sugar while it is still hard will stay hard no matter how long you cook it afterwards. Once they are properly tender, add about 25g of the brown sugar and a pinch of salt, and simmer for another ten minutes until the liquid is syrupy and the beans are glossy. The salt is not optional and it is not there to taste salty; it sharpens the sweetness and stops the whole thing tasting flat. Now the jelly, which is the reason the dish exists and is completely unlike gelatin. Whisk the agar powder into the cold water in a saucepan — cold, not hot, or it clumps instantly and you will never disperse it. Bring it to a full rolling boil, whisking, and then KEEP IT BOILING FOR A FULL TWO MINUTES. This is the single most common failure with agar and it is invisible until it is too late: agar does not dissolve at a simmer, and undissolved agar sets into a weak, weeping, grainy jelly with unmelted specks in it. It must actually boil. Pour it into a shallow container and leave it. Now the properties that make it Japanese rather than European: agar sets at room temperature, around 40°C, so no refrigeration is needed — which is exactly why it became the summer dessert of a country that had no fridges. It is firm rather than wobbly, it cuts cleanly into cubes that hold their edges, and it melts in the mouth at body temperature rather than dissolving slowly like gelatin. It is also completely plant-based, which is incidental here and was essential in temple kitchens. It will be set in about forty-five minutes at room temperature, faster in the fridge. Cut it into 1.5cm cubes with a sharp knife and a steady hand. While it sets, make the shiratama dango. Put the glutinous rice flour in a bowl and add cold water a teaspoon at a time, working it with your fingers, until the dough comes together with the texture of a soft earlobe — that is the traditional description and it is a genuinely useful one, softer than pastry and firmer than batter. Roll it into small balls about 2cm across and press a dimple into the centre of each with your thumb, which helps the middle cook through. Drop them into boiling water. They sink. Wait, and when they float to the surface give them another two minutes, then lift them straight into a bowl of iced water. THE ICE WATER IS NOT OPTIONAL — it stops the cooking dead and firms the outside, and dango left to cool in air go sticky, slump and fuse to each other. Make the syrup by dissolving the remaining 20g of brown sugar in a tablespoon of hot water and letting it cool; a proper kuromitsu uses Japanese black sugar and is darker and more molasses-like, and dark brown sugar is an honest stand-in. Now build it, and build it in a wide shallow bowl rather than a deep one so everything is visible: the agar cubes first as the base, then the fruit and the dango arranged around, then a generous spoonful of the sweet beans in the centre — that heap of beans is the an in anmitsu and it is what distinguishes this from its plainer ancestor. Pour the syrup over at the table, not in the kitchen, so it is still glossy when it arrives.","cookTime":"Soak overnight · Prep 30 min · Cook 70 min · Set 45 min","kcal":"~330 kcal","nutrition":"Protein 6g, Carbs 74g, Fat 1g, Sodium ~90mg","storage":"Every part keeps separately and almost nothing keeps assembled, so store the components apart and build at the last minute. The sweet beans keep five days in the fridge and freeze for three months, and are worth making in a large batch since they are the slow part. The agar jelly keeps three days in the fridge in its container, cut or uncut, though it slowly weeps a little water — pour that off. The dango are the exception and they are strict about it: they go hard and chalky within a few hours and are genuinely not the same thing the next day, so make them on the day, or leave them out. The syrup keeps for weeks. Assembled anmitsu should be eaten within about half an hour.","chefNotes":"Boil the agar for a full two minutes after it reaches a rolling boil — a simmer will not dissolve it, and the jelly will set weak and grainy with no way to tell until you cut it. And never sweeten the beans until they are completely soft, or they stay hard forever.","pairsWith":"Green tea, and specifically a bitter one — sencha or matcha against this much sugar is the entire point of the pairing. A scoop of ice cream on top makes it cream anmitsu, which is a standard menu item rather than a liberty. It follows a rich meal well because it is fat-free and cold, and it is one of very few desserts that genuinely refreshes rather than finishing you off.","trivia":"The jelly in this bowl is the reason modern medicine has petri dishes. Agar was discovered by accident in 1658 at an inn in Fushimi, near Kyoto, where an innkeeper named Minoya Tarozaemon served a seaweed jelly called tokoroten to a travelling lord and threw the leftovers outside. It froze overnight, thawed in the sun, and over several days dried into a white, porous, weightless substance. He boiled it out of curiosity, found that it melted and set again into a jelly that was cleaner and clearer than the original with none of the seaweed smell, and effectively invented freeze-drying in the process. The result was named kanten — cold sky. Two centuries later it reached a laboratory in Berlin. Robert Koch's team were trying to grow bacteria on solid surfaces and gelatin kept defeating them: many bacteria digest it, and it melts at around body temperature, which is precisely the temperature you need for an incubator. A researcher named Walther Hesse solved it after his wife Fanny suggested the substance she used to set jams and jellies, which she had learned about from Dutch acquaintances with connections to the East Indies. Agar does not melt until near boiling, almost nothing digests it, and it is transparent. Koch published it in 1882 and agar became — and remains — the standard medium of microbiology. Effectively every bacterial culture, every antibiotic sensitivity test and a great deal of twentieth-century biology sits on a jelly invented by a Japanese innkeeper who did not clear up after dinner. The dessert itself has a shorter story. Mitsumame — jelly, fruit and syrup — was Edo-period street food; a Ginza shop is credited with adding the heap of sweet bean paste in the 1930s and calling the result anmitsu, and the addition was popular enough that the older version now reads as the incomplete one.","howThisFeels":"Cubes of clear jelly knocking against the side of the bowl, and the black syrup running down through them at the table.","leftovers":["(easy) The sweet beans are the real leftover here and they are worth cooking extra of — spread them on toast with butter, which is a genuine and beloved Japanese breakfast, or stir them into yoghurt.","(easy) Keep leftover agar cubes in their syrup in the fridge and eat them plain and cold on a hot afternoon. They are almost calorie-free and extremely refreshing.","(creative) Blend leftover sweet beans with cold milk or oat milk and a little ice into a bean shake — it sounds unlikely and it is a standard drink in Japanese cafés, tasting somewhere between chocolate and toasted grain."],"servings":1,"sharedWith":"","versions":[{"name":"Mitsumame — No Beans (Budget)","icon":"💰","diet":["vegan"],"costPP":14,"howThisFeels":"Lighter, colder, more like eating fruit than eating pudding, and gone in about a minute.","trivia":"This is the original and anmitsu is the sequel. Mitsumame — mitsu for syrup, mame for beans, though the beans in the old version were a scattering of boiled peas rather than a sweet paste — was sold from Edo street stalls and later from the shops that grew into Japan's café culture. Dropping the sweet bean heap takes out the slowest, dearest and most filling component, which is why this is both the cheap version and the one people actually reach for in high summer. It is also the fastest: with no beans to soak and simmer, the whole thing goes from cupboard to bowl in about an hour, most of which is the jelly setting by itself.","delta":{"removeIng":[{"item":"30g dried azuki beans"}],"swapIng":[{"from":"45g brown sugar","to":"25g brown sugar"},{"from":"60g tinned peaches, in pieces","to":"90g tinned peaches, in pieces"},{"from":"40g pineapple, in pieces","to":"60g pineapple, in pieces"}],"addStep":[{"text":"With no beans there is no overnight soak and no hour of simmering, so this is genuinely a same-afternoon dessert. Use all the sugar for the syrup and make it a little stronger than you otherwise would — dissolve it in the smallest amount of hot water it will take, so the syrup is thick rather than watery, because it is now the only sweetness in the bowl and everything else is either neutral or sharp. Increase the fruit to fill the space the beans left, and lean towards something acidic; tinned peaches in their own juice and fresh pineapple together give you sweetness and sharpness in the same spoonful. Chill everything properly before assembling. Serve it very cold."}]}},{"name":"Anmitsu","icon":"🏆","default":true,"diet":["vegan"],"costPP":24},{"name":"Cream Anmitsu","icon":"🍨","diet":["vegetarian"],"costPP":31,"howThisFeels":"The first spoonful where cold ice cream, warm syrup and cold jelly all arrive at once.","trivia":"Cream anmitsu is not a modern western intrusion but a piece of Japanese café history in its own right, dating from the post-war decades when ice cream became widely available and the traditional sweet shops added it to stay relevant. It works for a reason worth understanding: agar melts at body temperature and has no fat, so the bowl has been entirely lean and clean up to this point, and a scoop of something rich lands with far more impact than it would in a creamier dessert. It also changes the diet label — the rest of this record is vegan by construction, and dairy ice cream is the one thing that breaks it. A plant-based scoop keeps it vegan and works just as well.","delta":{"addIng":[{"item":"50g ice cream"}],"addStep":[{"text":"Build the bowl exactly as in the main method, but assemble it at the last possible moment and keep everything very cold beforehand — the agar cubes and the fruit should come straight from the fridge, because a scoop of ice cream on room-temperature jelly is a puddle within two minutes. Set the ice cream on top of the beans last, pour the syrup over at the table, and hand it over immediately. Vanilla is standard and matcha is better if you can get it. Note that dairy ice cream makes this the one version of the record that is not vegan; a plant-based scoop keeps it so and behaves identically in the bowl."}]}}],"crossLinks":[{"name":"Matcha Warabimochi","target":"japan-matcha-warabimochi","emoji":"🍡"},{"name":"Mitarashi Dango","target":"japan-mitarashi-dango","emoji":"🍢"},{"name":"Dorayaki","target":"japan-dorayaki","emoji":"🥞"}]}
];
